        <p>( OLD MINING AS A PROFITABLE INVESTMENT. Parties with a little spare ..ash at their command would do well to pondar over the following remarks. It is a well ka iwn fact that many worthy families in this and other counties laid the fowl-teflon of their fortunes by investing in legitimate ruining enterprises. What has been done uefore can be clone again, but investors should make strict and careful enquiries before embtrking in undertakings of this description. There can be rrt doubt that if a judicious selection be made of the various public companies now work- ing, the shares in wh,ch (owing to the great depression) are quoted at merely nominal figures, and an investment made say in half a dozen we dare safe y say that in the course of a few months, an investor would see his money doubled or even trebled. _</p>
        <p>We donot. dvocate gambling or take prices I,v the so-called " Cover System," but advise our clients to calculate the num- ber of shares they ean take up and pay for, to lock them up and watch the price list. When a profit is shewn, wire or send others to sell, unless trustworthy advice Is received to contrary.. N tto be alarmecl at a slight fall in orice—in many inetances prices are put down by market manipulation and not hecause of any falling off In the intrinsic value of the mine—but wire tor infJrnsation,aud if there is any legitimate reason tor the fall, sell out end cut the loss. By following the ab.-we faithfully, risks are reduced to a minimum and large profits can he made. We may mention another form of mining investment. which has proved wonde fully profitable in the emcee of the past few years, we iefer to exploration and development syndi- cates. These syndicates are formed to explore certain gold fields, discover and open out mines, With undertakings of thi I kind a small amount of capital only is required. Claims es.n sic purchased first hand from prospectors or dlscovered and acquired by agents of the syndicate-, and proved and developed to an extent suffi.ient to warrant the formation or separate companies t,) work them.</p>
        <p>'New a Syndicate of this description will frequently acquire sufficient claims or grant • for a dozen companies the sale of a single group alone paying back the entire capital two or , three times over, so that in the event of a suecessfril floata- tion of ball dozen c mpanies, the shareholders in the ori- ginal Syndicate would hive th2ir capital returned many times over in cash tesides retaining a large interest in the various companies form a A preliminary Syndicate of this description is no s in cout,e of negociation, not for the pur- pose of discovering gold claims, 'eta, to acquire a number of claims already found and proved. The country to wdli this Syndicate proposes to carry ant its fint operations has been one of th". richest gold producers in the world, but th re has ba..n no rush or exlitement, as in the days of Calhera,,,u, Austrarian. Sonth African and Westralian discoveries, for tl simple reason that it has not been advertised. Prospectors are quietly toiling along. alining and crushing the ore and extracting the geld by very crude and aneient methods and miking good profits not- withstanding thep irn tire processes employed. The Projector of t, is St-ta.licate having been engaged in the minhg industry in the country for several years, is well acquaintdd %dill tile fleid, and has every confidence that when these mines are taken up and wArket by modern methods urge and profitable returns must he made. Further particulars can be obtained</p>
        <p>ANDREWS &amp; COMPANY, miNING AGENTS AND SHAREHOLDERS, 105, LONDON WALL, LONDON, B.C. 20 Years' Experience in England,?Africa, and Sollth America.</p>
