        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THE man who "can take a drink or let it slogs" sometimes finds the former accomplishment the more lasting. AGGRAVATING.—" Don't talk to me of scientifc discoveries and cathode rays!" said the publisher, angrily; "no sooner do I get a manual of physics printed for school use than the thing is out of date." HIS CAPACITY WAS LIMITED.—" Don't you think, /Larry, you could induce one or two boys to come to Sunday-school ?" "I could bring one," he replied. "De udder fellers in our alley kin lick me."</p>
        <p>MRS. A.: "I'm surprised that your husband earns so little if he works as hard as you say. What does he do?" Mrs. B. : "The last thing he did was to calculate how many times a clock ticked in the course of a thousand years." MA KES A DIFF ERENCE. —Tommy (surprised): "Why, papa, I thought that one spoonful of sugar was always enough for my coffee?" Tommy's Papa: "This is a restaurant, my son. Take all the sugar you want." A FRIGHT. —Jimson : "Have you a dog !" Smithkins : " Well, I guess ! We've gat one my wife embroidered on a tidy that we hang out on the porch, and it frightens the tramps speech- less."</p>
        <p>CORONER: "You say you told the servant to get out of the house the minute y ni discovered the fire, and she refused to go?" Mrs. Burntout : " Yes, sir; she said she mu q' have a month's notice before she'd think of leaving."</p>
        <p>MASHED: "That was a rather sensible observa- tion of mine, don't you think?" Lady: "Why, certainly. Didn't you notice the look of surprise on my face?"</p>
        <p>"This is a remarkably high-flavoured roast," said the King of Mowpka. "It is from that late Chicago individual," said the purveyor-in-chief. "I am really surprised. That Boston missionary told me explicitly and distinctly that Chicago people were utterly devoid of taste." OUR. Yoßtax Asttomp.—" Jane. you've broken as much china this month as your wages amount to. Now, how can we prevent this occurring again ? " "I don't know, mum, unless ye raise me wages." ALL THE LATEST IMPROVEMENTS—Jim: "Got de tools all here, Bill?" But : " Ves." Jim: "An' de dynamite capsols, an' de smokeless bombs, an' de non-detonatin' powder ? " : "Yes." Jim: "Then git out de Roentgen camerer, an' we'll see wot's inside de vault afore we goes any furder." EGOTISM.—" Youmay talk about your superiority all you please," said the horse to the bicycle ; "but you can't go unless somebody rides you." "True !" retorted the machine; "but you never induced the girls to wear bloomers, nor did you originate the New Woman. I did all that." As GOOD AS GAS.—Victim: "You say you supply balloons to guests on the top floor in case of fire? Are they already filled ?" Chicago Hotel Clerk: "Oh, no. Just wait until the fire has made progress enough, and you can fill your balloon with hot air." A BURNED CHILD.—Mr. Homenights : " Whew : hire's an account in the paper of a terrible mur- der!" Mrs. Hotnenight s "Read it." Mr. Homenights : "Wait a minute till I see whether there's a patent medicine advertisement at the end of it."</p>
        <p>_ LOVING Mother: "Well, Tommy dear, what did you learn at school to-day?" Tommy (with an air of disgust): " Eltunp ! didn't learn nothin'." Loving Mother: "Didn't learn nothing? What did you do, then?" Tommy: "Didn't do nothin'. A woman wanted to know how to spell 'cat,' and I told her. That's all." '‘ DRATH TO OUR BEST FRIENDS.' "—A favourite toast at convivial entertainments in St. John's, Newfoundland, is "Death to out best friends." However frequently the sentiment may be repeated, it is invariably received with shouts of applause and laughter, and honoured by the tui est bumpers. A stranger hearing it for the first time might be shocked or bewildered by being called upon to drink such a heartless toast, but to the initiated it has a harmless significance. It is equivalent to "Prosperity to our fisheries "—the " friends" who are devoted to death being the seals and codfish. BOUGHT AND SOLD.—A good story is told of an old-fashioned miser. He was never known to have anything in the way of new apparel but once; then he was going on a journey, and had to pur- chase a new pair of boots. The stage left before day, and he got ready and went to the hotel to stop for the night. Among a whole row in the morning he could not find the old familiar pair. He had forgotten the new ones, and he hunted and hunted in vain. The stage was ready, and so he looked carefully round to see that he was not observed, put on a nice new pair that fitted him, then called the waiter, and told him the circum- stances, giving him ten shilling for the owner. The owner never called. The miser had bought his own boots ! EASY TO IDENTIFY.—An amorous young man sent a letter to a German lady, and this postscript was added :—" That my darling niay make no mistake, remember that I will wear a light pair of trousers and a dark cut-away coat. In my right hand I will carry a small cane, and in my left a cigar.—Yours ever, Adolphe." The father replied courteously, stating that his (laughter had given him authority to represent her at the appointed place, at the time agreed on. His postscript was as follows :—" Dot mine son may make no mistakes, (viii be dreshed in mine shirt sleeves. I vill year in mine right hand a_glub ; in my left hand I viii sear a six-shooter. You vill recognise me by de vay I bats you on de head a goopl, times twice mid de glub. Vait for me at de corner, as I have some- dings important to inform you init....Your frent, Heinrich Muller." "I ALWAYS thought you were a man of courage," she said, bitterly. "I am," he protested. "You ! " she exclaimed, sar- castically. " Yes," he replied, haughtily; "I have proved my courage dozens of times." " Where ?" she asked, "On the football field, in the hunting field—everywhere that courage is needed." "Then," she said, in a tone of convic- tion, "you do not love me." "Ellen," he cried, "retract those words! You know not what you say! I love you madly—devotocily.•' "So you have said b&amp; ore," she returned, sorrowfully, "and yet"— " ipeak ! " he implore l, as she hesi- tated. "What makes you doubt e.. iier my love or my courr.;e ? " "You have ast•:od me to be yours," she said, in a low, passioN ate voice. " You have said that you wished to marry ine and for two weeks WQ have been engaged. ' " Glorious two weeks !" he interrupted. " Pm, (luring that time," she continued, " you ; v steadfastly refused to speak to my father." ; ; ',row clouded instantly. "I have," he admittc ; but that is purely a matter of discretion. courageous, Ellen, but I never claimed to 1(.. irdy.</p>
