      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>DRAB Nrioraz, Hats are much smaller; but, as the height had increased rather than diminished, the effect of huge bows and plumes was exaggerated in the extreme. Bird of paradise feathers were numerous, but so were huge, stiff birds and wings. Blue and green were enormously popular; nearly every woman seemed to be wearing a blue or green straw, felt, or chenille hat, trimmed with shot blue and green bows, and a vivid green parroquet perched as stiffly as possible in the most conspicuous place.</p>
        <p>I have seen very few red hats—but perhaps it is rather early for them as yet,—but one is deserving of description. It was something of a torreador shape, in dull red felt, flecked with a brighter tin. and with white and black. A deep band of red velvet was round the crown, and at the side numerous quills, apparently of turkey's feathers, dyed red, with their characteristic markings still showing. A huge bow at the back of black and white plaid ribbon completed the chapeau.</p>
        <p>A lovely toque of sapphire mirror velvet is trimmed on the left side with small black ostrich tips and black paradise osprey caught with a paste and steel ornament. Black chenille and jet trim- ming appear on the crown.</p>
        <p>The new coats have small gigot sleeves, and no revers, or very small ones. Stitching was very prevalent, this being done in elaborate designs, so that a thick cording seemed to form the pattern. Basques were rather longer, with very little ful- ness, some of them cut in "battlements," and finished with three rows of stitching.</p>
        <p>A very stylish little coat is made of fine biscuit. coloured box cloth handsomely braided with black. and trimmed with Persian lamb fur on the revere. collar, and cuffs. The basque at the back sets it flat pleats, for fluted basques to coats are no longei fashionable, and the high roll collar an be worn either up or down, to suit all weathers. In choosing a new dress for autumn wear, it is very important to fix on a thoroughly reliable material, which will stand a certain amount of in- different weather without losing its original beauty. The serges, in blue and black, and in permanent dye, are invaluable for winter wear, and these can be had in various weights suitable for women or children's wear.</p>
        <p>A trim tailor costume h.s the coat of entirely new shape, fitting well at the back, and with a straight, double-breasted front, strapped and stitched. Such coats can be bought ready-made from 21s. to 255., in black, tan, or dark green cloth, either of which are fashionable this season; but the girl who is clever enough to make one at home should select a thick beaver or box cloth that does not require lining. The latter is also unlined, merely hemmed up to a depth of three inches with leather or the morocco-finished American cloth, which excellent for the purpose and very inex- pensive. _</p>
        <p>- A handsome dress is made entirely of brochd satin, on a pale pink ground, with ribbon design in moss-green. The skirt fits the figure accurately round the hips, and spreads downwards in ample folds, forming a short train at the back. The bodice is quite plain at the back, where it laces, or hooks invisibly, and the slightly full front is lightly embroidered with minute steel sequins and pearl beads. The zotiave pieces of handsome pearl embroidery. The graceful coarselet is of moss- green satin, and comes from the side seams only, and the moderately full sleeves are set in rather large pleats on die shoulder. This dress would be strikingly eiiective in ivory brochd with black satin corselet and black gloves; or a fair girl should have it made in ciel-blue, pale green, or mauve, while her darker sister could wear pink, maize, or amber. Dark-haired girls with blue or grey eyes usually look charming in blue, which intensifies the tint of their eyes and adds delicacy to the corn- ,plexion, but blue must be avoided by brown-eyed brunettes.</p>
