      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LORD ROSEBERY ANDI HIS LADY</head>
        <p>LORD ROSEBERY ANDI HIS LADY</p>
        <p>CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>In the course of Lord Rosebery's speech at Edinburg the following passage occurred :—" Well, I had at !AP. beginning of this agitati n—in fact,when the massaerl, had taken place, but before the agitation had tsk...dr place, it must have been early in September—a let: from a very intelligent and a very right thinking la% who said, • Why, if all Europe fails, do we not go itie alone? We might be defeated.but in what nobler csilt could Great Britain be shattered ?' (Laughter.)—WATl I wrote back with much deliberation to say that not wish Great Britain to be shattered. She at no; rejoined, My lord, your answer saddens ter.)" With reference to this passage we (the " News") have received the following letter from Le brook, Bowdon, Cheshire :—Sir,—As the writer of to, letter to Lord Rosebery, whom he has, I feel sure 111°h tentionally, misrepresented in his speech at Edinbulgtl I think it due to myself to offer an explanation. Willi to said was that I would rather that England shou!d of shattered than that she should be deaf to the alio anguish which appealed, in this case most justly, t° for help—and I said nothing of defeat. In the letter I said that of course I did not believe Eng"',l3r would be shattered, but that until she came to the be termination to do right and fear no consequence 50 would accomplish nothing. As to the sadness,it I explained, because of the lack in Lord RoseberY,wm he only shared with other public men (Mr. Glacist°r bad then not spoken),—l meant lack of faith in a]' God, before whom the strength of Russia or France, any Power is but weakness7—l am, yours faithfUl EDITH TATTERSALL."</p>
