        <p>COLEMAN'S WINCARNIS. COLEMAN'S WINCARNIS. 4'°oo MEDICAL MEN have sent Testimonials in RR favour. Sold in Bottles 2s. 9(1. and 3s. 6ci. everywhere.</p>
        <p>The Independent Labour Party ar,t in OPirits about the Bradford contest. Tney have Plenty of money, lum told, and mean to make a tremendous fight,not with any hope of winning the kat,but of keeping the Liberal out of it, in accord- Ince with their general policy. So confident are the I.L.P,'s about what they can do at East Brad- fOrd that I hear this evening they feel sure of put ting their man, whoever he may be, in the second Noce on the poll.</p>
        <p>Lord Salisbury will have some difficulty in filling the vacancy in the Primacy of All England caused O the pathetic death of Dr. Benson at Hawarden. Ibis tragic event was the general topic of conver- sation at the beginning of the week. It is not ?any weeks since I saw him on horseback in the rark,looking bale and hearty. Only a few names tire being mentioned at present in the way of Ruesses,and not much weight attaches to any of the ,4tiggestious made. The Bishop of Winchester is, however, being named as likely to be chosen, on count of his intimate relations with the Royal ramily.</p>
        <p>, On Saturday morning the perusual of his Edin- otirgh deliverance was followed by a rally in Lord tosebery's favour among the middle class and taoderate Liberals, hut upon the strong Radica thalanx ppposed to him it made no impression. :the Moderates following the lead of some writers Pfl. the press, were strongly disposed to reinstate min at the head of the party by a vote of confidence Ind to invite him to withdraw his resignation. Later in the day this feeling cooled considerably down, and since then it is manifestly cooler still Ittid Moderates who on Saturday would have voted etithuqasticdly for him now talk of the wisdom of ~..delay in considering the question of the leadership. With them Lord Rosebery represented moderate !..nd safe progress upon the old and well-recognised ones of advance. Ho represented also the Imperial spirit and the preservation ot the honour of the untry abroad, without the infusion of any air of diligoism. Be bore allo as head of the Liberal party the mark and seal of Mr. Gla&amp;tone's choice, and Was the political Elisha who had been selected by the greater prophet at the moment when he retired from public life. All these considerations help at trat to blind the temperate friends of Liberal pro- kress who sit on the back benches above the Opposi- tion gangway. until a more dispassionate view of the facts of their position began to open their eyes.</p>
        <p>Another consequence flows from the present iltuation, and it is that Lord Rosebery is the last Peer-Premier that will ever lead Liberalism. The tace expires with him. It is not conceivable that 1,1113' Peer, even a great genius and strong personal- ity, would accept a function which is practically *!Workable Lor Rosebery's account of his own difficulties in the position he held is quite enough to frighten any other Liberal peer from accepting the responsibilities, anxieties, worries, and miseries of an office in which he would have to play the part 11 a mere second or dummy to perhaps half a dozen Itole colleagues in the House of Commons.. The in- Conveniences of the late arrangement have been too Palpable to bear repetition, and Liberal chiefs, men (If influence, and the wire pullers of the party, can- 11.ot he blind to the discord that the selection of their party leader from a class which is cut off from the Chamber in which the work of the country is Pettily done would now produce both in the House :1! Commons and the country. No such party leader, Il.roited to a speech or two at the opening of a ses- lion and a speech or two when the great Bills of the ,house of Commons come up to the Lords at the Close of a session, can possibly impress Liberalism 4r make way with his party. Lord Rosebery's !eeignation, therefore, marks simply (mother stage lb democratic development.</p>
        <p>The vagaries of French writers on England can- -4,0t well be matched anywhere. A new writer, M. t4Ouis Martin Chazey, seems anxious to distance all countrymen. Fie believes everything to our qlscredit, and puts it into his book. Amongst other ,things he gravely states is that Arthur Young in 787 and 1789 sowed the seeds of the French Revo- lution. Our young children are fed on chalk milk ,t 0 Make room for their successors, because chalk tills the young. On Sundays, instead of reading the Bible,Englishmen find their occupation in pain- 11111Y deciphering the labels of their bottles—" Old Torn Gin," "014 Scotch Whisky." We speak of ourselves personified as John Bull, but our true title is "John Snake." Several of our habits and customs he dese.ribes as " schoking," and Dickens, first are told, wrote a book called "Pickwik," whilst uur meal in the day is our" breachfeater."</p>
        <p>There are 0/ninon.' hints of something amiss in the Mediterranean Flee. The ordinary Fleet and the ?articular Service Fquadron do not amalga- &amp;its. Rear-Admiral Dale, the commander of the tatter, has asked the Lords of the Admiralty to Cow him to be relieved of his command, and it teems that his ship, the Revenge, is under orders L4r home. There was Rll idea at one time (if recall- the whole of the Particular Service Squadron, Kit it, now looks as if Rear-Admiral Dale would e home, and the new commander, Sir John Hop- i na, would add the squadron permanently to his trividual command. This would seem to be the at solution of existing doubts and difficulties.</p>
        <p>%Public attention is returning to the Armenian restion, which, to the dismay of many leading dberals who had taken part in the agitation,seemed '0 be in danger of disannearinq under the excite- Itbent created by Lord Roftebery's resignation and tibe? depressing influence of his speech. It, is now ii2ng realised that neither of these influences is .2Lely to be so disastrous as was feared. The influ- Tice of the agitation in England on the settlement ,the question mainly depended on foreign coun- AP.I!S being convinced that it waq. a genuine and lelnterested movement, and, as Sir William Ilar- titirt said, "the voice of the nation." If Lord .°1„ ebery had made his speech as leader of the Ziberal party it would have discounted the import- of the agitation most seriously, but his resig- '4tlon before speeking seems likely to have an t:ltire!y contrary eff:•ct. In resigning his office be- he could neither restrain, nor guide the rising popular voice he gave the strongest proof i.ta irresistible force, and in foreign countries his lißnstion is likely to be regarded as only one kereyee less significont than would be that of Lord s'illsbury on similar grounds.</p>
        <p>„ The feeling of Sotch Liharalism oil the leadership Itlestion has been one of the elements about which 5011ticans in London have been most uncertain and itl.Zious. Londoners see before their eyes every to striking instances of the ” clannishness " of iiTteh men, and consequently there is an impres- an. just or unjust. that Scotchmen often set aside IT Other and higher considerations in order to stand tiT a compatriot. After the Pdinburgh meeting thete was a natural fear that this characteristic of t2scotch people might sway them so far from a t:l9trd for the supreme intereste of the party as to Zs a dogged stand nn what they might think the LTonal interests of Lord Rosebery, even to the I'M of bringing about a schism in the ranks. The ileht4rs which are now coming to hand and the as- Onces of such leaders of Scotch Liberalism as te"e Come to town are entirely dissipating these .es. It is confidently stated that on the abstract kestion of a peer leader fully half the Scottish %Millie have all throug.h recognised that the diffi- i uLges were overwhelming, and that t would be an intage if, without any slight to individuals, a "ge would be made.</p>
        <p>pt!'ithough they are fast receding into the dim re- Vote of history, the world has not forgotten that Chamberlain had his "Radical days." The pre- %,e stages of his political development are, how- not so well remembered. They are likely to 4,_4recalied to memory next month, when the Secre- lb,;3' for the Colonies is to be the guest of the even- qbat the dinner of the Birmingham Parliamentary Itting Society, of which forty years or more ago ha "8 a member, and was noted as a leading ex- -1416.nt of Conservative views. This was.of course, titerlB extreme political youth,and before he became kth,•etarY of the Birmingham Republican Club and liek'eved notoriety as such. Birmingham's "Repub- tottlYeyor" has in these later days apparently hu'eted the circuit, and reached the point from tltta-Iu set out. The chief interest in his future titer le whether his development will take an en- -114 lY 11'"Iv direction, or whether ha will follow the ..„,,2_1155 and make the circuit a second time.</p>
        <p>TEE WORLD TUNS AWAY FROM ITS. THE other day we had a talk with a man who knew as little of the world around him as a baby. Yet he was a man of naturally fine intelligence. He had just been relieved from prison. Ten years ago he was incarcerated under n life sentence. Recently, however, circumstances had arisen which proved his innocence and he obtained his freedom. But nothing seemed as before. He had been stationary while the world moved on. Many of his old friends were dead, and all were changed. A big slice of his career was lost, and worse than lost. Could he ever make it up again ? No, never. Besides, although he had committed no offence, the mere fact that he had been :convicted of one, would always place him at a disadvantage. Different as it is in all outward conditions long illness produces results which resembles those of enforced soli- tude. When confined to our homes by disease we are virtually out of the world. Friends may, and do, pity us ; but they do not lie down by our side and suffer with us. Ah ! no. They go their own ways and leave us alone. In the midst of company we are still alone. E 3. joyment, food, sleep, fresh air, movement, work, &amp;c.— those are for them, not for us. Alas ! for the poor prisoner whose jailer is some relentless disease. Who shall open the iron doors and set him free? "I never had any rest or pleasure." So writes a man whose letter we have just finished reading. "In the early part of 1888," he says," a strange feeling came over me. I felt heavy, drowsy, languid, and tired. Something appeared to be wrong with me,and I couldn't account for it. I had a foul taste in the mouth, my appetite failed, and what I did eat lay on me like a stone. Soon I became afraid to eat, as the act wa I always followed by pain and distress. Sometimes I had la sensation of choking in the throat as if it could not swallow. I was swollen, too, around the body, and got about with difficulty owing to increasing weakness. "At the pit of my stomach was a hungry, craving Isensation, as though I needed support from food; yet the littlo I took did not abate this feeling. My sleep was broken, and I awoke in the morning unrefreshed. For four years' I continued in this wretched state before I I found relief."</p>
        <p>This letter is signed by Mr. Charles H. Smith, of 19, New City Road, Glasgow, and dated February 15th, 1893.</p>
        <p>Before we hear 11:Av he was at last delivered from the slavery of illness, let us listen to the words of a lady on the same theme: Mrs. Mary Ann Rusting, of Station Road, Misterton, near Gainsborongh. In a brief note dated January 3rd, 1893, Mrs. Rusting says she suffered in a similar way for over fifteen years. Her hands and feet were cold and clammy, and she was pale an d bloodless. She had pain in the left side and palpitation, and her breathing was short and hurried. No medicines availed to help her until two years ago. "At that time," she says. "our minister, .the late Rev. Mr. Watson, told me of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. and urged me to make a trial of it. I did so, and presently felt grmat relief. It was not long before the bad symptoms all left me, and I gradually got stropg. I keep in good health, and have pleasure in making known to others the remedy which did so much for me."</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith was completely cured by the same remedy, and save had be known of it sooner he would have been saved years of misery.</p>
        <p>The real ailment in both these cases was indigestion and dyspepsia, with its natural con- cequences. Throughout the civilised world its course is marked by a hundred forms of pain and suffering. Men and women are torn to piepes by it as vessels are by the rocks on which they are driven by tempests. So comprehensive and all-embracing is it that we may almost say that there is no other disease. It s;-, 'Fos life transformed into death,bread turned into poison Watch for its earliest signs—especially the feel- ing of weariness, lanE.Tour, ant fatigue, which announce its approach. Prevention is better than cure. But, by the use of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup,cier e is aiways possible; and poor :captives in the loathsome dungeons of illness are daily delivered as the hand of the good Germo nurse swings open the doors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WARLINGTON CYCLING cm.</head>
        <p>WARLINGTON CYCLING cm.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night last week the members of the above club inaugurated their winter prrgramnse by holding a grand smoking concert at the headquarters,Norton Arms Hotel. Toe large room was filled to excess by a large and enthusiastic body of wheelmen including representatives from Widnes, Bowdon. and other cycling clubs. The chair was occupied by the President Mr. T. Morris, and amongst those present supporting him were Messrs. J. Jeffrey, A. Webster, T. J. Hulse, Dr. Kisby, C. Leigh, C. H. Wood, J. Critchlev, J. Podmore and Messrs. A. Beoth and E. Pickavance, jun., hon. secretaries of the club. The mtvical part of the programme was arranged by Mr. J. Davenport (one of the members) and he is to be congratulated on the splendid array of talent he provided for the occasion. Mr. J. Pemberton sang " Maid of Athens," and " Let MP like a Soldier fall," in a masterly manner; while Mr Sherlock was accorded a grand reception for his rendering of "The Storm Fiend ". and "The Mighty Deep." Mr. J. Davenport's splendid tenor voice was heard to great advantage in " Mice, whe e art thou " and in the duet with Mr. E. Whitehouse. "The Sunlit Highway" WAS daintily sung by Mr. E. Whitehouseithe same gentleman singing the solo part of the National Anthem at the close. The Glee Party consisting of Messrs. Seattergood, Davenport, Whitehouse and Sher- lock, sang" The Banks of Allan Water," "Comrades in Arms," and "The Tack," in .such a manner as to provoke the utmost enthusiasm, their efforts being repeatediy redemanded. The lion of the evening was Mr. Hubert Langford, the eminent society entertainer, who has been giving recitals to crowded and fashionable gatherings at Ryhl and other noted resorts. There is no doubt whatever that he is in the front rank of humorists, and Mr. Davenport is to be congratulated on securing his services for the benefit of the club. His songs were, "DP ole umbrella," "Dutch woman's dame," and " Jostice Dimple." and for each he was vociferously encored. His great feature, however, was his humorons sketch, entitled "At a party." The audience vile! simply convulsed with laughter for 20 minutes, and at the finish he was accorded a tremen- dous ovation. The accompanist was Mr.F.Booth, and he acquitted himself admirably in every respect. During an interval in the proceedings, Mr. Jeffrey proposed the health of the President, which was heartily accorded. Mr. Morris briefly responded and expressed his pleasure at being amongst the members again. Mr. T. J. Bugle, in a few well chosen remarks, proposed it vote of thanks to the artistes. In seonding. it Mr. E Pickavance, junior, in a witty little speech expressed on behalf of himself and fellow clubmen their great indebtedness to the artistes who had done so mush to entertain them, and ventured the remark that the smoker was only ti.e forerunner of many such gatherings the committee in- tended to hold during the coming winter season. The vote of thanks was carried with acclamation. The pro- ceedings closed with the National Anthem, and the party separated after one of the m )st successful and enjoyable smokers the club as ever held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>SIR WILFRID LAWSON ON THE TURKS AS TEETOTALERS.</head>
        <p>SIR WILFRID LAWSON ON THE TURKS AS TEETOTALERS.</p>
        <p>Speaking at a temperance meeting at Maryport on Monday evening, Sir Wilfrid Lawson said he had seen a statement lately in some newspapers that there could be no good in total abstinence from drink, because the Turks were teetotalers. It was said, " Here are these wicked Turks filling the world with horrors,and they are teetotalers," as if that were the cause of it. These Turks were taught from their youth up to execute these massacres. It was part of their religion,and he believed that if the people of England had been taught from their youth up that it was their business to fall upon a cer- tain set of people whenever they saw them, and that they would go to Paradise if they did so, they would do it even iff teetotalers. He quite agreed that being tee- totalere' did not prevent the Turks doing all this ; hut on the other hand, if they were not teetotalers would they be any better ? If there was any sense in this ar- gument about the Turks, being teetotalers, the remedy would be for them to take to drink, but, in his opinion, .that would simply result in making them both mur- derers and drunkards. Rut he was not quite sure that drink was not the cause of these massacres. He had read somewhere—he did not say it was true, as it was impossible to believe a lot they read—that the Sultan had taken to drink, and it was quite possible that drink might be at the bottom of all these awful atrocities. -</p>
      </div>
