      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>INQUEST.</head>
        <p>INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroner Brighouse conducted an inquiry at the Police Station on Monday relative to the death of John Stapleton,earter,who was fatally injured on Friday. The Town Clerk and Mr. T. Longdin (borough sur- veyor) were present on behalf of the Corporation. Mr. J. Dignum appeared for the firm by whom deceased was employed. Mrs. Staplet4n, wife of deceased, 11, Gee-st.,Warring- ton, said he was 45 years of age,and a carter for Messrs. Jon. Blundell and Ce. She last saw him before the accident going from the Weighing machine about 11.30 on Friday morning. She next saw him dead at the In- firmary. Deceased had been working for the firm about 17 years. The horse he used was one he was in the habit of driving. Mr. John Dignum said the horse was a remarkably quiet one. They had had it two or three years. It had never taken frir,ht before. Arthur Wakefield, 112 Catherine-st., said he was in charge of the Corporation steam roller on Friday morning. He was a, the end of Guardian-st.. Froghall- lane, with his roller, when he saw deceased with a cart. He•gave a signal to sop and witness did so and put the break on so that the engine woal i not run back. The deceased signalled to witness's flagman to hold the horse's head. The flagman did so but when opposite the engine the horse bolted. Deceased at the time was walk- ing alongside of the horse, and both of thetas were hold- ing it. They were dragged some distance and the carter fell. He was thrown bodily across the footpath and partly on the road. The wheel of the cart passed over him. _</p>
        <p>In answer to ths coroner witness stated that he was quite stationary when the horse and cart passed him. Deceased was walking on the near side of the horse, which he was leading. The horse passed the roller later on the same afternoon and did not betray any fear. William Fleetwood, Ashton-street, gave similar evi- dence to the last witness. When the horse passed again the same afternoon it never took any notice of the engine but walked steadily past. Mr. James Evans said he was driving behind the engine. He saw it was stopped and the flagman hold of the cart-horse's head. When the horse bolted neither of them could hold it. The carthorse seemcd restive in ooming down the bridge. The driver of the roller and his flagman did all they needed and could do and the whole thing was a pure accident. Witness assisted by P.C. Fish picked deceased up when he got to him,but be was dead. . .</p>
        <p>WV Ea Mrs. Stapl4on in answer to the Coroner informed the jury that her husband was insured in the Royal Liver Society. The Town Clerk expressed on behalf of the Corpora- tion their very great regret that the accident had oc- curred. It appeared to be due to some extent to the presence of the steam roller. They were giving £5 to the widow for funeral expenses. Mr. Ditnum also spoke expressing the regret de- ceased's employers felt at his sad death. 7 A verdict sf " Accideptal Death" was returned. DEATH OF THE ARCHBISHOP OF CANTERBURY AT HA.WARDEN.</p>
        <p>A great sensation was caused in Hawardsn village on Sunday morning by the sudden and tragic death of the I Archbishop of Canterbury. The Archbishop and Mrs. Benson crossed from Belfast to Stranraer on Friday night and after an excellent passage took the train to Carlisle and rested for the night there. They reached Hawarden Castle on Saturday evening at six o'clock on a visit to Mr. and Mrs. Gladstone. The Countess Grosvenor and the Rector of Hawarden were the only guests at dinner. Mr. Henry Gladstone informed our representative that it was noticed how well the Archbishop looked, and in what good spirits he was. Ile was full of his Irish tour, and spoke of it with great delight, though is seemed it bad been by no means a mere holiday. On Sunday morning the Archbishop and Mrs. Benson were prevent at the eight o'clock celebration of the Holy Communion, and went again to church for the eleven o'clock service. The Archbishop walked from the Castle to the Church with Mr. Henry Gladstone and Mrs Drew. Mr. Gladstone, owing to slight indisposition, did not accom- pany them. Mrs. Gladstone, Mrs. Benson, and Miss Dorothy Drew drove from the Castle in a closed car- riage, arriving at the church a little later. It was not intended that the Archbishop should take any part in the service and he sat in the chancel in the seat usually oc- cupied by Mr. Gladstone. A few minutes after the ser- viee commenced, when the Rector (the Rev. Stephen Gladstone) was reading the Absolution, Mr. Haswell, a member of the choir, observed a peculiar movement of one of the Archbishop's arms, and the Archbishop, who was in a kneeling position, then fell forward in a state of collapse. The sudden seizure caused a general sensation among the congregation, but the service was continued. Mr. Henry Gladstone and Mr. C. B. Toiler, a church- warden,immediately went to the Archbishop's assistance, and with the help of other members of the congregation carried him to the Rectory, where he was place A on a ' couch in the Lihrary. Dr. Burlington, who happened to be present, attended him, and was soon joined by Dr. Roberts, another local medical man. Dr. r Burlingham states that the Archbishop gasped feebly as he was being borne out of the church, but' was quite unconscions. Respiration ceased very rapidly, and the Archbishop nagged peacefully away. Mr. Henry Gladstone, Mr. Dr!w, two protemional nurses,and others rendered assistance. The unexpected announcement by Dr. Burlington that life was extinct received with great grief by all those present. When the Primate was removed from church it was not thought that he was suffrring from anything more serious than an ordinary fainting fit. The Rector, addressing the congregation, said : "I am asked to state that the Arch- bishop. of Canterbury, who has been in our midst, has had a fainting fit. He had an especially fatiguing time in Ireland,and no doubt this has effected his health. He was here at the early celebration this morning. He has been removed, and has evervthifig around him that he requires. May I ask the congregation to remember him when we say the Litany? While the Litany was be- ing read Mr. Henry Gladstone and Mr. C. B. Toltec entered the church and whispered something to the Rector. The Rector continued reading, and at the conclusion of the Litany offered a spe- cial prayer for the Armenians. Theo, addressing the congregation, he said " I think it better to let the congregation know that I have just heard that the Archbishop has passed away. We shall therefore sing a last hymn now; then I shall read the Funeral Collect at the altar." The hymn" For ever with the Lord " was sung, and the Rector Teed the Funeral Collect. The organist, Mr. Pringle, next played the Devi March from "Saul," the congregation remaining standing. Dr. Dohie, who was sent for from Chester, did not arrive until after death. The medical men held a con- sultation, and concurred is the opinion that dea,ki was due to failure of the heart's action. Tnough no inquest will be necessary, Dr. Burlingham has communicated a statement to the Coroner to the effect that the Arch- bishop was suddenly seizsd with a stroke of apoplexy, and became instantly unconscious The tragic news was broken to Mr. Gladstone by Mrs. Gladstone on her return to the Castle. Mr. Gladstone bore it with won- derful fortitude, although it came as an alarming shock.</p>
