      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ST ITEs.</head>
        <p>ST ITEs.</p>
        <p>MILLIONAIRES AND THE PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>A special correspondent of the "Telegraph " in the United States, writing on " Giant Monopolies and Big Fortunes," gives some particulars of the tremendous trade" combines" on the other side of the Atlantic. The law, he says, shuts out foreign competition, and monopoly holds the field. The short road to the huge fortunes of trade in the Republic has had for its direc- tion, " Bar the foreigner and crush the small competi- tor." He proceeds : " During the last six weeks I have travelled some thousands of miles in New York State, Connecticut, Rhode Island, Massachusetts, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Mary- land,and New J .rsey, and have seen something of the working of the sweet simplicity of this system. The city of Cleveland may serve as an illustration. Cleveland is a town of 300,000 inhabitants, with manufactures of steel and iron worth some nine millions s erling per annum ; it is also the home of Mr. John D. Rockefeller, and the headquarters of the Standard Oil Company. The steel and try) business may he better treated in re- ference to Pittst,ird, but the S'andard Oil Company deserves a few lines all to itself. Tne commercial his- tory of the whole world elsewhere has nothing to equal the daring and success of this marvellous combination. It is the parent and model of all the trusts in the United States, and in principals are the richest men in the Republic, perhaps in the world. Contrary to every pre- sumption one would naturally make in alvance,the men who founded this gigantic corporation did not own the oil lands, nor discover new processes of refining, nor bring capital into the business. The chief of them had been a clerk in a country store.' and about 1802, in the midst of the Civil War, and when the great oil era was just beginning, he contrived with difficulty to start a little refinery in Cleveland. His counsel, some years later, described him as man of brains and energy,but without money.' With him were associated his brother and an English mechanic, and by and by others joined them, until the coterie included nom@ thirteen all told, none of them having much capital. But they had Dan. ton's audacity. They combined and combined. After a struggle of a few years they got hold of the railways that command the outlets from the oil region ; they obtained special rebates,' that enabled them to outbid and undersell small competitors. This power they grad- ually extended, until every oil producer and refiner in Ohio and Pennsylvania was at their mercy. Some they bought out,many they starved. The prosperity of the oil region, which had been astounding, was suddenly arrested. Intense public discontent was engendered, which brought the injured country to the verge of civil war. A contract with the Erie, the Pennsylvania, New York Central, Atlantic and Great Western, and the Lake Shore Railway Companies roused the people in 1872 to aetual violence. _</p>
        <p>"There was an incipient revolution in Pennsylvania, and the startling contracts—the existence of which was denied by the Oil Company—were declared by the several riiilway chiefs to be cancelled.' But the popular out- break had no lasting effects, and the monopolists con- tinued their victorious march. They reduced the sup- plies, raised the prices, and controlled markets. Thus, the capital of about zero in 1882 was eight years later worth £200,000: in t‘vo years more, £500.000 ; in 1875, £700,000 ; in 1882, £14.000,000; and in 1888, "accord- ing to the testimony of the Trust President,"£29,6oo,9oo. Then the New York and Ohio legislatures arrived at the opinion that the proceedings of the trust were unlawful, and in 1892 the patriotic gentlemen composing it "dig- solved"—when their estate had got to a market valua- tion of 03,300,000. Dissolved—but never a soul in known to have taken out his quoto of the assets. Why ? Because the Standard Oil Company still persists, and still controls all the markets During thii time dividends had risen upwards of £2,000,000 sterling per annum. What they have been of late years nobody knows. A report of the N.Pf York Senate says All this vast wealth is the growth of about twenty years; the prop- erty has more than doubled in value in six years and with the increano the trust has made aggregate dividends of over 50.000 000 dollars. It is one of the moat active and poasibly the most formidable moneyed power on this continent." Two years ago the Attorney General of /taw York declared :—" The members of this combination are now presidents and directors of 33,000 miles of railroad, one-fifth of the total mileage o, the United States. Its surplus is invested in banking, in natural and manu- facturing gas companies, in beds of iron ore and coal, and in crude oil production, in lead and zinc, in turpen- tine and cottonseed oil, in steel, in jute manufacture, in ocean steamships, in palatial hotels, and in street rail- roads." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Six years ago a friendly New York journal made out that Mr. Rockefeller's income was 20.000.000.i015. a year, which, it was observed,proudly, 'makes him the richest man in the United Statea—nerhapg in the world.' Another paper raised it to 36,000,003d01g. I believe these to be huge exaggerations. The secretary of the company modestly put his own income at £750.000 per annum, and that of the principal members of the concern—he did not say the chief—at £1,800,000. The total of Mr. Rockefeller's estate is reekoned here variously at some- where between £25,000.000 sterling and twice as much. There may well be discrepancy ; it is diffieult to keep pace with the growth of such colossal fortunes. A few days age a little piece of his real estate—a beautiful sum- mer•honae at North Tarrytown—which had been rated at £2o,ooo,was put up by the Commissioners to £200,000 The whilom ' bookkeeper of a country store' protests. Methinks he cloth protest too much. Meanwhile, there are other worlds to conquer. Mr. Rockefeller is the owner of a latee steel plant near Cleveland, and he is said to be forming an enormous pool in steel. The little scheme credited to this Napoleon of combinations is to control the Writ as Carnegie is said to do the Rage. I am inclined to believe the report because it is so vehe- mently denied. "Riding on the Baltimore and Ohio line the other day, while the train was winding through the sinuous, rugged, and picturesque valley of the Potomac, the thought occurred how wonld the British housekeeper stand the tyranny of these trusts. Think of him retir- ing to rest on Septembgel, and waking next morning to find that coal had risen fis. a ton. Ridiculous.absurd,im- possible ! Yet, this is just what has happened there. The anthracite coal region of Pennsylvania,is in the hands of a combine,' as the slang ig—including the railways— and they have recently made the announcement of a rise of idol. 50c. Anthracite is a hard and nearly smokeless fuel, universally burnt in houses and many factories, and its monepolists control 50.000.000 tons per annum. The little rise they have just ordered means, therefore, a net addition to their income of £15,000,000 sterling. The simple-minded Briton, reared in a land where the strongest corporations are weak be- fore the law. asks :—• What is the reasoitnfixfoors ee tphriis?3'. But the Coal Trust does not give reagent, This is the great American secret of commercial success. 'fix your own prices.' I have before me a list of 139 combinations, pools, syndicates combines,' and trusts covering nearly everything that human beings eat' drink, or use, each one of them trying to emulate the' Standard Oil exemplar. •' Nothing is to, big or too little for these concerns, from locomotives to lucifer matches, beef to buttons. I can find scarcely anything of congequence that has es- caped, except bicycles, and a friend assures me that a cycle trust is a dead certainty very soon. While I am writing comes the news that the Leather Trust has cornered and raised the price of that commodity. 31 the game gees merrily on. And yet the Constitution of the Republic, the laws of the several States, the decisions of the State Courts and of the Supreme Court are all against these monopolies. If it were not so woful it would be ludicrous to see how the Standard Oil Company has set , at defiance for years State Legislatures and judges ; yea, even the Federal executive and the Interstate Commerce Commission of Congress. There is an absolute mono- tony of condemnation by the people, the Legislature, and the law. Yet I fail to find a single instance of effec- tive legal restraint, while all the time the evils are so palpable. Bankruptcy, broken hearts, and death,' says Mr. Demarest Lloyd. have kept quick step with the march of the conquerors in iron and oil.' rhe railways are the greatest offenders. The potent irresponsible rail- way magnates, it is universally believed, have shared the plunder; and, without any question, their preferen- tial treatment has created and ruined trades. Hence the strength of the Populist cry that the great means of con- veyance shall be owned by the State. No man of sense will allow hinuelf to be dazzled with the aggregated splendour of these illicit dealings, nor mollified by the stories of this or that charitable contribution. " All this has to do, and much to do, with the Presi- dential election. If it be poesible for the Orator of Nebraska to reach the white House he will owe his triumph to the hatred of trusts as much as to Free oilver. In the speech which Mr, Bryan gave in New York noth- ing more pleased his vast audience in Tammany Hall than the following declarations :—" If I am elected as the Chief Executive. I shall use all the power I have Lo drive every trust in this country out of existence as quickly as possible. While I do not wish to array class against class, I am willing to array all the people who suffer from the trusts against the few who compose them." . . _</p>
        <p>" I regret to say that one hears nnthing like this from Mr. M'Hinley. The Republican party is pledged to Pro- tection, the main prop of more than half the trusts. The Democrats, guided by Mr. Cleveland, made sugar free ; then the republicans reimplied a duty ot 40 per cent ad valorem, and the Sugar Trusts springs into being again. It is said thlt when this orvanisation can put up sugar half a cent—per pound it means revenue to its coffers at the rate of four millions sterling per annum. It is pleasant to know that German competition,</p>
        <p>ROWLA.ND3' MACA.SgAR OIL is the only reliable and genuine nreserver *ma beautifier of the hair ; it prevents it falling off (n. t,irning grey, anti eradicites Scurf awl Dandriff is also sold in a Golden Colour for fair hairei ladies and children. Bottles. 3s, 6d., 7s ; 10s. 6d. equal to four small.</p>
        <p>ROW ANDS' ODONTO i the best and safest dentifrice which can be used, as it contains no acid, mineral or gritty ngredients ; it whitens the teeth, prevents and arrests decay and sweetens the breath, 2s. 91 per box, Said by Chemists, Perfumers and Stores ; can be sent post free by A. ROWILLND &amp; SONa, 20, Hatton Garden, London, on receipt of advertised prices in postal order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WHY WOMEN ARE ATTRACTIVE.</head>
        <p>WHY WOMEN ARE ATTRACTIVE.</p>
        <p>Why is one woman attractive and another not ? It isn't entirely a question of age, or features, or intellect. The most admirable and attractive thingabout an attrac- tive woman is her womaniinefA. Everybody admires a womanly wonaan. She must have laealth, of course, be- cause without it she would lose the brightness of her eyes, the fullness of her cheeks, and her vivacity. Health brings all these things, but health means more than most people think of. If pale, nervous, and weak, a woman lacks good health. Women who are pale and wan should not resort to iron, drugs, and tonics, except by the advice of a properly qualified medical man. They should try instead to nourish and build up their blood by the vital nouriPhment imparted by Dr. Tibbles' Vi. Cocoa. And so rosy cheeks and comeliness may be attained. Surely the road is pleasanter than the thorny and nasty path paved with drugs. Nurse TIIIO'BOO, Alexander Hotel, St. Leonards-on-Sei., writes : " I have tried Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa and like it very much. I shall have much pleasure in recommend- ing it to my patients." Miss S Percival, Post OFfice, Burgh, writes:—"l do not think any other can equal yours. My father has been taking ordinary cocoa, but I think Dr. Tihbles' Vi-Cocoa is better. I will tell my friends of your Vi-Cocoa." Mrs. King, Lineen Cottage, Wimbledon Hill, Surrey, I writes :—"I think Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is delicious, and quite fulfils all said ab eit it." Mrs. Midden, Bradwardine, Bournemouth, writes : " I am pleased with Dr, Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and like it and will certainly use it in future." Merit, and merit alone, is what we claim for Dr. Tibble's Vi-Cocoa, and we are prepared to send to any reader is ho nanvs the Warrington " Examiner " a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibble's Ti•Cocea free and post- paid. There is no magic in all this. It is a plain honest, straightforward offer. It is done to introduce the merits of Vi-Cocoa into every home. Dr. Tibblea' Vi-Cocoa, as a concentrated form of nourishment and vitality is invaluable ; nay, more than this ; for to all who wish to face the strife and battle of life with greater endurance and more sustained exertion, it is absolutely indispensable. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is made up in 61. packets and 9d. and is. 61. tins. It can be obtained from all chemists, grocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, Limited, Suffolk House, Cannon Street, London. E. C. Write for free sample.</p>
