      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ALLEGED SERLOTTS ASSAULT AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED SERLOTTS ASSAULT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE CASE FOR THE PROSECUTION. HAMPSON ADMITTED TO BAIL.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday morning, be- fore the Mayor (in the chair). Alderman Harrison and Mr. Monks, John Flarnpson was brought up on remand charged with inflicting grievous bodily harm upon Joseph Coleshaw, of Wazabrook-street. Mr. Jenkins said he appeared to prosecute in this case. They would remember the facts connected with the last remand, to which he intended to refer very little indeed. There were times perhaps when it was as well to goiern oneself and exercise restraint. There was only one communication, perhap-,in ju tice to him- self that he should read, and it was from the Town Clerk Town Ci'rk'4 Office, Warrington, 89! October, 1896. Dear Sir,—Re Hampson. I understand that the igistrates' Clerk has taken the opinion of the Public Prosecutor as to your right to appear in this case. Having seen that opinion, which is in your favour, the Chief Constable will now hand over to you the prosecution of the case, and give you any assistance you require.—Yours truly, J. LYON Winrrut. Messrs. Jenkins and Co., solicitors." Mr. Jenkins added that a letter had been received by him from the Public Prosecu- tor on the subject which placed his right as paramount. Re was sorry to say that the oil man Coleshavr was not stiff ciently well to come to the court that day, and he simply asked for a further remand for eight days. Alderman Harrison : Is it true that his leg is broken?</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins: I don't think that is fair. I may say something to prejudiee Hamoson, but I have ne desire to do so. Mr. Monks : We will confine ourselves to the remand to-day. Mr. Jenkins The ordinary gossip of the town has nothing at all to do with the matter. Alderman Harrison : My only reason for asking was that it seems such a pity to be bringing Mr. Hampton here every week. and I want to know whether there is a probability of his being brought here frequently in con- sequence of the alleged broken leg Mr. Jenkins: I cannot agree with you again. It is not a case for pity whatever. If a man chooses to place himself in Mr. Bampson'a position he does not deserve any eymnathy of that kind at all. I have simply—and I think I shall do it—to impartiallY exercise the rights of a prosecutor in this case, and pity where wickedness is concerned should not enter into our eonsideration. Mr. Browne said he told them last week that he could not possibly object to a remincl,and he could not consent to any longer remand that eight days, because the statute did not permit more. But Htmpson had been in gaol a fortnight, and he suggested that as the injured man was progressing favourably there could be no °Mac- tion to bail being allowed of a substantial nature. Mr. gampson had been known in Warrington for a consider- able number of years, and be submitted that this was a case, seeing that all danger was over to the injured per son, when substantid bail ought to be allowed. The only object as that he should appear a* the trial. If they got substantial bail it would save these unnecessary remands.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins said he bad been instructed by the rela- tives of the injured man to make no objection to substan- tial bail being al:owed. He was, of course, sorry to see Mr. Hammon in such a position. The Chief Constable said he had made inquiries at the Infirmary, and found that the injured man was getting on nicely, and that he would be able to appear in the course of a few days.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: Adjourn the case sine die with an inti- mation to those engaged in it when the prisoner will be again brought up. The Chief Constable: His (Hampson's) friends should look closely after him. Mr. Browne: He is an army pensioner, and his ad- dress is bound to be known. The Chief Constable: I don't fear that he will run away. I fear for the man's own safety. Mr. Hanaoson junr., said he would undertake that nothing wrong went on ;so far as his father was con- cerned.</p>
        <p>Mr. Monks; 1 think we must exact one condition: that he must keep sober during the time. Prisoner said that he would have no drink, and be would also keep out of public-houses. The Bench consented to accept prisoner's son's bail of ZlOO.</p>
        <p>Two STIIANGIC FALLS. An inquest on the body of Sarah Andrews, 129, Church-street, Warrington, was conducted by Mr. R. Brighouse at the Parade Rgotn,Warrington,onMon- day.—John Andrews,husband of deceased, said she was 51 years of age. On the 27th August she went to Southport for the day with her daughter and her sister-in-law. She appeared to be in good health. Previously however she had complained of dizzi- ness. When witness met deceased at the station she told hire she had had a fall. She did her work un- til Saturday when Dr.T. S. Smith was called in. She died on Sunday,October 11th. Previous to the 27th August she had been well but since then she bad been ailing.—A letter was read from Dr. T. S. Smith which stated that the fall was due to the diseased condition of the arteries of deceased's brain.—Mrs. Elizabeth Chorley said that on the date in question she was at S3utliport with deceased. They were coming out of a restaurant when she turned round and fell. Ihere was nothing in her way to cause her to fall. She was apparently dizzy. She fell in az exactly similar manner at Southport about a month or six weeks previous to the 27th Angust. Deceased hal had dizzy bouts before.—A verdict ot death from natural causes, most probably apoplexy was returned. , ,</p>
      </div>
