      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NOTES ANP NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ANP NEWS.</p>
        <p>The following curious story is mkt in the ladies'cllumn of the" Whitehall Review": A few of us thought it, would be a good idea to beg an audience with the Czar- ina, and ask her to use her influence in getting somo- , thing done to stay the hand of the Sultan. Her Im- perial Majesty consented to give us an interview, on ,1 condition that it was kept private until such time as she ' had left England. One afternoon, ten days ago, two of our members and I went to Bilmoral. The young Em- press received us with grave dignity. She was dressed in a flowing robe of white silk, resembling a tea-gown and was very fair to see. There were bands of golden embroidery on the bodice and the long hanging sleeves, and her lovely head emerged from her white draperies flower-like in its beauty. I stated our business briefly. The Empress remarked that, so far, she had interfered little in politics, but expressed herself ready to do any- thing in her power to help in bringing about a crusade against the existing circumstances in Constantineple. "A more womanly or Christian action," remarks i the Empress, "than helping to alleviate the sufferings of the poor Armenians is hardly conceivable. Rest assured of my heartfelt interest in the cause you have at heart."</p>
        <p>Comparisons between the entry into Paris of the Czar' ma and that of Queen Victoria in 1855 seem much in favour of that city at present. It is recalled that when' the Queen was obliged by the rain to shelter under an umbrella, one of the crowd, who perceived that it was not very effective offered his own, which was accepted' with a smile. The next day he received his umbrella back again and with it a superb gold snuff box, on which were engraved the Royal Arms.</p>
        <p>From private information we learn says the "Daily Chronicle" some interesting details about the bombard- ment: of Zanzibar. The steamer Wasgow, which defied tlaie British arms was manned by natives who were evi- dently unacquainted with the guenery of a British war- ship. They opened fire with the saluting guns with which the Glasgow was in the habit of hailing the com- ing or speeding the parting guest. At first the British flagship took no notice of this performance. One ball hit an Italian vessel, which promptly slipped its cable, and moved out of range. When the British Admiral understood that the Glasgow, only 400 yards off, was firing at him, he signalled that she had better desist. The saluting guns paused a while, the skipper of the Glasgow trying to decipher the signal; then the fire began again. Summoned to strike their flag, the Arabs had net the least idea what this meant, and a crew of bluejackets had to make it plain by hoisting the Union Jack on the defi- ant steamer. Presently the Glasg3w began to sink, and as it was obviously indecorous for the Union Jack to sink with her, the British flag had to be hauled down.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile the Palace was being knucked to pi.ces, and the wife of the late Sultan—the Sultan who was supposed to have been poisoned by the usurper Said !Nand—was observed in a dangerous position standing on the fragment of a parapet with a wall crumbling on either side of her. In her fright she had lost her veil, and with the instinct of her caste she felt that she must hide her face in something; but nathing was handy ex- cept a musical box. When she Was rescued by the blue- jackets she was found secluded in this instrument from the gaze of the infidel. What tune the musical box played on this occasion is not recorded.</p>
        <p>It has been mentioned that Mr. Mackis, the new edi- tor of the "Leeds Mercury," suffered for some time from an injury to his eyesight, caused by a blow from a cricket ball, and it may be added that he also wears a single eyeglass, whereby hangs a tale. When he was a reporter at Manchester he was sent to report a lecture given by the lato Professor Freeman. at the Athenaeum, and, as was his habit when specially interested, put up his glass and intently regarded the speaker. Professor Freeman, always a very irritable person, chancing to look down at the reporters' table, in the midst of a sen- tence, was.like the Ancient Mariner, held by the glitter- ing eye, paused, and almost broke down. Re made a fresh start, but again caught the shining eyeglass intently turned upon him, and, abruptly stopping, said quite snappishly, "I do wish, sir, you would not glare at me se." It was Mr. Mackie's turn to be disconcerted; he dropped both glass and head too. andlthe professor was himself again.</p>
        <p>Sympsychography is a portentous word,and so it should be to sum up worthily the phenomena with which it is concerned. The world knows something already of the remarkable photographic experiences of Mr. Inglis Rogers, the American spiritualist,who professes to have obtained pictorial images of certain mental processes. Mr. Rogers alleges that after contemplating fixedly for a time so simple a work of art as a British postage stamp,he then placed himself before the lens of:a ca:nera in a dark room, and was rewarded by finding that the sensitised plate reproduced a shadowy portrait of our racious Sovereign, with the words "One Penny " on the brim. Mr. D. S. Jordan, however, a gentleman holding a distinguished university position on the other side of the Atlantic, has now. contrivad to go one better than his predecessor in this branch of occult science. He gets ,together six persons of suitable capac- ity' and bids them concentrate their minds upon the harmless, necessary cat—in the abstract, of course, without reference to any individual of the species. Each then projects this mental image into a separate lens, all the invisible rays being focussed upon a single plate,and the result is a representation of the ideal cat. Mr. Jordan hopes very soon to be able to reverse the process, and to extract from the feline brain a true picture of the ideal man. But here a difficulty comes in. The cats may re- fuse to take the matter seriously, and may laugh at the philosopher.</p>
        <p>According to the Australian papers just received ar- rangements are being carried out by Mr. Pound, the Queensland Government Bacteriologist, on behalf of the Central Rabbit Board, for tests for the destruction of rabbits by inoculation. The experiments will be made within a triple-fenced area, of which the inside fence will enclose half a square mile. The two outer fences will be half a mile apart, in order that the diseased rab- bits may net spread before the success of the experi- ments is demonstrated. A sum Jf £2,200 bas been spent on the exp-,riment.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of Ayr Tcwn Council a csmmunicatiou Was submitted frcm Chief Constable Mackay calling the attention of the Council to a recommendation by Cap- tain Monro, ELM. Inspector of Constabulary, as to bicycles being got for the use of the police in visiting the outlying districts of the burgh. The Chief Constable approved of the recommendation, and added that a number of the constables were trained cyclists.—Mr. Fraser said he was present when Captain Moor° in- spected the pilice force, and lie had stro -igly recom- mended the getting of bicyles. He(l!klr. Fraser) moved that the suggestion should be carried out.—Dean of Guild Girvan seconded.—Mr. Young moved that no bicycles be got. Where would a thief be the time the policeman was looking for his bicycle? (Laughter.) If the policemen had lighter boots they would be bettor able to give chase. Be thought that in a burgh like Ayr there was no need for a bicycle for the police.—Treasurer Meikle seconded the amendment. He thought the police officers in Avr were of too heavy calibre to ride bicycles. (Lsughter.)—The motion was defeated by a majority of 11 to O.</p>
        <p>These who advocate the paving of our streets with wood may And their enthusiasm for this form of pave- ment a little damped by the results of certain investiga- tions into the hygienic aspect of the question which are published in a French medical jaurnal. .Experiments made by MM. RODET and NrclioLas showed in brief that the " microbic infection "of wood pavement is not a mere surface matter which can be washed off. The micro-organisms exist in considerable numbers to a depth of at least two inches, and are present in enormous mul- titudes in superfieial layers of the wood. No amount of cleaning it is said, can possibly remove them from these parts, the result being that the wearing away of the wood must give rise to a form of dust very highly charged with microbic life. The experiments do Hot show however, in the opinion of the " Hospital," that the microhes are of a very injurious nature. " What they might be during an epidemic," continues that journal, " and how far they might then become associated av'th more active pathogenic forms, we can only guess by analogy, which in bacteriological matters is not a very safe proceeding ; so mat, as aff %irs stand at present, the case against" wood pave- ment a. 51 means of disseminating disease must be held to be 'net proven,'" althouga open to much suspicion." The results of these experiments, it may be added, would have been more interesting if we had been told how the microbic character of wood pavement compares with that of other forms of pavement.</p>
        <p>Professor Oliver Lodge writes to the " Li Yerpoal Nst , to warn the public against a notion that a lightning stroke is necessarily fatal. It stops the vital organs, he tells us,but it rarely destroys them. If respiration can be artificially maintained for a sufficient time there is a fair chance that the heart will resume its suspended action, and that the stricken man will recover. The practical outcome of this is never to pronounce a light- ning-struck person dead until the well-known method of resuscitation from drowning has been practised upon the apparent corpse for two or three hours. Experience has justified this teaching both in America and in France were it has been strenuously urged and practised by Dr. d'Arsonyal. This is a matter of great importance. Comparatively few people are killed by lightning in this country, but it seems probable enough, if we are to believe Professor Lodge, that the number could be still further reduced if artificial respiration were practised.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Priestley, who has three times contested the Stamford iivision in the Liberal interest, and who was formerly in the Bradford trade, was chosen on Men- ay evening as the Liberal candid at for the .Eastern division of Bradford. Mr. J.K. Hardie was unanimously selected as the Independent Labour candidate. Tile Conservatives met on Monday night to select their can- didate.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>o'l !)EAR ME, 1 FORGOT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>TO GIVE</head>
        <p>TO GIVE</p>
        <p>TUDOR WILLIAMS' BALSAIVi of HONEr''</p>
        <p>TO MY cRILDRP:N .BEFORE THEY</p>
        <p>REFIRED TO BED.</p>
        <p>I am certain thus' will cough all Night wizhout it. There is rilthiorz in the Market equal to it ; thorouzhic up to date. ONE OF THE MOST REMARKABL,* ,TEDIES OF THE AGE.</p>
        <p>TUDOR WILLIA.MS' PATEN r t %I</p>
        <p>OF HONEY.</p>
