      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>D. Tudor Williams, MEDICAL HALL, ABERDARE.,</head>
        <p>D. Tudor Williams, MEDICAL HALL, ABERDARE.,</p>
        <p>Sir William Harcourt, celebrated his sixty-niath birthday on Wednesday, and the squire of Malwood received a good many very pleasant congratulatione from divers and sundry quarters. He has always managed to maintain his early friendships, and to conduct his attacks upon members of his own poli- cal circle in a way that fails to disturb their friend- ship. That is, he has no malice, no spitefulness about him, and his sense of humour saves him in many a delicate situation. Just 14 ow his name figures in many ways, but he looks on quietly from his retreat in the New Forest, and shows more 'philosophy than some of his critics and advisers. Perhaps it is this power of reserve which makes the situation so interesting.</p>
