        <p>LIB speeca of Lord Rosnasia• Lord Roseber ' Speech. 5' 3 delivered at Edinburgh at the end of last week was a very notable utterance in inure raspuetS than one. •B will probably rank as the ablest and most power- ful of all Lord ROSEBERY 8 political orations. It is said that, while at tea beginning of the speech the audience seemed to be slightly offended bf! the line of argument with respect to the Turkiol3) atrocities which his lordship entered upon, before: the conclusion of the address there was an abed lute an:Annuity of opinion teat the case againA British intervention Bad been thorougtily esti&amp; lished. That is an oratorical achievement a which Lord RosEBBRY may well be proud._ A 9. it may be at once admitted that if the foreign' policy of this country is to be dominated and shaped in accordance with the principles lai down by the Fa-13.11EMIF.it the arguments colltainBt'. in the Edinburgh speecia are practic:Sely uns,nswer- able.. For what are the guiding rc.les whir.° Lord ROSEBBRY asks the people of this coungt to accept in their dealings with the problem rail by the Sultan's detorininatiou to exterminate e whole race of Christians ? 'They appear to be three in number. The first is that British interests"' construing the word " interests " in its malt material sense—are to be paramount. The socOl°. is, that, no matter how much we may be shoelo4. by the terrible tales of murder and mutilation, Wl' fate of the Armenians is not a British interest' The third is that a treaty which has not bee°. acted upon for nineteen years, except as reggae' one of its provisions, is not to be considered in tai way binding upon this country. If the people this country are prepared to accept these three' conditions as the "golden rules" of their foreigli: policy it may be taken for granted that the life el riSingle British sailor will be riske'' opzier to sava the Christian populatinii. 61- Armenia from auniailation. But I‘l4o, the British people prepared to fina