      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>VVESLEYAN CHAPEL, MooRE. On. Sunday the narvest thanksgiving services were aeld in the Wesleyan Chapel, Moore, when the pulpit at hoth the morning and evening services was occupied by 42. e Rev. J. Gunnell. As usual many willing hands isted in tbe appropriate decoration ot the chapel. The ?drum was neatly decorated with oat fringe, scarlet and dahlias ; the communion table with varied Z_llita and white auras, flanked with palms, cocoanut Zqd inciiarubber trees. A gigantic loaf was presented Z??_ _Mrs. Massey, and a beautiful miniature haystack was Z 4.08 by Mr. Joseph Forster. The following ladies and ite;titleixian assisted in the work of decorating :—The 111,1" es B. Dutton, Sloane, A. De Prez, and E. Brodie, Basnettt, Mrs. Banner, and Messrs. Fleet. Basnett, elle. and Forster. In addition to appropriate hymns, the choir ably rendered the anthem, " It is a good 1/123g to give thanks," the solo in which was sung by P. James Antrobus. Collections were made at the 21.1 e cf each service, which were considered very -stiefacterv. BAPTIST CHAPEL, GOLBORNE-STREET. ti,,un Sunday the annual autumnal services in conneo- -64 with the :above chapel were held when the Rev. 4rles Green, of Liverpool, preached, taking as his text th the morning St. John xx. 21, "Then said Jesus to Ment again, Peace be unto you : as My Father hath sent tse even so send I you, "and in the evening St. John vi. "Then Simon Peter answered him Lord, to whom 1:11 we go ? Thou haat the words of eternal life." Both t:lnons were attentively listened to and there were good rogations: The two anthems. "As pants the and "By Babylon's waters," were sung. Collect- "be were made in aid of the chapel funds.</p>
        <p>111, ',CASIIIRE AND CHREIIIIRE COUNTY BOWLING</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. The half yearly meeting of this Association was (nci at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Corporation-street, 01:1 Tuesday evening, Mr. Alderman Meakin presid- 4R. All the affiliated districts were represented. i.,eral suggested alterations in the rules and by- were discussed. It was resolved—" That a 4311 b- co mmittea be appointed to inspect all greeps ei.,ataliated club, and report if they are in a fit I.7ndition tor county engagements." It was then Posed by Mr. Bayley (Levenshulme)—" That toetubers of any club in the Association-be allowed bowl free of charge over any affiliated green on ttenting their club card."—Several representa- h approved of the intent of the resolution, but teld it to be a matter exclusively for decision by I:.ttlers of greens. It was, therefore, dropped.— C. Isherwood (Leigh) proposed-.---" That bowlers not permitted to take part in a county team *he th n they reside upwards of three miles from headquarters of a club to which they belong.' Fairclough (Newton-le-Willows) and Mr. litrtworth (Old Trafford) said this would seriously bettheirdiStrict clubs, inasmuch as their mam- ttliieniP extended up to and beyond a three or four ea radius.—Remarks unfavourable to the re- were also made by Mr. Walmesley (Hazel Teve) and Mr. Lawrence (Withington Midland).— e,resolution was put., and was supported by only 4'4 votMr. Collier (Leigh) moved that the 4 ebe altered rendering it obligatory for semi- -4 and finals to be trundled on Saturdays. After h?ussion the proposition was waived in favour of t „-"ion by Mr. J. W. G.Coombs placing the fixture roliut dates in the hands of the Council, a pro- Its „elcue of same to be issued to all clubs affiliated it 1Y as possible after the next half yearly meet- kziat the beginning of March. A proposition was th4e to return to the old method of each affiliated eit,l .sending a representative to play in the Lance- Ase,ilre v. Yorkshire team, but a majority of those tkitt"eut supported the appointment of a selection tnittee, a 4 acted upon in the early part of the rte7nt year.—Tile annual distribution of prizes toll( place. f I NEW REGULATION POST CARD in FROM 0/0 PER 11000.</p>
        <p>"E " XA z NTINER" heFICF, BE W.SBY-STII HET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND w!DNES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
