        <p>Inner a loss of prestige, yet there is no question that so long as it is seen that we are not able to protect from awful cruelties the people whom we covenanted to protect we must of necessity lose credit both in our own estimation and in the estimation of other civilised people. Lord ROSE- IERY'S reference to the Cyprus Convention formed one of the least convincing parts of his sPeech. That Convention was entered into nine- teen years ago. Under it we secured promises of reform from the Sultan, and we took over the island of Cyprus. We have kept Cyprus during these nineteen years, but the reforms which were Promised by the Sultan have not been carried out. Lord It,SEBERY is of opinion that under these circumstances the treaty is null and void; in other Words that we are justified in retaining Cyprus, but that the Christians of Turkey have no right t:0 call upon us to secure the promised reforms. It 18 a curiously one-sided method of interpreting treaty obligations, but it is very evident from the tecent agitation that, on this matter, too, Lord ItOSEBERY is not in harmony with the dominant feeling in this country.</p>
        <p>The magazines of this month,— British Industry like those of a month or two past, give considerable space to the consideration of the question of British trade. In most of the articles tile British manufacturer is lectured in the well- known style about his methods of doing business and his inability to repel the inroads of the Wily German. German and Zanzibar have been a good deal coupled together lately, in a political eense, but it appears that both in trade and poli- ties, Germans are the rivals of the British in that Interesting island. The British Consul writes that unless the British manufacturer will come clown from his pedestal, and prodace, as his foreign competitors do, an article that will meet the prac- tical requirements of the buyers, it is likely that a trade which might be fairly profitable to him will drop more and more into other hands." Another writer says that in many of our markets the Germans are " making progress at our own expense by their superior energy, by their elastic system of credits, and ley their willingness to oblige particular customers by Meeting their special wants." But the writing is not all on one side. An English gentleman who Ilas represented a North Country firm for seven Years on the Continent, writes that he has suc- ceeded in beating the Germans both inside and ciltside Germany. What is more, he declares that tile number of Englishmen and Scotchrnen who represent British firms on the Continent is in- teasing. At the same time this gentleman admits that the German factories are splendidly equipped an far as laboratories and technical apparatus are !clammed. Most of the German and Fr3nch firms keep qualified analytical chemists, and though the Work done by these chemists is not very highly 4Poken of, it is expected to "tell in the end." In °es respect, it seems, there is, no comparison be- !Wl:fen English factories and those of the Germans. When," says this English traveller, "one com- res the tumble-down shanties that very often 40 duty for workrooms in England with the halls %-there's no other word for it—in Germany, I am aggered at the contrast." In conclusion, he gives tst as his opinion that, after all, the mainstay of 4ritish industry is the superior quality of the .lIIUch-abused British workman. In proof of this, it le mentioned that a Continental firm recently en- gaged a number of Englishmen to work for them, Ittld although the Englishmen who offered them- telves were " a glorious lot of swillers " [yet they could do three times as much work in a vastly kiperior style to the German workmen in the aarae time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>AN INTEMPERATE SAILOR.</head>
        <p>AN INTEMPERATE SAILOR.</p>
        <p>On Saturday before Mr. F. J. NormAn, a sailor named Isaac JohannFsen, was charged with b.ing drunk the previous night in Brunswick-street. A fine of ss. includ- ing costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY OF WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Major Wareing has consented to comply with the wishes contained in the requisition of the members of the Council, to permit himself to be nominated for the position of Mayor of Widnes. ST. EDWARD'S CATROLIC CHURCH.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi is making his annual ap- peal on behalf of his schools. The debt upen the institu- tions amounts to £65 and the pastor earnestly appeals for liberal support.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>A sitting of this Court was held on Tuesday at Run- corn Session House by His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. There were for hearing 64 ordinary, 2 default one adjourned, and 47 judgment summones. There were no cases containing features of public interest.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Mondayl at the Widnes Police Court, before the Mayor (Alderman Sadler) and Mr. J. Mercer, the follow- ing persons were fined for drunkenness :—Annie Whit- taker, no fixed abode; Edward Gxley, 27, Alforde-road; and John Hoelighan, 59, Grenfell-street, Widnes. DRINK.</p>
        <p>At the Petty Sessions, Runcorn, on Monday, before Messrs. F. J. Norman and J. W.Lightburn,James Robin- son, Nelson-st., was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Wellington-st. on the Ist. P.S. Gill proved the case, and he was fined 103. and costs. or 14 days in default' r. rzr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HEAVY PENALTY.</head>
        <p>HEAVY PENALTY.</p>
        <p>On Friday last at the Runcorn Police Court before Messrs. Speakman and Norman, Elizabeth Barker,a well- known character, was charged with indecent conduct in Mersey-ereet, the previous night. This was the pri- soner's Und appearance, and she was fined 40s. and costs, or in default a mnnth's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT THE ALKALI WORKS.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT THE ALKALI WORKS.</p>
        <p>George Molyneux age 20 years and residing at Quarry Bank, Weston, met with a sad accident on Wedne-day at the works of the United Alkali company. Molyneux was engaged running a wagon on a raised plattorm,when he slipped and fell a distance of 29 feet. Medical assist- ance was procured and the injured man taken home. He suffers from a severe shock and injuries to his side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>DRUNK.</head>
        <p>DRUNK.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Speakman and Mr. Taylor on Wednesday morning at the Runcorn Police Court,James Wilson,bar- her, Bridge-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly ths previous night in Egerton-street. P.C. Lakin proved the case and the prisoner was fined 51. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ELLSSMBRE-STRBET SUNDAY SCROOLS.</head>
        <p>ELLSSMBRE-STRBET SUNDAY SCROOLS.</p>
        <p>W. C. LeCouteur gave a very interesting musical en- tertainment in tile above schools on Wednesday even- ing. Songs were contributed by Mr. W. Cooke, Miss Heatberington, and several others. Supper was after- wards provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thaaksgiving services in connec- tion with the above Institute was concluded on Sunday, when the preachers were, morn.ng the Riv.H.B. Firth, sfternoon Mr. W. Shawiand evening the Rev.W.Scholes. There were good congregations. The choir rendered special music. On Monday evening a fruit banquet was held, the proceeds of which are to be devoted to the forthcoming nautieal bazaar.</p>
        <p>TILE BAZAAR AT WIDNES. The bazaar in aid of the Trinity Primitive Meth.dist School was re-opened on Friday afternoon, in the pre- sence of a goodly company. The ceremony was preformed by Mr. B. Dutton, who delivered an interesting address. He was thanked for his services, after which the saies were recommenced. A number of articles being left,the proceedings were continued on Saturday evening. The receipts reached £l2O, but it is anticipated that thii amount will be increased when the tickets &amp;c., have all been brought in. ASSAULT ON A WIDNES SERGEANT.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at Widnes Police Court, before the Mayor and Mr. J. Mercer, Mary Curley, of no fired abode, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the pre- vious night, and also with assaulting Sergeant Carter. l'he evidence showed that the prisoner was off Waterloo- road, behaving in a disorderly manner. He apprehended her, when she took hold of his hand and bit his finger. She also struck him once or twice.—The prisoner being an old offender, she was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days for the first offence, and sent to prison for a month for the assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A boatwoman named Beech, wife of the captain of the flat December. belonging to Mr. William Bate, of Ron- corn, had a narrow escape from drowning on Saturday night. The craft,laden with timber, was moored at a wharf in the Bridgewater Canal, and about 11.30 p.m. the woman was walking along the plank connecting the shore with the boat when she fell into the water. Her screams were heard by the canal constable, Henry Moores,lso yards away,and be ran to the spot and seized the woman's hand just she was sinking exhausted. He was unable to lift her ashore,but Police-ennstable Hughes went to his help, and together they rescued her, and carried her into the cabin,where she recovered under the restoratives applied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Tall ARMENIAN QUESTION.</head>
        <p>Tall ARMENIAN QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Gastaidi, preaching at St. Edward's Catholic Church, Runcorn, on Sunday evening, being the Feast of the Maternity of our Lady, made special reference to the Armenian Question. He said they had that evening a resolution to be placed before them which came from the local authority of Runcorn. The follow- ing resolution was unanimously passed :—‘, That this congregation trusts her Majesty's Ministers, realising te the fullest extent the terrible condition in which their fellow-Christians unier Turkish rule are placed, will do everything possible to obtain them full security and protection ; and assures Her Majesty's Ministers that they may rely on its cordial support in whatever steps they may feel it necessary to take for the purpov." The some proposition was adopted at St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>THEFT BY BOYS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Police Court, before the Mayor and Mr. J. Mercer. on Monday morning. George Wright, aged 11, of 19. John-st., and John McStay. aged eight years, of 65.Ja mes-st.,were charged with stealing is 6d.in menov, belonging to Elizabeth Garquin, of 14, Water-st. The boys went to complainant's house on Saturday night about eight o'clock, and asked to be permitted to stay there. The request was granted. Mrs.Garquin retired, leaving a dress skirt in the kitchen, in the pocket of which was a purse containing the sum named. On com- ing down next morning the prisoners had gone, and the purse was on the sofa empty. P.C. Ramsbottem appre- hended them,and Wriqht told him that McStay took the money and gave him 91., which he had spent in pies, pop.and football cards. They pleaded guiltv,and Mr:Stay, owing to his tender years, was discharged, hut Wright, who had been previously convicted, was oriered to re- ceive six strokes with the birch. NEW CHURCH FOR WINTON.</p>
        <p>An important meeting was held on Monday evenme at the Vicarage,Runcoro, to consider the advisability of building a new church for Weston. The Rev. Canon Wood presided, and there were also pre.ent Messrs. T. T. Sill, W: H. Bankes, A. G. Guest, J. Ford, jun., and the Rev. W. H. Stables (hon sec). From the report, it anneared that the secretary had succeeded in rasing £175 during the vear,which included enbanriptiona from Lord Egerten £lO, Mr. W. H.Bankes £so,Messrs. Green. sit. Whitley and Co. £2O, and the Rev. W. El. Stables £2O. Altogether £1,814 was in hand or promised. The committee discussed the matter at great length, and fin- ally resolved to ask for tenders as soon as £l,OOO had been obtained in cash or dependable promises. Towards the £lB4 required to make up this amount the Rev. Canon Wood promised £5O and Mr. James Ford, jun.. £lO. It is expected that the work will be put in hand about the beginning of the new year. ST. PAUL'S WESLRYAN CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, two sermons were preached in the above church by the Rev. Thomas Hind. of Rhyl, before large congregations. On each occasion the choir rendered special music. In the morning they Interpreted the anthem "I was glad," and in the evening "Come unto Him" and "God is gone up." The organist was Mr. W. H. Grine, and the choirmaster Mr. R. Morris. On Mon- day evening a musical service was givr n in the presence of a large congregation. The devotional exercises were led hy the Rev. T. Brackenburr. The choir were as- sisted by an orchestral band. Several choruses were given from the 12'h Mass; Mr. S. Harrison sang " Cal. vary " ; Miss Taylor" I know that my Redeemer liven ,2; Miss Simpkin, "Creation's Tlymn " ; and Mr. Parry "The Holy City." Mr. R. W. Piernnint played the trumpet solos " Cujus animam " and "Star of Bethle- hem"; and Mr. W. Cook. A..R C. 0., performed two organ solos. The programme, which was of a diversi- fied character, was very highly appreciated, the respec- tive artistes acquitting themselves with great ability. One and all they are to be congratulated upon the efficiency with which they fulfilled their duties. Mr. G. Bowcer presided at the organ, and Mr. 13. Crnahy proved an able conductor, and on the proposition of the latter gentleman a heater vote of thanks was accorded to the band. The enßretions. which were in aid of the choir fund, realivd about £2O, a sum in exces of preci- ous efforts.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool on Tuesday an inquest was held on the body of Thomas Watson. aged eighteen, the Precnot football player who was killed hv lightning on Saturday. The evidence showed that the Present team were about to enter the Newsham Park gates to play a match when a great flash of lightning occurred, striking down a dozen of the players and spectators. Watson was instantly killed but all the others recovered after treatment. A verdict of accidental death was returned.</p>
        <p>RANGHS.—See the 'Herald Trnproved Lifting Fire Ranges.—Sole Agent, I AR;AND, Bridge-street.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>rrf-iE EXAMINER SATUR Y. OCTOBER 17 1896.</head>
        <p>rrf-iE EXAMINER SATUR Y. OCTOBER 17 1896.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held on Tuesday in the Board room, Dutton Workhouse. There were present Mr. J. Knowles (chairman), the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Rev. J. Simpson, Messrs. S. Yarwood, A. R. Norman, the members ot the Rural District Council, and Mr. G. Ashton, acting as clerk in the absence of his father, Mr. J. Ashton.</p>
        <p>AUDITOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. F. .Tordisou, the auditor, reported that he had audited the accounts of the Board and found them correct.</p>
        <p>CATHOLIC INMATES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi proposed that two young girls Catholic inmates of the House, be sent to St. Asaph School, at the same rate ag the children were maintained in the House.—Mr. S. Y4rwood seconded.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward moved the recommendation of the Finance Committee that accounts amounting to £1,322 16. 9d. be paid. The accounts were principally tor the quarter.—Mr. Brandreth seconded the motion. Mr. Yea wood said that last half-year Mr. Both had the contract for conveying the boxes from the clerk's office to the Work- house, and he found on the 21st July an item of ss. 6d. Mr. Booth did not on that day, nor on the 18;h August, Ist September and the 15th September, specially convey the boxes to the Workhouse in accordance with the terms of his contract.—'The Clerk said he did not under- stand Mr. Yarwood at all. It was the Board day on those days.—Mr. Yarwood : It was Board day. The boxes were conveyed on Mr. Ashton's carriage, one carriage did for both. He thought it was not fair to the contractor.—The Clerk explained that the contract for conveying the boxes was 53. 61 and Mr. Ashton paid Mr. Booth 53. also; that was 10s. 6d. for the day's journey. If the boxes and Mr. Ashton were conveyed separately, it would cost more. Mr. Ashton had been conveyed in the same carriage as the boxes since he was appointed clerk.—Mr. Yarwood said his son's tender for conveying the boxes separately was ss.—The Clerk : Mr. Yarwood did not send in a tender this time for the boxes.—Mr. Yarwood said be mentioned the matter because it was not fair and the Board must not accept any more contracts than Mr. Booth's in future and conse- quently he would have the opportunity of raising the prices which had been brought so low.—The Chairman suggested that they leave the matter over until the next tender day. They couldn't deal with it at the present time.—The Rev. J. Simpson supposed that the work had been done for the amount specified in the contract. —The Clerk: Yes.—Mr. Aaron remarked that the dis- cussion was a waste of time.—The Chairman put the motion to the meeting and it was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>VISITING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Visiting Committee recommended that as there was no probability of the elder girls being admitted to the Board School at Dutton the Guardians should advertise for a schoolmistress. —Mr. Woodward said the chairman of the North Western Poor Law Conference most em- phatically expressed an opinion that poor law schools in workhouses were bad, wholly bad,and nothing but bad, and that opinion was endorsed by the whole of the con- ference.—Mr. Brandreth said the Board ought to send the girls to Dutton Board School. The Dutton School Board were obliged to take them in and provide accom- modation for them. It was the feeling throughout the country that children should not be brought up in work- house schools.—Mr. Woodward suggested the sending of the children to the Dutton School.—The Master said there were only three girls to be sent to Dutton Board School, but there were 14 attending the Preqton-oth-Hill Board School, and when they had passed the first standard they would be available for the Dutton School. —Mr. Woodward said he should support any movement for fighting out their question to the bitter end.—The Master, said they wanted either a schoolmistress, or the children to go to the school quickly.— The Clerk said Mr. Linaker, clerk to the Dutton School Board, had most emphatically stated that his Board would not accept these children until they were forced to do so.—Mr. Woodward 'squired whether there was room in the Dutton School Board for the children.—Mr. Talbot (a member of the School Board) said there was no room at all in the school.—Mr. Woodward : Is not a portion of the room taken up by the children living out- side the district.—Mr. Talbot : tall the children lived within three miles,and the Inspector had told Mr. Stokes, the schoolmaster, that they could not send any children away from the school,who were in attendance. If the School Board were compelled to take the workhouse children, then they the School Board) could not help themselves.—Dr. Gastaldi proposed to lay the whole matter before the Education authorities. They must demand free education for their children,and he thought the Dutton School Board were legally bound to make provision.—Mr. Woodward seconded. —Mr. Brandreth suggested that the children be sent to the Board School at Dutton. If they were refused admission, then the Guardians would be relieved of the responsibility.—Mr. Talbot said three or four children might be arranged for,but this was a case of taking the whole lot, and the boys as well. The School Board were determined to fight the matter out until they had orders from the Edu- cation Department to accept the children.— Mr. Brandreth's motion was carried, as also was Dr. G tstaldi's proposition.—The Master said the present staff was not sufficient. They must either appoint an industrial trainer or a schoolmistress.—The recommenda- tion to advertise for a schoolmistress was left in abey- ance until the next meeting.—This committee also recom- mended that Messrs. Timmins of Runcorn, be asked for an estimate for two iron staircases one for the boys' and the other for the girls' dormitories. The committee fur- ther recommended that two water tanks be purchased / one to be placed over the main building, and the other ' over the hospital, with an indicator in the hospital show- ing the amount of water in the tanks. These precautions were taken in consequence of the recent fire at the House. It was resolved that eleven beds be purchased for the male vagrant ward at a cost of 21s. 9d. each, in place of the present wooden, slopes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>LECTURS BY DR. BOWMAN.</p>
        <p>The first of a series of lectures, in connection with the Runcorn Free Library was given on Monday evening, by Dr. Bowman in the Technical Institute Art Room. Mr. J. W. Lightburn, J.P., chairman of the Urban District Council,presuled,an I there were also present the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. raylor J.P., C.C., F. J. Norman J.P., and E. Imison C.C. After a few introductory words from the Chairman</p>
        <p>Dr. Bowman said his subject that night was "three famous books," viz., Newton's " Principia " Grove's "Correlation of Physical F,4rces "and Darwin's "Origin of Species." He would however, confine himself to one of these books, and that was Newton's" Principle." In the course of his remarks, Dr. Bowman said that in the intellectual as well as in the moral world, heit sacrifice was one of the most great laws of progress. Somebody had to toil on patiently, and obscurely for some master mind to gather up the threads into one focus. Great were the services in this case,rendered by the man who gathered them together. This book settled the question of the motion of the heavenly bodies. Astronomy was one of the subjects in which the ancients made little progress, which was due to the fact that they were continually endeavouring to find the explanation of things, instead of discovering what things really were, then finding the explanation when they knew the facts. The lecturer then proceeded to deal with the discoveries of leading men in astronomy, and pointed out how these led to further investigations, which resulted in the completeness of the correct calcu- lations of to-day, with regard to the heavenly bodies. Newton was born in 1642 and died at the age of 85. He was of a studious disposition in his boyhood days, and in time set himself to study the problem of the heavenly bodies. Be discovered the law of gravitation from the well-known incident of the apple falling from the tree to the ground. These and other discoveries were re- corded in the" Principle." In this book the high-water mark of human investigation was reached. Newton had, beyond doubt, left them a legacy,and generations yet to come would turn to that book as a model of research, which could not be excelled. (Applause.) Mr. Norman proposed, and Mr. Imison seconded, and Dr. Gastaldi supported, a vote of thanks to the lecturer, who in responding proposed a similar compliment to the Chairman which was seconded by Mr. Taylor and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council WAS held on Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., presided, and there were also present Messrs. H. Talbot, E. Wallington, T. Lewis, S. Woodward, E. Aaron, J. Knowles, T. Baxter, C.C., .J. Brandreth, J. B. Chadwick and G. Ashton (acting clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LATCHFORD LOCKS</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD LOCKS</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Clerk of the Grappenhall Parish Council, stating that the following resolution had been passed unanimously by his council :--"The opinion of the deputation of the Grappenhall Parish Council is that if it is impossible for the Manchester Ship Canal Company to adequately protect the footway across the Latchford Locks from danger, they consider that their obligation to light them is increased thereby, and think that they ought to afford better protection to the approaches." The Grappenhall Parish Council must again urge your council to take this matter up and put pressure on the Ship Canal Co., otherwise they must appeal to the County Council as they feel, after the loss of life that has already occurred, it would be a failure of duty on their part to remain inactive."— The Clerk : The Act of 1885 says the Ship Cand Com- pany must provide footpath with hand-rails across the locks, free of charge. This they had complied with. But in reference to the lighting of the locks, there was no- thing mentioned in the Act. He did not think they could force the Canal Company to do any more.—Mr. Woodwaed p oposed that the question be put before the County Council.—The Clerk: The Grappenhall Coun- cil objected not to the footpath but to each side of the canal which was unprotected. According to the Act it was only across the canal the Company had to erect hand rails.—Mr. Brandreh thought the Ship Canal Com- pany °light to be made to protect it in a proper manner. —The Clerk: They do protect the footway across the water. It is the unprotected sides the council ob- jected to.—Mr. Woodward and Mr. Brandreth proposed and seconded that the matter be referred back to the Grappenhall Parish Council to take what steps they thought necessary, and this was agreed t 6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>SUTTON WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Sutton Parish Council, re- questing the committee to write to the Local Govern- ment Board with reference to the water supply for Sut- ton, with a view that the water supply may be obtained as soon as possible.—The Clerk explained that the Local Government Board inquiry was held re the water supplY in June. but no report had yet been received from the Local Board Inspector who conducted the inquiry. An order authorising the work to be done, ought to be re- newed in a reasonable time.— The Clerk was instructed to communicate with the Local Government Board on the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>DIPIITHBRIA AT styTTON.</head>
        <p>DIPIITHBRIA AT styTTON.</p>
        <p>The Sutton Parish Council Inquired on what grounds were the houses (kaown as Bonework'd Cottages) al- lowed to be inhabited without a supply of good water for domestic purposes being available within a reasonable distance.—lnspector Farrington said the District Council granted the certificate for occupation of these houses. conditionally,on Mr.Linaker giving an undertaking' that the houses would have a supply from Marsh...ate Farm, until the proposed water scheme was carried out. There had heenfa case of diphtheria at one of these honses.hut be. ,could not say whether the water out of the shallow well at Sutton Dock, which was certified as un- fit for domestic use,had been used fordomestic purposes. The people residing in the houses.had told him that they had not drunk the water from the well —The Clerk was instructed to take steps to have the well closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICERS REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICERS REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the report of Dr. Adams, medical officer,which ctated that in accordance with instructions recetved he visited Frodsham and Fradsham Lordship schools, and found no reason at present to close tke schools, which had only been re-opened on the sth inst. Scarlet fever had occurred in connection with Stretton schools, five houses being invaded. He recommended that the school be closed for one month.fnmigatinn. dis- infection, and lime-washing, to he carried out in the meantime. Two cases of dinhtheria had occured at Alvanley,and he recommended that the schools be closed for three weeks and that the water to the houses he analysed. A complete inspection should also he made of the sanitary arrangements of the house cellars. One fresh case of scarlet fever had occurred at Preston Brook. Two cases of scarlet fever had occurred at put. ton.the children attending Dutton School. One cse of typhoid fever bad occurred at Kingsley. Three eases of diphtheria had occurred at Suttonsone patient had died; a water supply might have been uaed,whieh was open to suspicion. He had visited Norton Cottage,Norton.in re- ference to the alleged nuisance. There was an overflow of liquid sewage from a midden on farm premises ^l7l- - hnd flowing in an open channel opposite to the cottage, and also an outfall of sewage on an open space below Norton Cottage. Each constituted a nuisance, dangerous and injurious to health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ENTERIC FEVER AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>ENTERIC FEVER AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At, Widnes an epidemic of enteric fever broke out last July. the disease causing in that and the fellnwing month alone as many as 16 deaths. ThP attacks num- bered in the came period upwards of 100. The disease sprang up so rapidly that the scarlet fever wards of the hospital were cleared and used few enteric fever patients, and even thus only some half of the Caaec could he ad- mitted. The medical officer of health and his staff did all in their power to stay the spread of the epidemie.and in searching for a cause found that they had to absolve ' alike water and milk from haying helped in its diffusion. The water, indeed, is frnrn deep wells some distance from the town, and is so piped as to leave little chance of in- suction of foul matter into the mains. The sewerage, tr-^, is of recent construction, and does not favour the idea of causation of disease by way of sewer emanations or the like But the water closet system is of very small dimensions in the place. the general method of exere- ment disposal being by faulty and evil-smelling midder p; ivies, of imperfect construction, and such as permit of the escape of their cententa to the surface of yards, even into adjacent yards, causing grave nuisance, especially in summer. Dr. Bruce Low reported to the Local Government Board in the early part of last year that the town has long been subject te fever prevalencea,the deaths in the four- teen years to 1894 having n”mbered 196, of which as many as 106 took place in the last five years of that period. In these same five years the attack from fever numbered 587,0 f which total 311 occurred in tha months of August, September. and October. Dr. Low severely rendemned the midden privy system, and ears that the 3.000 existing are situated from 6to 30 feet, from dwell- ings. After describing the local circumstances. he sacs that the air around the dwellings in Widnes la habitu- ally fouled by emanations from collections of human and other filth stared close to the houses tinder very unfavourable conditions, and the tainted atmosphere that is there breathed cannot fail to have an injurious effect upen the inhabitants. The circumstances were such as greatly to favour the diffusion of enteric.. fever. It is to he hoped that the 'Town Council will sea the necessity that exists for a wholesale extension of the water carriage system of excrement disposal, and pro- ceed to carry it out.—British Medical Journal.</p>
        <p>The extensive bobbin works of Messrs. F. Bolden and Sons at dither() were almost completely destroyed by o” Saturday night, the damage being estimated at £7,009 A correspondent states that the fire is evidently the wrrk of an incendiary, rind that a large number ‘‘f Saturday night incendiary fires have occurred during the p.st two or three months at Clitheroe.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY and Eleotro•Plated GOODS.—GARLAND, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>THE FINANCIAL STA CEMENT.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the above Board was held on Monday evening in the Board Room at the Town Hall. Mr. Pritchard presiAed.and there wtn'e also present—the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr.E. Imison, C.C., Mr. C. Timmins, and Mr. G. Moss (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted the financial statement for the twelve months ending September 29h, which showed that the balanc,., in hand at the beginning of the year was £1,089 is. 5d., the grant from the Educati m Department £1,184 63., grant from Science and Art De- partment £42 14s. 61, fee grant £514 103., under the Technical Inktruction Act, 1889. £9l 63. 4d., school fees £42 173, 6J., sales of books and other articles £36 103. 81, from the rating authorities, being equiva- lent to a rate of sd. in the pound,£l,2so other receipts £24 123. 6d.,total £4,305 184. 11d. The expenditure had beeFi :—Administration £4lO 143. 61, maintenance £2,772 16s. 11d., contribution to industrial schsola £2O 15s 6d.under the Technical Instruction Act £l3l 15.4 d., principal repaid and interest £329 13. 6 1., [maintenance of blind and deaf at schnol £7l 93. 2d., balance in hand £570 9s. 21 total £4,305 183. 11d. The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the accounts said the amount received from the authorities had been similiar to the sum of the previous year, namely at the rate of SJ. in the L. That would not have met all the liabilities had they not in hand a balance of £1,089 :at the commencement of the year.—Mr. Imison seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>AN INCREASED PRECEPT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved that a preceipt be signed for £1,200. This was an increase of £3OO over the corres- ponding period last year, necessitated in consequence:of their balance being reduced as shown by the financial statement, and owing to the increase in the expense in maintaining their s3hools. They were aware that they had taken over the Wesleyan Schools, one of which was not the property of the Board. For this denominational property they had to pay annually £l5O, for rent, light- ing, heating • and caretaker. Also in making no their estimates they anticipated a source of income of £l2O a year from the fees of the Wesleyan Schools,but no sooner had the authority taken aver those institutions than an agitation was set on foot by the parents ot the children attending them for their abolition. This was largely supported by the racepayerstand they were placed in the position that they ought ta submit to their abolition. Consequently they were deprived of that source of income. They had therefore no alternative, but to levy this amount upon the general body of rate- payers. Those two items largely explained the increased amount. The additional £4OO, they believed, would cover any increased expenditure for the next twelve months, so that there would be no augmentation in the precept to be levied six months hence.—Mr. Timmins seconded the proposition. He thought it was the best pclicy to adopt, and he could not see any other position for them to take.—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi expressed sor- row that such a precept was to go before the ratepayers, sounding as it did £1,200 for the maintenance of the efficiency of their schools. They could net, however, prevent it. They were aware that all their schools did well, but considering the depression in trade in Runcorn it seemed extravagant expenditure.—Mr. Imison was sure every member of the Board shared Dr. Gastaldi's sorrow, and also the Overseers joined him in the expres- sion. No one desired to lay heay. burdens on the rate- payers, especially when times were so bad. The increase was consequent on the depression in commercial circles. They could not have reduced estimates, and empty houses whilst the standing charges remained prac- tically fixed without further charges being made upon those who ha.' to bear them. Some of the largest works had had hundreds of pounds taken off their assessments, and every property owner knew only too well that some hundreds of houses remained untenanted. So long as that existed, the natural conse- quence was that the rates would not come down to the level they so much desired. But it could not be urged by that Board that their rates were less than those in larger areas. He recently saw a statement in 20 School Board districts, and was delighted to find that in all of them Runcorn was considerably the lowest. That Board, fromlthe commencement,had been an economical one and had been a good example to some Boards of far greater pretentions. The members at no period had had a dis- position to spend money unnecessarily, or use the money of the ratepayers to compete unfairly with other schools that were established and existed in the district. He thought the Chairman might take some credit for this, for he was an economist and had advotated fair play t') denominational schools. This was nut only fair to volunte., y schocls, but to the ratepayers, for they saw that the notions some people might have indulged in, that denominational schools were main- tained for the benefit of managers, were a delusion. Directly they took over the Wesleyan schools they found that instead of being a source of profit they were one of expenditure. It seemed to him to be a sound policy,independent of any leanings he had for de- nominational schools, not to oppose them, for it must be obvious from an examination of their accounts that the transfer of the schools and the increase of the children they had to teach were followed by an additional ex- penditure. That proved that denominational schools were a source of saving to the ratepayers,and they could only be glad that they had not more transfers during the existence of the Board. He looked forward to • better times, and if so their burdens would be lighter. It was their duty to make them as small as possible, so as to encourage people to come among them and assist them in bearing their burdens. The motion was adopted. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above Council was held on Tuesday evening in the Schoolroom. Mr. J. Stokes presided, and there were also present Messrs. Ackerly, Randall,Looker, Pritchard, Povey, Astbury, Barrow, Hughes, Jeaeock, Birch, and W. Woodier (clerk). A letter was read from the Clifton Parish Council re- questing them to provide fences for the pits near to Hal- ton Lodge.—The Clerk said he had seen Mr. Linaker Previous to receiving the letter who said the job would be put in hand at once, but the work had not yet been done. The Clerk was instructed to write the agent re- minding him that the pit was yet unprotected. A letter was read drawing the attention of the Coun- cil to the very bad state of the road near Ivy cot- tage. This road was never adopted by the late Dares- bury Highway Board, or yet by the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council, and no one therefore repaired it; also the foothpath leading to the Grammar School. It was ankle-deep in mud and was certainly detrimental to the school children. The writer also drew attention to the pit near Halton Lodge which was quite unprotected from the road.—After a lengthy discussion, it was de- cided that the Clerk write to the District Council re- questing them to take over the road. The Clerk was also instructed to write to Mr. Evans, the agent, in re- ference to having the footpath cindered.</p>
        <p>TRINITY WESLEYAN CHURCH BAZAAR.—III order to acquire the sum of £l5O, for the renovation and decorat- ing of the above church and schools the Wesleyans of Halton decided to open a two days bazaar. Mrs. C. E. Parker, of Penketh, Warrington, opened the first day's proceedings on Wednesday. The Rev. T.Brackenbury, in introducing Mrs. Parker, said the Wesleyan Church in Halton was a credit to the neighbourhood. He highly complimented the friends who were connected with the good work, on the liberalty and ingenuity and labour they had bestowed upon the bazaar.—Mrs. Parker, who was received with loud applause, said it was fashionable now-a-days to disavow a belief in bazaars. How- ever, she did believe in bazaars, because they afforded an opportunity fer people who had not money, but who had clever hands and willing hearts to give their time, their labour and their self saclifile for the good cause, and she often thought that in God's sight this was of far greater value than a few sovereigns, that had cost no sacrifice whatever. In conclusion she expr-ssed the hope that they would be successful in their efforts. (Applause.)—On the propo- sition of Mr. J. Lea, seconded by Mr. J. H. Gamon and supported by the Rev. R. J. Clifton a vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Parker. Business was then proceeded with. The following were the stall holders : Fancy stall, (1) Mrs. Clifton, Mrs. Boys, Miss Lea, and Miss Rowe. —(2) Mrs. Looker, Mrs. M'Cullock. Miss Gamon, Miss Howard. Dairy stall : Mesdames Baguley, N. Nixon Clarke and R. Sixon.and the Misses Astbury and Clarke. Reireshment stall: Mesdames Houghton, Rowe, Brad- bury, Sutton, Rowlinson, and Miss Looker. Hardware stall : Messrs. S. Barrow, Woodier, Rowlinson. Tomlin- son, and Hughes. Beehive: Queen be, Miss K. Baguley; busy bees, Misses A. Saunders, M Newport, C. Looker, B. Tomlinson, and M. Barrow, There was a cloak room in charge of Mr. A. Rowe, a r,hotograph exhibition by Messrs. W. H. Bradbury and J. B. Houghton and Misses Baguley and S. Astbury ; and Mr. W. Aathury displayed the treasures of the Art Gallery. The" Post office" officials were Misses A. Barrow, B. Gregory, L. Jones and E. Goulden, whilst Miss Boys had control of the fish pond. Mr. C. Higginson was in charge of the rifle range and Aunt Sally. The receipts on Wednesday real- ised £l5B lls.—The bazaar was rt-opened on Thursday, when there was a large attendance. Mr. Simpson, of Runcorn, in opening the bazaar, said it was about 58 years ago, since he paid his first visit to Halton. In the I early days many from Runcorn nsed to come to the Sun- day school anniversaries, and on othor occasions. He believed he wag the only living representative of those who visited the village so long ago. He was surprised, considering the amount they obtained by the first day's proceedings, to find so much done in connection with the I various churches in the circuit. In conclusion he hoped success would attend their efforts. Mr. Looker proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Simpson. Mr. W. Rowe seconded. The motion was carried, and the sales were again commenced.</p>
        <p>WI3LEYS Timm, Sankey's Sonls, Bibles, Prayer Ryiks. and ...tationery in great Variety at Jonx c. OnEsnes, 34, Bridge-st. &amp; 23, Butterreorket-st , Warring ton.— Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The ordinary meeting of this COUT4CiI was held on Friday evening, in the church schoolroom, when there were present :—The Rev.D.S. Bowen,rdessrs. A. G.Guest, R. R. Clarke, L. Rosenberg, T. Grounds, F. W. Clark, J. Tudor,J. Ford, sen.. D. Somerville, J. Ball, Basnett, and J. Collier (clerk). The Clerk apologised for the absence of the chairman, who was away from the town, and on the motion of Mr.Rosenberg,seconded by Mr.R.R.Clarke, it was unanimously decided that Mr. Guest preside over the proceedings. The Clerk read a letter frs.m Mr. Rowland. clerk of the Clifton Parish Council, saying that he was instructed to draw their attention to the unfenced state of a deep pit adjoining the footpath from Clifton to Weston, near the slaughter-house,and ask them to take steps for the safety of thelpublic. He (Mr. Collier) mentioned the matter to Mr. Sill before he went away. The matter had been ex- plained to Mr. Evans, the agent of Major Orred, who of late had had his time taken up very much,and if a letter was sent to him it would remind him, and doubtless, at tention would be given to it.—Mr. Rosenberg moved that the letter be sent to Mr. Evans, with a request that he give the sub ect attention .—Mr. Ball seconded the motion which was carried.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that he had written to Mr. Ashton for a reply as to what had been done by the Rural District Council on the subject of ashpita. He had received as a reply a copy of the Local Government Board letter to him. He understood that if the District Council moved in the matter two men would have to be employed, and a contract would have to be entered into for the empty- in! of the ashpits in Westoa and Weston Point. In Appleton it cost the ratepayers £l2O a year. He had( however, been informed that ~there would be Loca Government Board bylaws sent out neat month. Mr. Somerville, in accordance with notice of motion, proposed "that the clerk of this Council apply to the Runcorn Rural District '7ouncil, for the appgintment of this Council as a larochial committee." In doing so he said it had been suggested that he brought the matter forward in the interests of someone, or at the instigation of others. He did nothing of the sort. He consulted no member of the Council before sending the notice of his motion to the elerk,and he doubted very much from the feeling of the meinhers whether he would find a se- conder. Be referred to t.e section of the Act showing that certain powers might be delegated to the Parish Council as a parochial committee, under the Public Health Act. The question was optional, as shown by the word "may " and not 11 shall," and they should lay their wishes before the District Council. There were at present several parochial committees under the Rural District Council, includinc, one at Halton. He could not und, rstand why they had not one at Weston, unless in- fl,tences had been brought to bear .4hich had not been in other townships. Their township was one of the most wealthc in the Council's district, for they had works heavily rated, the Weaver Navigation, and other places, —Mr. Tudor sec nded and Mr. Ball sunported.—After considerable discussion the voting was taken, with the following result :—For, Messrs. Somerville, Tudor, and Ball (three); agains Messrs. It. R. Clarke, T. Grounds, Barlett. Rosenberg, Ford, and Clark (six). The Rev. D. S. Bowen and the Chairman did not vote. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>BABIES OF FIRE, BA.BIES BURNING UP, with itch- ing and burning eczemas, and ether torturing, disfigur- ing skin and scalp diseases. None but mothers realise how these little ones suffer when their tender skins are literally on fire. To know that a warm bath with Con- CURL SOAP and a single application of CurrcoßA (oint- ment), the great skin cure, will in the majority of cases afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy cure, and not to use them without a moment's delay, is to fail in our duty. Think of the years of suffering entailed by such neglect, years not only ot physical but of mental suffering by reason of personal disfigurement, added to bodily torture. Cures made in infancy and childhood are speedy, permanent, and economical.</p>
        <p>Lord Charles Beresford was on Tuesday evening chosen as the Conservative candidate for the Eastern division of Bradford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FiINERA.L OF THE LATE MAJOR PICKMERF, J.P.</head>
        <p>FiINERA.L OF THE LATE MAJOR PICKMERF, J.P.</p>
        <p>With marked manifestations of regret, the remains of the late Major Pickmere, J.P., of Cedar Lawn, Thelwall, were laid to rest in the Warrington Cemetery, on Tuesday afternoon. The funeral was attended by gentlemen of all shades of political and religious opinions, and along the route of the pro- cession Nlinds were to be seen lowered and shutters up. This was especially noticeable in Bridge-street where the deceased had carried on business for many years. Spectators lined the principal thor- oughfares through which the procession passed,and good order was kept by the Chief Constable and his efficient staff, who are to be congratulated on the success of their eff or ts. At about a quarter-past two the cortege started from Cedar Lawn,the deceased's late residence. S2veral fine wreaths—a few of the many sent in—were to be seen on the coffin through the glass sides of the hearse. The coffin was of fumigated oak, heavily moulded and brass mounted. At the head was a brass shield containing the inscription : "John Richard Pickinere, of Thel- wall, b.rn September Bth, 1824, died October 9th, 1896." Another inscripti A] on a brass plate at the foot read as follows : " Honorary Freeman of the Borough of Warrington, fide rt amore." Following the hearse the procei3sion was in the following order :—Mrs. Pickmere's private carriage containing hers-,lf and Mr. E. R. Pickmere (her eldest son) and Mr. T. W. Pickmere (youngs4t son). Then came a carriage containing Mrs. E. R. Pickmere (daughter-in-law), Mrs. T. W. Pickmere (daughter-in-law), Master F. Pickmere (grandson), and Miss Edith Pickmere (granddaughter). No. 3 carriage contained Mr. E. T. Pickmere (brother), Mrs. E. 'l'. Pickmere,Master John R.Pickmere (nephew), and Miss Pickmere (sister) ; No. 4, Mrs. Ellis (sister), Mr. J. G. Jones (brother-in-law), Mrs. J. G. Jones ksister), Miss Hilda Ellis (neice) ; No. 5, the Rev. Jenkyn Jones (nephew), Mr. and Mrs. Seddon; No. 6, Mr. Elliott, Mr. Harvey Elliot, Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Tinsley ; No. 7, Mr. Sy Ivanus Chandley, J.P.; No. 8, the servants at Cedar Lawn.</p>
        <p>Oa reaching the large space near the Wesleyan Chapel Latchford, the cortege was joined by members of the Corporation, officials, and local magistrates, who had proceeded there in conveyances from the Town Hall at the invitation of the Mayor (Alderman Fairclough). Those present included the Mayor; the Deputy Mayor (Alderman Greenall), and the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) ; Aldermen Harrison, Francomh, Wright, Hutchinson, and Sutton; Councillors Baird, Bennett, Dr. Cannell, Edison, Furness, Garner, Heaton, Lee, Longton, F. W. Monks, Roberts, Rogers, Shaw, Smet- hurst, Wilkinson, Davies. Messrs. Elba Armitage, T. Birchall, John Geddes, E. J. Guest, R. Garnett, S. Wright, Dr. Smith, John Rylands, W. H. Bleckly (magistrates),Mr. W. J. Forshaw (president of the War- rington Blue Coat Friendly Society), Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr. George F. F. White (deputy magistrates' clerk), Mr. James Deas (manager of the depots and water engineer), Mr. Luke Talbot (chief constable), Mr. Robert Young (cemetery registrar), Mr. Fred Taylor (secretary to the gas depart- ment). Headed by members of the police force,who were succeeded by members of the Fire Brigade, under the direction of the superintendent, Mr. Turner, the enlarged procession started off for the Cemetery. At the entrance to the Old Warps the pr,cession was joined by the following representatives of the Warringten Amateur Dramatic Society—Mr. J. T. Picton, Councillor Woods and Mr. Dennis Davies. Proceeding to Bridge Foot, the procession was joined by represe-tatives of several institutions, and members of the Volunteer corps of which the deceased had formerly been Major. The Vol- unteers were under the direction of the commanding officer, Colonel Ridgway, who had with him as officers Captain Edelsten, Captain Chorley, Captain J. D. Fair- dough and Lieutenants B. Fairclough and E. L Fletcher; the Warrington Licensed Victuallers' Association was represented by Messrs. John Sampson, Peers Brimelow, Peter Lake, Thomas Walsh, John W. Kershaw, John Monks, John Williamson, James Lea, Peter Chrimes, James Standish, John McClinton and Nathaniel Hawkins; the Conservative Association, by Messrs. William Holbrook (registration agent), George Jolley, W. H. Sinnett, George Armstrong. Ward, George Watkins (manager of the Conservative Club), and Joseph Part- ington ; the boys of the Blue Coat School, also joined in the procession, under the charge of their. headmaster, Mr. Rose. Messrs. J. E. Davies, T. Tunstall arid W. Proud, represented the Warrington Savings Bank ; Mr. George Dakin and Dr. Langdale, the Warrington Infirm- ary. The Volunteer Band, under the conductorship of Mr. T. Armstrong, then commenced to play the Dead March in " Saul," and the now completed procession wended its way up Bridge-street and down Buttermar- ket-stteet and Church-street to the Parish Church where a funeral service was held.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately, en accident occurred during the pro- cession. Mr. and Mrs. W. Tinsley, accompanied by Mr. Elliott, of Nantwich, and his son, were being driven slowly in a carriage down Butterrnarket-street and when opposite the shop of Mr. Hill, druggist, the animal took fright and bolted off down the street. Great excitement prevailed for a time, and as the street was crowded, it was feared there would be loEs of life. 'Phis was averted, as Mr. Wilding pluckily seized the horse and brought it to a standstill. The oc:.-..up-ints of the carriage,who were very much alarmed, especially Mrs. Tinsley, got out at once.</p>
        <p>Shortly before three o'clock a congregation began to assemble in the Parish Church. The time announced for the service was 3.15, but it was long after this time before the funeral party arrived. Amongst those who attended the service were the Revs. John Yonge, W. H. Drummond, F. Slater, H. V.Pigot, and Richard Greenall. Messrs. F. Monks. J.P., William Long, J.P., William Owen, J.P., William Houghton, J.P.,James Tinsley,J.P., S. Chandley, J.P., John Fairclough, J.P., Drs. White, I Fox, Sherratt, Bowden, Captain Buckton, and Messrs. Robert Davies, W. B. Roberts. R. G. Henshall, Thomas Morris, C. Jackson Holmes, John Harrison, T. Birtles, A. Chandley, R. A.Naylor,W. D. Jeans,William Richard- sou, S. flunc,J.L.Tunstall and J. Houghton ; the church wardens ofSt. Elphin's (Messrs. R. T. Fairclough and A. Woods), and the eidesmen (Messrs. John Dewhurst, W. Gill, and J. Craik) assisted in finding accommodation for the cengregation. Daring the time occupied in waiting for the arrival of the cortege the organist (Mr. W. S. Woods), played several selections on the organ. On the procession arriving the coffin,which was borne on the shoulders of several men, was met at the church gates by the officiating clergy.namely,the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis), the Rev. Jenkyn Jones (nephew of the deceased), the Rev. E. J. Wilcocks, Rev, T. P Williamson, and the Rev. A. A. Markham, and the sure pliced choir. The coffin was placed in the centre aisle near the pulpit,and as those in the procession walked to their seats in the church the organist (Mr. W. S. Woods) rendered the air " 0 rest in the Lord." The service commenced with the singing of the hymn commencing "Abide with me,fast falls the eventide." The lesson was read by the Rev. T. I'. Williamson, and the 90th Psalm was sung. As the congregation left the church the Nuno Dimittis was sung to Barnby's chant. The procession was re-formed and pabceeded to the cemetery where the concluding portion of the service was read. A body of police was drawn up at the en- trance to the cemetery gates, and the Volunteer Band again played the" Dead March "as the mourners and the publio walked in procession to the graveside. The sides of the grave were lined with ivy and moss, inter- spersed with chysanthernums, scarlet geraniums, and arum lilies, and this had been carried out under the di- rection of Mr. Ralph Johnson, who was in charge of the funeral arrangements. There was a large attendance at the cemetery and the service at the grave side was con- ducted by the Rev. T. P. Williamson and the Rev. Jen- kyn Jones. Letters of regret for inability to attend the funeral had been received from Messrs. Edward Milner, John White, J. E. Worsley and Dr. Gornall. The wreaths sent were very numerous, and were conveyed in a 'bus. The procession was nearly a quarter of a mile in length and there were no fewer than forty-three conveyances. The following is a list of the ladies and gentlemen who had forwarded wreaths :—Mrs. Pickmere (widow), Mr. and Mrs. John Crosfield, Mr. and Mrs. Hankinson, Mr. and Mrs. R. T. Fairclough and family, Mrs. Grundy (West Leigh, Lymm), Mrs. Fletcher (Whitley), Mr. and Mrs W. Tinsley, Mrs. W. H. Hesketh (Southport), Miss Annie Brassey (Bowdon), Mrs. Werlitz (Snuttgart. Ger- many), "Sisters Fannie, Eliza and Adela," the Rev.T.P. Williamson, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Bleckly, Mr. and Mrs. T. Connor, the Warrington Borough Police Mrs. Anderson (Rainhill), Mr. and Mrs. Herbert drassey (Chester), Mr. and Mrs. J. Platt (Llandudno). Mrs. Peter Rvlands, Miss Rylands, the Misses Tinsley 'Penketh), Mr. and Mrs. John Geddes, Mrs. S. Reynolds (Whatcroft Hall), the servants at Cedar Lawn, the Matron of the Warriligton Infirmary, Mr. and Mrs. Armitage, Mr. and Mrs. T. G. R. lands, Miss Rylands, Mr. H. Divi-s (Southport), the Rev. Wilfrid Stanton, Mrs. Stanton, the servants at Warrington, the house surgeons of the Warrington Infirmary, Mrs. Litton and family, Mr. and Mrs. J. Hepherd, the etnplo es. Bridge. street, Mr. and Mrs. Percival Pearste, " Edith and Ralph "(granddaughter and grandson), Miss Hunt, Mr. and Mrs. Ray,Mrs. P. Robinson and family, Southport; the sons and daughters-in-law. Alderman and Mrs. Edward Greenall, Mr. and Mrs. I. H. L. Jones- Parry, Alderman and Mrs. Harrison, Mr. and Mrs. Edward Pickmere, the servants of New• holme. Miss Astle (Nantwich), the emploNei of Messrs. Pickmere and Son (Nantwich), Mr. and Mrs. Elliott (Nantwich), Mr.a.nd Mrs. J.E. Davies,the Colonel and officers of the Warrington Volunteers, Dr. and Mrs. Sherratt, Mr. and Mrs. Seddon (Bolton), Dr. and Mrs. Bell (Soutliport),thn Warrington Dramatic Society. On a card attached to the wreith from the Dramatic SoOety were the words :—"ln loving remembrance of their Pre- sident, from his old comrades, the Warrington Dramatic Society"</p>
