      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CASE OF CHILD NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>On Friday at the Warrington Borough Court. before Alderman Wright. Dr. Gornall and Cpuncillor Roberts, Joseph Thorpe, 19, Edward-street, was charged with neglecting two of his children on the sth October and divers other dates.—lnspector McAteer prosecu ed and stated that defendant was a labourer and father of three children. He was charged with neglecting Mary Ann aged nine and Thomas, aged 4. Defendant lived at 19 Edward-street. On the sth October he (the Inspector) went to defendant's Mouse and found the two children in a filthy condition. They had no beds to lie on. The defendant was a drunken man and had been repeatedly warned by witness during the past 18 months. His wife was at present in the Workhouse and the children were systematically neglected. He went away at night leaving them alone.—Mrs. Spensley, a neighbcur corro- borated as to the condition of the children. She had to burn the boy's clothes. Witness had kept the children for a fortnight.—A Mrs. Tarby stated that when the In. spector undressed he girl the vermin dropped from her. —This was defendant's second appearance for neglect, and he was sent to gaol for two months, the children going to the Workhouse. LEADING A CAT AND DOG LIFE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday before Messrs. John Fairclough, J. H. Fleming and J. Geddes at the Warrington Borough Police Court, John Gsrnett, a bricksetters' labourer. was charged with assaulting and beating his wife Rachel Gat nett on the 11th inst.—Mr. Arthur Browne repre- sented defendant.—complainant said that on Sunday morning she remarked to her husband that it was a shame for her to have to get up on such a bleak morn- ing. He threatened what he would do at her if she did not go downstairs. She said she would neither be punched ner let the children be punched as they had been. Defendant then hit her on the head, and tried to put her oat of the room,and choked her with his hands. Complainant had a belt in her hands at the time and she "gave it him about the head."—By Mr. ,Browne - She admitted using the buckle end of the belt. She cc.lnted a separation and had seven children. Two of them worked. She did not think Bs. a week was sufficient allowance.—Mr. Browne said his client ienied hitting his wife on the head, but only pushed her. She had been nagging at him for some time.—The Chairman (Mr. John Fairclomeh) : They seem to be living a cat and dog life.—Mr. Browne: There is no doubt about that. I suggest that defendant leave her the house and furniture and give her Bs. a week.— The Chairman: 81. is not enough with the rent at 4s. a week.—Mr. Browne urged that the past few weeks had been a had time for bricksetters' labourers and that the next few months would be the same.—Mr. Fleming: There is plenty of work now. I can't get men in fact.— The Chairman: It is a great pity you cannot agree. Your wife has evidently got a. temper but you have not done what you ought to do to keep your wife and family. We will give you a trial and adjourn the case for a month and you must pay her 10s, a week and keep away from her altogether.—Defendant was then bound over to appear in one month's time, and the parties left the Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINS-TON BOROUGH TOLICR COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Aldermen Wright (in the chair). Dr.</p>
        <p>Gornall ,4nd Conn&amp;!for *,berts</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—James Hill was granted tarn - prwary authority to sell at the Higher Seven Stars, Bridge-st.. In lieu of James Wilson. A CASE WITHDRAWN.—The cale of child neglect against ;Tames Stockton 8 Twelve House,, was withdrawn on the application of the Impactor as there was an improvement in the home awl the conditio of the children. T3VO BELT icosE CHARACTERS —Alice Healey was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Hill-st. on the 15th October.—P.n. Clarke proved the case.—liatey Jones was charged with assaulting Alice Healey.—Mrs Healey said defendv,t hit her with a clog.—lt was stated in Court that both defstriants were continually fighting, and the bench fined them lOs and costs each or 14 days. ASSAULT CASs.—Bridget CARYL 6 Bel/TO-Van:l, WIR timed 10s. and costs for assaulting Mary Ann Rowlands. 1 Berry's- yard, on the 3rd October.—Sarah Ann Woods, 14 Margaret- street was charged with assaulting Annie Lee, 10 Margaret- street, on the same clate.—D.fendant was tined ss. and costs or 7 days.—Josephillulkck. 283 Win wick-road, was charged with assaulting Annie Jones on the 3rl Ontober.—Mrs. Jones stated that defendant struck her in the eve and knocked her down. 'There hal teen it qu,rrel between their two children. —Bullocks was fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before the_ Mftvor (Ald.rwm Fairclough). Alder-</p>
        <p>man Harrlron ;Me. Mr. Monk,</p>
        <p>_ . A ppl,Tokrlny.—Mr. Browne spoiled, on behalf of the land- lord of the Chip Inn, Rank Quay, for pe,lniseion to build two additional bedrooms and to make some etrncfnral alteration,' for nnrely domestic pnrpnsee. The plane had been submit'ed to the Chief flonetable and the Watch Conaniittee.—The Chief Constable eald he had no objection to make. He had visited the hones. and he thought that the alterations were needed.— The application was granted. WANTED FOR FrOITSEBRMARING. —Thomas RURhtnn WlB by ought no for having broken into a Truse at /lurelem.—The Chief ennetable stated that he had received information fram the Staffordshire n lice to the effect that a man named Rush- ton was wanted for housebreaking at 13nrslem. A description of him was also given. Re instrac• ed Detective-eetgeant Monaghan to investigate the case The detective afterwards saw the man coming out V the Glad-tone Arms and when he was charged with the offence ha re lied "It is all right It is my father's house. I went in for my Own property."— Prisoner was handed nver to the Staffordshire police. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- • MgarLY Daulm. —Sarah Maloney was fined 2s. 6d for being drunk in Ruttermarket-street. on Saturday.—P.C. Marsh said that he looked her lin for her own saietv. DRuxx Awn Iltmonnxatx.--Thomas Mitchell war charged with being drunk and disorderly in Hill-street at 9.30 on SaturdaY night. PC. Bylatt or,ved the case, and clef .ndant was fined 5a and costs.—Margaret Griffin was charged with similar a offence in Warburton-street. on Saturday, the 3rd Inst. PC. Coehett stated that he was on duty in Warburton- .tree", th, day in Question, in company With P.C. T,ewis. They there raw the defendant, who was drunk and making use of filthy language He took her into the house three times but. to nn purpose. P.C. Lewis corroborated. ()Wend- ant waa fined sa. and costs.—Patrick McLoughlan wit&lt; fined 2s. fal.. including c,sta, for haviTur been drunk and disorderly on the 3rd inst.. P.C. EVRIlii proved the ease BRE kr.IT or 'ME PRACF:. --:Tohn William Jackson, James Jackson. Michael FloYd and Thomas Knox were charged with haying on October 3rd committed a breach of the peNce.— P.C. Baxendale said that on the day in question he was on duty at, the corner of Priestley-st,-ee` and Aikin-street. Whilst lookinc, towsrds the Railway Inn he notined a crowd, and on proseeedine towards the sceoe of the disturbance he saw tiTe for defendants fighting. They were all bleeding. He gotSh -Id of oneof them. and would have taken him to the Police Station but he professed to give him the names and addresses of the other defendants, upon which he let him go. It had sin"e been found that all the addresses were incorrect. —Defendants were bound over to keep the peace for six months In their own sureties of £5 each and to pay the (awls VVEDTZESDIY.—Pefors Yfessrs. John Fairclough (chairman), John Geddea. and J. H. Fleming.</p>
        <p>A DRSERTJohn Fitzverald WA.PI charge-4 with being a 'des prter from Vials', South Lancashire Regiment statinned at Perm Th—Lanne-Sergeant Larkin proved the case.and defend- ant was enmmitted to Strangew lys Gaol to await an escort. THuitsPAY.—Before Alderman Sutton, and Mr. J. H.</p>
        <p>Fleminq</p>
        <p>A T,LEnV P VxTr.vstvE Tffrr.vmt.—Catherine Bolton, 109 Bostock-st reet, widAw, was charged with stealing from a staii</p>
        <p>THE PuBLIc ,tun W&amp;RNED against the use of l'oreign cocoas containing alkali, producing highly coloured prepara- tions offered as " Pure cocoa" Cadbury's Cocoa is absolutely pure and unadulterated, and is therefore the best. The .. Lame, de,eribes it es " the standard of highest purity at present attainable.-"--Advt.</p>
        <p>PT` _Ca-;</p>
        <p>in the iMarket Place, at 4.20 the previous afternoon, a ladies' cape, value 4s. 61 , te propertyof Rachael lasker.—Detective Sergeant Monaghan said that at 420 on Wednesday afternoon defendant was seen in the Market near Mrs Tasker's stall. The cape was missed and prisoner was followed by a witness named Fisher and near th?. Infirmary the daughter of prisoner was seen to be wearing the cape. Witness apprehended pri- soner and charged her with the offence. She sail "I don't know whatever made me take it." tie afterwards searched her house and found " all this property, some of which has been identified." —The property referred to was clothing of almost every description and sufficient to stock many a stall comfortably.—Prisoner was r,manded for a week. THEFT BY A GIRL OF TWELVE.—Bertha Grounds, 9, Oliver-street, aged 12. was charged with stealing f om a stall In the Market-place at 11 a.m. on Wednesday, a piece of silk and three pieces of calico value 6d. the property of C. H. Lewis.—D.S. Monaghan said that at the time in question the girl was at Ellis's stall and purchased a small piece of calico. Mr. Ellis then watched her and saw her take a small piece of silk Information was given to witness who in company with Inspector Place followed the girl and found several articles in her basket.—The mot her of defendant said the girl had been brought up honestly and had never stolen anything before.—The magistrates recorded a conviction gainst defendant an discharged her.</p>
        <p>S.EIT [SItDAY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman), Mr. C. B. P. Borron. and Mr. R. Stone. THE DRUNKEN LIST.—For 13.9ing drunk and disorderly,the following were dealt with :—Michael Clarke at Haydocic on the 21st, 2s. 64. and costs; Martin Maguire. at Haydock on the 25th, 2s. fld, and costs • James Moran. at Haydock on the 26th. 2s. 6i. and costs ; Michael Clarke at Haydock on the 26th, 2s. 64. and costs ; Patrick Carney same time and place ss. and costs ; Thomas Thornley on the 3rd October, 2s. 6d. and costs; Isaac Roberts on the 2nd Uctober at Golbarue, 2s. 6d. and costs ;James Phillips on th 6th October at Newton. ss. and costs; and Thomas Keenan on the 3rd Octo- ber at Newton. ss. and costs. OISCENE LANGUAGE.—James Downs, was summoned for having on the 19th Sentemher used obscene languagA in Legh- street, Earlestown —P.C. Burns proved the case and a fine o 6s. and costs was imposed. CIVF.LTY TO A Do.—Peter Sutton, of Burtonwood, was charged with having on the 18th September at Burtonwood illtreated and abused a dog by kicking the same.—lnspector Bilson, R.S.P.O.A. said he appeared to prosecute. tin the date named the defendant was a lodger with Mr. and Mrs. Bothwell. of Broad-lane, Burtonwood. Whilst partaking of his supper the dog came up to him, and he threatened to kill it. The animal was taken out of the kitchen and put into the back yard. The defendant who was drank afterwards went into the Yard and dealt it a terrific b ow with his foot, hurling it fifteen feet —Elizabeth Bothwell. Broad-lane. Bur- tonwood and James Bothwell gave evidence corroborating the inspector's statement. The dog was made lame and suffered great pain. —lnspector Bilson said he had examined the dog and found that one of the legs was dislocated. He thought the dog would always be lame.—Defendant denied that he had his boots on when he kicked the dog .—The Chair- man said the mazistrat•is were convinced that defendant used an unnecessary amount of violence. The defendant was drunk at the time and that was t e only redeeming feature about the ease. (Laughter.) He would be fined ss, and costs. _ _ _ _ • __</p>
        <p>THEFT OF COAL —Mary Rowley. Catherine Janes, Mary Jane Beddoea, Ruth Gibson, and Frances Stainer, all of Gol- borne, were charged with stealing two cwts. of coal value is. the property of Messrs. Richard Evans and Company Limited. Defendants pleaded guilty.—Mr. Stuart, solicitor, prosecuted, and stated that the Company did not want to make a vindictive prosecution of the ease ; it was simply as a warning and to prevent it happening again. The defend- ants lived at Gollyirne and numerous complaints had been made of people trespassing on the land of the Company and stealing coal from the tip. The police had been communi- cated with and P.O. Gough went to the colliery on Sattirday. the 26th of September, and saw the defendants picking coal. He intercepted them as they were coming away. He found they had four sacks and an apron filled with coal weighing altogether two cwt.—P.C. Gough and Mr. James, manager of the colliery, gave evidence, and the magistrates warned the defendants. and fined them Is. and costs each. A TRWIAL CASE —John Pilling a boy, living with his parents at Haydock, was charged with assaulting and beat- ing a little girl named Sarah Ellen Rigby. —Mr. Wilson, of Wigan, prosecuted, and Mr. Stuart, d -tended —The evidence by the complainant showed that she was stopped by the de- fendant and two other boys when rota' ruing on an errand. They pulled at her basket, and the defendant afterwards struck her on the head.—ln cross-examination she admitted that the blow did not hurt her.—The magistrates ordered the defendant to pay the casts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR WEAK LUNGS.</head>
        <p>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR WEAK LUNGS.</p>
        <p>For people with weak lungs, a winter , rarely passes without a bad ceugh, and the utmost care is necessary to prevent some more serious form of lung trouble. It is a great mistake to treat such a cough or such a con- dition of the lungs with an ordinary soothing cough mixture. What is needed is a food medicine, such as Angier's Pctroleum Emulsion. This remedy possesses decided antiseptic power, and has a wonderfully sooth- ing and healing effect upon any inflamed or catarrhal condition of the throat and lungs. It not only cures the most stubborn coucih and heals and strengthens weak lungs, but the whole body is invigorated. It promotes appetite,aids digestion,and greatly improves the weight, strength, and general health. It has been thoroughly tested in the hospitals and by the most eminent lung specialists in the kingdom, and has won their cordial approval and endorsement. Remember it is both food and medicine, and is pleasant to take. Prescribed by the medical profession and sold by all the leading chemists, price 29. 91. and 4s. 6,1. A sample bottle sent free on receipt of 31. to cover postage. Descriptive pamphlet post free. Angier Chemical Co., 32, Snow Hill, London, E.C.</p>
