        <head>MASSED BAND PERFORMANCE IN THE</head>
        <p>MASSED BAND PERFORMANCE IN THE</p>
        <p>PARK.</p>
        <p>CLOSE OF THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>Wei.%</p>
        <p>The series of band performances in the park was brought to a conclusion on Saturday by a performance given by a massed band of nearly 250 performers.lt had been arranged for ten bands to he present.but only nine putlin an appearance, the Buckley-street band being ab- sent. A heavy thunderstorm broke over the town about two o'clock, but,fortunately,the weather was fine during the performance of the bands, which took place in front of the Town Hall and there was a good attendance of the public. Amongst those who listened to the perform- ance were the Mayoress (Mrs. J. Faircleugh) and a num- ber of other ladies, Alderman Wright, Councillors! Dr. Cannel!, Heaton, Bennett, Shaw, Baird, Monks, Mr. F. Menks. J.P., the Borough Treasurer (Mr. Fairhurst),the Chief Constable(Mr. L. Talbot),Mr. C.Madeley(librarian) Dr. Gornall (medical officer of health),Mr. Place (market inspector), Dr. Bowden, and Messrs. Kiernan, John Harrison. and Joseph Taylor (secretary to the Bands Committee). Mr. Thomas Armstrong efficiently con- ducted, and the programme was as follows:—QuicksteP. "King Lear" (Rimmer); overture, "Crown of Victory" (Ripley); chorus. " Thus rolling serges rise" (Handel) ; grand march, "The Challmag." (Jones); valse, " Wood- land Flowers" (Wadson); " God save the Queen." The programme, it should be stated, was opened by the band playing the Dead March in " Saul," as a mark of respect to the late Major Pickmere. rhe items of music were very well rendered and did credit to the performers. Councillor Dr. Cannel! (as deputy ,chairman of the Town 11,14 Park and Baths Committee),who spoke from the top of the steps in front of the Town Hall, asked Mr. Monks to say a sew words before the audience separ- ated.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks IP., said he had been asked to propose a vote of thanks to the associated bands for their per- formances that d.y and during the summer months. He had been informed by the Secretary that that was the Seth performance of the season, and he thought it was very creditable to all particularly to Mr. Armstrong,who, he was sure, had been put to considerable trouble in bringing the bands to such a state of perfection. On be- half of his fellow-townsmen he begged to thank the members of the bands most heartily. The association was one with which some of them had been connected from its inception. Mr. Armstrong was one of them, and he knew the difficulties which had to be overcome. They were particularly grateful to him for the interest he had taken in it, and he wished to express that feeline on behalf of himself and his fellow townsmen. He hoped that the Corporation might see their way. to still further render monetary assistance to the bands, and they might yet hope to hear such grand performances in the Park as were to be sometimes heard on the Continent. He was sure that Mr. Armstrong had about him an amount of musical ability that would add very much to the pleasure of the townspeople who listened to them in the Park. (Applause.) Councillor Heaton seconded the motion, and in doing 80 said he regretted the absende of the Mayor and the Chairman of the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee (Alderman Bolton). He coupled with the vote the name of Mr. Joseph Taylor, who had worked hard now for several years as secretary to the Bands Committee. (Hear, hear, and applause.) • The motion was carried, and three cheers were given for Mr. Armstrong and the secretary, after which the National Anthem was played and the proceedings ter- minated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the week ended Fcliday, October 9, 116 sea- going vessels, with an aggregate net registered tonnage of 36,944 tons, entered and cleared from the port of Manchester, as compared with 130 vessels of 54,160 tons in the previous week, the falling off being largely due to the stormy weather. Twenty-eight vessels of 21,005 tons arrived from or sailed for foreign ports, and 88 ves- sels of 15,849 tons, entered or cleared coastwise. Four full cargoes of timber arrived from British North America, general cargoes from New York, Stettin, Ham- burg, Antwerp, Ghent,Rotterdam and Boulogne, esparto grass. and fruit from Spain, logwood from the West Indies, and over 700 tons of bone ash, hornpiths, &amp;c., from Santa Elena. Three steamers and a barque cleared outwards for the Brazils, five steamers for the Continent, two for Christiania, one for Stettin, one for Iceland, one for Egypt, Sc‘., and one for the Persian Gulf.</p>
        <p>To THE DEAF.—A. Gentleman who cured himself of Deaf ness and Noises in th- Head after fourtesn years' suffering, will gladly send full particulars of the remedy post free.— Address F CLIFTON, Amberley House, Strand, London, W.C,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>FOOTB ALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTB ALL.</p>
        <p>HO. G: BUSSEY &amp; CO., are makers of High Class appli- ances for Football, Golf, Hockey, Lacrosse, and all Sports and Games, also for Gymnasia and Physical Exercises. Illustrated Catalogue may be obaLined from local Deilers or the Sports Manufactory, London, S.E. Trade Mark G.G.B.</p>
        <p>RUGdY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. ROCEIDALE ST. CLEMENTS.</p>
        <p>This match was played on the Wilderspool enclosure en Saturday before aboat 4,000 spectators. The day was very dull and damp,but the rain held off during the match. As the fixture was outside the League,the weak team that Warrington had on did not matter so much as otherwise, but it was rather surprising that the homesters should eventually win. The combinations placed on the field were :—Rochdale: Rigg, full back ; Costello, Butterworth, Midgeley, and Kershaw, three- quarters; Halkyard and Garnett.half backs ; W.Waugh, J. Waugh, Tweedale, Farmer, Ashworth, Ridehaugh, Parkinson,' and Bridgens, forwards. Warrington: Hee- som, full back ; Foster, Scott, Cross, and Radford,three- quarters ; Bate an Houghton. half backs; Taylor, Thornton, Robey, Meacock, Nevins, Swift,Burrows, and Barratt, forwards.</p>
        <p>W. Waugh kicked off for Rochdale from the pavilion end, no return being made. A scrimmage ensued from which Rochdale got possession, but Bate prevented Gar- nett getting in his kick. The game then crossed the field, and Bate secured, making a good run and then transferring to Scott. who missed the pass and the ball rolled into touch. From the line out Midgeley caught the oval and made off down the field. He attempted to kick in touch but Heesom put in a return which re- gained all lost ground. A round of passing Ye oWc kaerd- rington backs was nullified by Kershaw, who le on. Another scrimmage put the ball into the hands of Bate who handed it to Cross, and the latter kicked across the field to Redford. • clear course was left for the line, but Radford failed to secure the ball quick enough. Still, the homesters were pressing and Houghton transferred to Bate, the latter to. Radford who kicked a little too hard and the ball went dead. Cross was next prominent with a good run which Scott failed to supplement. Rochdale then got into Warrington territory and a good dribble was stopped at the right time by Radford. Warrington were then pen- alised and Butterworth took the kick, sending the ball, however, dead in the corner. Immediately the leather was in play again Taylor gained ground by a strong kick. From a scrimmage Garnett got possession and attempted similar tactics ; but Heesom prevented any advantage accruing to the visitors by another kick into touch. A round of passing by Bate. Houghton, Scott and Radford merely transferred play to the other side of the field where a line out was made. Loose play enabled a kick to be put in which reached Rigg and the latter was applauded for a neat return after eluding Foster. Plan again became loose and the Warrington forwards were observed to be exchanging until Barrett received a forward pass and another scrimmage followed. A pass was then taken by Radford who put the ball in touch. Houghton again get the ball out and passed to Foster'; the latter ran very trickily but was held when half-way across the field. Cross and Rigg made exchange kicks from which there was another scrimmage. Halkyard gained possession and kicked into Warringtnn quarters. Heeenm stopped the ball and running along the field with it he kicked in tench placing Warrington again on the offensive. Cross forced another minor and immediately the ball emerged again. Houghton kicked dead. The kick out was charged down by Rnhey. Houghton took ' the hall. He ran for the line then passed to Bate who was tackled hut managed to pot the ball into Taylor's hande.and the latter scored. Taylor also took the kick end made a very good attempt at goal. On re-starting Warrington were soon in the vieitors' quarters and But- terworth gave relief by eluding his pursuers and kicking across the field. Bate attempted to get away by a sham pass lent was greesed. The acrimmage which followed enabled IC.rqt•aw to kick eee bnt Ffeesomhs return found its usual piece in touch. Line-outs and scrimmages were now frequent and Halkverd managed to send a pass to Midg,ley, hut before he could move Cross tackled him. Another kick came to Heeeem who returned with such effect that Rigg, the home cnstodian, was brought down on his own line. Some serimmaging took piece at the corner where Garnett gave relief. This was only tempo fay as the home forwards were soon on the Rochdale line again, but a wild pass out of Radford's reach prevented any score. Play then raged in front of the visitors' goal and Robey almost got over. A scrim- mage right under the posts put Bate in peaseeaion. " Smack " then showed that he had his wits about him, for he ragged back to Scott who dropped a neat goal. Another eieurt by St. Clements gave them a temporary hold and Riga managed to put Warrington off-side by touching Barrett. Cross kicked once more and was fol- hewing up well when Kershaw was forced to kick dead. Roth Rigg and Cross made marks, Cross managing to gain a little ground. Honghton and Scott exchanged but Foster failing, the hall went into touch. Half-time wee then sounded with Warrington leading by a dropped goal and a try to nil. Taylor re-start-ed and from the first scrimmage Roch- dale were penalised, the Warrineton kick finding touch. Scott Mete the hell from a line out but Halkvard saved. Rigg made another useful return and Kershaw forced the hemestera to concede a minor. The Seines now put nri a little pressure and from a pass from Garnett, Mideely was just becoming dangerous when Bate jerked hie feet from under him. This produeed another scrim- nsaem, Bate seeuring, and making a lengthy run, passed to Hrefehtnn, hut his co-half was tackled. Cross took another pass hut was brought down under the goal. Another pass missed Scott hut he managed to knock the ball to Foster who almest got over at the corner. A good chance was now afforded for further scoring but crime of the home backs mulling Costello secured and raced as far as the half-way line before being stopped by Heeentn. Rigg was again tackled in his own territory and Scott made his mark in front of goal but it is disallowed. A kick of Bate's wee charged down an'i the visitors looked like getting away when Foster sent the ball into touch. Again the homeatera found their way to the corner, and Houghton in dodging round the pack had hard lines in twine knocked in touch. A mark by Rive' transferred play to mid-field. Bate. Scott, and Redford exchanged hut the latter wag downed before going far. Houghton rave the ball to Bate who was seen making for the line at ton sneed when he fell. Radford made a good dribble and Butterworth was knocked in touch after a useful run. Costello took the ball once more, and showing a el-an pair of heels rot into Warrineton territory and kicked into touch. Immediately after, he again secured and ran half the field before fleesnm stopped him. A smart bout of peesfne put Warrington again on the effeneive and Radford looked like scoring when he was tackled. Another chance seemed open for him, but the pass wag ruled forward. Heesom saved daringly, and Butterworth replied with a kick which Cross secured. The et hietle then sounded, and Warrington retired vic- tors by one dropped goal and a try (seven points) to nil. ST. 'HELENS RRCRRATION V. RUNCORN. This match was played at St. Helens on Saturday. The teams did not meet last season owing to Runcorn being in and the Recreation out of the Northern Union, but in the previous three seasons! the Cheshire team had been successful five times out of six. There were 5,000 specta- tors present when play started. The teams were fully representative, Paley playing for Houghton. In the first few minutes play varied considerably. W. Faulkner with the goal at his mercy. was pulled up from behind. After good passing from Richardson and Butterworth from a scrimmage, Recreation went down the field. and Warder only just. saved five yards from the line. Sher- rock failed to take the hall from Anpleton and Hughes, and Reneorn again had the advantage. The Recs. were penalizel for informality at a scrimmage, and W. Faulk- ner placed a goal. Shortly after Lengley scored, hut the try remained unconverted. With the score of six points against them the RPM roused up, and some spirited Play in which Sharrock almost dashed over was cheered Both teams were playing strenglv. hut the Runcorn backs got more chances from the Pcri,nmago. Robinson had to leave the field with an injury to HS face, but was able to return in a few mintatea. The recreation were now 1 having the advantage, the half hacks repeatedly getting poeseeeinn. Rencorn offered a stubborn defence, and the RPCA could not break thrneeh. Jolley played a fine fell hack game, and after half an hour's play Flnghes made a dashing run, passing to Detail, who was collared. Molynenx picked up from the ensuing scrim- mage, and die-mem-1 a goal is a reeelt. Runcorn imme- diately rushed to the Roca.' lines Jest before half-time Warder scored at the corner, hut W. Faulkner failed. Half-time :—Thancern, one penalty goal, two tries; Recreation, one dropped goal. After the interval Rim- corn had the advantage of a stiff breeze, and genie began to press, Through the strong attack, frequently started by J. Faulkner, and taken up by Butterwerth, Robinson, Warder and Richardson—the smart quick passing being a treat to watch—it was not long before Richardson shot over near the corner. ecorine the third try, the goal kick by Farmer going rather wide. Resuming, the home side, assisted hv Mnlynenx with tricky play, went off with a strong effort, and Hughes slipping when he seemed clear of Jolley lost them the only apparent chance they had of crossing the Runcorn line. Still the game went on at a rattling pace, there not being a dull minute, both sides laving with ereat determination. In the last fifteen minutes the Cheshire men were on the Recreation line, and it was only by a smart defence that Runcorn had not a hie spore. Final :—Rnneorn. one goal, three tries; St. Helens Recreation, one dropped goal. WARRINGTON A y. WERNETIT.—The Warrington A journeyed to Werneth on Saturday, and recorded an excellent win by a goal to a try. The opening stages of the match went all in favour of the visitors, and a try was early notched which was improved. Werneth re- taliated before the interval, hut the point was not improved. During the second half the fine play of Boa • cow defeated all the attempts of Werneth to draw level, and to this player must credit be given in being the mainstay in helping to record so smart a victory,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>nu-7'f \ ":7) kJ'</head>
        <p>nu-7'f \ ":7) kJ'</p>
        <p>_t_</p>
        <p>;1</p>
        <p>WARRINUMN ST. MARY'S V. ST. HELENS "A."—This match was played at St. Helens on Saturday. St. Helens winning the toss. Caldwell started, and for a short time the play was at the centre, but the " A " team rushed the ball down the field, Roughley saving from a scrimmage. The St. Helens halves got nicely away, but the final pass was faulty,and Bennett relieved with a kick to centre. The Saints had hard lines soon after, and a race with Rutter and Barton ended in Barton saving. The Saints afterwards began to press, and a drop at goal by Siddall only resulted in a minor. Soon after Warburton scored after good dribbliog by the forwards, but Siddall failed. From the kick off the visitors pressed, Higginbottom scoring, Roughley im- proving the try. Half-time score : St. Mary's, one goal one try. St. Helens, nil. St. Helens started and the Saints had to defend. The visitors were penalized for kicking the ball out of scrimmage, but Allen's kick was charged down. The Saints, who were playing only 14 men, W. Roughley being hurt, had to defend for a time, -it. Helens forcing a minor, but afterwards Sefton scored for the " A" teem, whilst soon after the same player scored two more in quick time, none being im- proved, and the junior champions were eventually beaten by one point, the final result being—St. Helens " A" three tries (nine points) ; St. Mary's, one goal one try (eight points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S V. ORFORD BARRACKS.—PIayed at Dalton Bank, on Saturday. In consequence of the English cup tie being postponed St. Elphin's arranged this "friendly" to fill up. As they had not yet suffered defeat this season it was not expected that the soldiers would be the first to do it, but they gave a very good exhibition. In the first half matters were very even, St. Elphin's having, if anything, the best of play, but were only able to score one by Dugdale before the interval. On resuming, St Elphin's again assumed supremacy, Hug- dale and Hickinbottom both scoring goals, the Barracks retaliating by scoring a very nice one After this the SAints had the upper band, but the Barracks' backs were very good, and only scored one more goal—another by Dugdale. Towards the finish the Barracks made mat- ters very warm for Robson and company, but did not quite manage another. Score—St. Elphin's, 4 ; Barracks, 1.</p>
        <p>THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL V. LYMM GRAMMAR SCHOOL.— On Saturday the Lymm Grammar School football team played their old rivals the Warrington Grammar School. The homesters had a strong team on and defeated the visitors by 17 goals to nil. STOCKTON HEATH V. THELWALL RESERVES.—PIayed at Thelwall, on Saturday, when the visitors ran out victors by ten goals to nil. The scorers for Stockton Heath were: Risley three. Hobin and Norbury two each, Lytlagoe and Nicholls one each.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S BRIGADE V. " GUARDIAN " OFFICE. This match was played at Marsh-house-lane, the ground of the former team on Saturday last. Lee kicked off for the visitors. Owing to the Saints good defence and smsrt combination. the" Guardian" team had no chance whatever of scoring, and when the whistle was blown for time they had to leave the field defeated by welve goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Monday even- ing. Mr. Bate presided, and there were also present : Messrs. T. [Talton, G. Thompson, W. Taylor, Thomas Kilshaw, mad J. Wright (clerk.) Several ratepayers were also present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>POOTPATH TO SANKEY STATION.</head>
        <p>THE POOTPATH TO SANKEY STATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Meldrum,manager for the Cheshire Lines Com- mittee, wrote on the 7th inst. with reference to the foot- path leading to Sankey Station, stating that he sub- mitted the matter to a meeting of his committee, and it had been decided to provide the footpath asked for, and their engineer had received instructions to go on with the work.—Mr. Halton thought the thanks of the Coun- cil were due to Mr.Meldrum for his kind and immediate attention to the matter.—Mr. Thompson said the Coun- cil passed a resolution thanking Mr.Meldrum,and he did not think it was necessary to pass another.—Mr.Halton said the Clerk should acknowledge the receipt of the letter.—Mr.Thompson seconded, and it was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SWING BRIDGE AT SANKEY BRIDGES</head>
        <p>THE SWING BRIDGE AT SANKEY BRIDGES</p>
        <p>The foll )wing -letter was read from the London and North-Western Railway Company : Engineers' Office, Euston,October 6th, 1896.—Dear Sir,—My assistant,Mr. Andrews, has forwarded me your communication sent to him on behalf of the Great Sankey Parish Council on the subject of the swing bridge carrying the Liverpool and Warrington road ever the canal at gankey Bridges. All that the Railway Company contemplate doing is to renew the existing bridge according to its present dimen- sions, the width being 13 feet. If the exioencies of the public road traffic necessitate a bridge 24 feet wide, then it seems to me to be hardly a matter for the railway company to deal with, moreover the expense of such a structure would be very considerable, and one that I could not see my way to recommend the railway com- pany to incur.—Yours etc.' Francis Stevenson."—Mr. Thompson said he would like the railway company to know the opinion of the Council that there should be a footway across the canal there, and that they hoped the company would take the matter up so that children and pedestrians might be able to cross the canal with safety. —The Chairman said the present bridge was very dangerous. When horses were crossing it,children were in a very dangerous position owing to the lack of that footpath.—Mr. Halton said that in the</p>
        <p>communication read it stated that the company could not see their way to construct a bridge 24 feet wide. He would like to know who had mentioned to the company a bridge of that width ? The Clerk said he had not done so.—Mr. Halton said the communication read as if someone had written to the company suggesting that width.—Mr. Thompson : The company take it that you want a double bridge.—Mr. Halton said he would like to ask whether any member of the Council had written personally to the London and North Western Railway Company with reference to the bridge? He was sorry Mr. Gorse was absent although he did not wish to suggest that he had written to the company. But he felt assured that someone had done so and if someone had not interfered in the matter he was sure the bridge would have been completed at the present time. He (Mr. Halton) had inquired from cer- tain indiyidvals connected with the railway company and they had told him that they would assist the Council in their application. —Mr. Thompson said he kiade several enquiries at the bridge with reference to the new structure which was to replace it and he had been told that the new bridge was made.—Mr. Halton : Then the new bridge was made before I broached the matter? Mr. Thompson : Yes and I told you so.—Mr. Halton said he was sorry the Council had been supersedei in the matter by some per- son writing to the company, for he felt sure the Council would have been successful in their application. He quite agreed that there should be a footpath on the brithze, for he believed that if there was not one before long there would be sometCoroners' inquests in conse- quence. In one day, 320 vehicles had been counted crossing the bridge, and there must also be nearly one thousand p issengers daily. This, he contended, was too much for a bridge, thirt.nen feet wide, without a foot- path.—Mr. Thompson said he thought the railway com- pany would have consented to. make a footway across, or at least have seen a deputation from the Council.—lt was ultimately decided that the Clerk should write to the railway company asking that an official should meet a deputation from the Council with reference to tbe foot- way. SANKEY BROOK.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said he had written to the District Council asking them zo see if any steps could be taken to miti- gate the nuisance caused by Sankey Brook. Re de- livered the letter personally on the Clerk who told him that the District Council could do nothing in the matter. —Mr. Halton said when he raised the matter previously before the Council he was told that they were powerless in the matter. The letter had not yet been read to the District Council and he and Mr. Thompson would have anopportunity of getting to know who was responsible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MEETINGS OF THE COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE MEETINGS OF THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice give!), Mr. Halton proposed that the meetings of the Council should he held monthly instead of quarterly. He thought there were many mat- ters which needed consi.ieration —Mr. Taylor seconded, and the motion was unanimously passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LIGHTING QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE LIGHTING QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gorse in a letter to the Clerk gave notice that it was his intention at the next meeting to propose the lighting of Liverpool-road between Sankey Bridges and Sankey Chapel.—Mr. Thompson said the cost of lighting that portion would be about in the 2. The Mersey Lead Company intended to put another lamp on the road and perhaps Mr. Houghton would put another one at the end of his works. Ho would propose that the clerk write to the Corporation asking them the cost per lamp. He thought about half a dozen would be needed. —Mr. Halton said he was in favour of the road being lighted but he did not think six would be sufficient.— Mr. Thompson said he thought the cost would be some- thing like £2 18s. 61. per lamp per annum. But it would be better to get the amount definite.—lt was de- cided to write to the Gas Department.</p>
        <p>This concluded the busineig. SANKEY SCROOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of this Board was held on Tuesday evening in the Board Schools,Great Sankey. Mr. Halton presided, and there were ale's present :—Mr. Bate, Mr. Mr. Wright, Mr. Kilehaw. Mr. Jeans (clerk), and Mr. Clarke (schoolmaster).—The Clerk said the meeting had been called to present the accounts and books to be passed. They were obliged to have that meeting within fourteen days after the school end year.—On the pro- position of the Chairman the accaunta as presented were passed.—The Clerk asked for permission to sign a pre- cept on the overseers for the sum of £lOO which he stated would be sufficient to take them to the end of the present year. This would be the second call this year, making a total of £2OO. It was pointed out that previous precepts had amounted to £240 in a year. The rateable value of the township was £7,948 103.—The precept was Qianerl.—The Clerk said he had received a letter from Miss Jordan, certificated teacher,stating that she wished to resign.—lt was decided to advertise for a successes.</p>
        <p>NEW B JOKS, MAGAZINES, ETC.</p>
        <p>MACMILLAN AND CO.—'' Camps, Quarters and Casual Places," by Archibald Forbes. The author- ship of this book is enough almost to ensure its popularity, for few writers are better known than the veteran war correspondent., Archibald Forbes. Some of the articles in the volume have already appeared in two or three of the leading magazinss, but not all of them, ana as there are nineteen it may easily be imagined that there is a fair quantity of ;dreading under this alliterative title. It is not a budget of exciting stories but one plainly reminiscent of Mr. Forbes' many experiences. Diversity of theme is a feature of the articles, which are all well written and with a touch of humour where appropriate. Some of them can scarcely be described as of enthralling interest, but if they lack incident calulated to arouse enthusiasm, they instil into the mind a knowledge of people and customs worth apprehend- ing. Two or three of the articles dealing with battles in recent wars, are valuable as an expression of opinion on events viewed impartially by one essentially an onlooker. An ability to criticise is one of Mr. Forbes' charac- teristics, and he exercises it very fairly in this volume. The price is (7s. 6d.)—A complete edition of the works of Wordsworth in 16 volumes is at present. being issued by Messrs. Macmillan. No. 7 is the most recent part, and contains an etching of the poet as frontispiece. . The additional nine volumes will be issued monthly at a price of ss. each. The edition is eJited by William Knight and parts 1 to 8 contain the poems of Wordsworth ; 9 to 10 the prose works of Wordsworth ; 11 and 12 journals of Dorothy Wordsworth ; 13, 14, 15, letters of the Wordsworth Family; and part 16 life of William Wordsworth.—Another trio of the Tenny- sonian People's Edition just issued are—" The Death of (Boone," "The Spinster's Sweet-Arts," and "Demeter." Of courss, there are numerous other poems besides the one giving its title to each neat little volume.—The series of Illustrated Standard Novels is by no means at an end yet ; and points to being a valuable udition to the library when complew. Month after month the 400-page volumes are placed before the public in their attractive covers and they should command a ready sale. Good illustrations adorn the pages of all in- cluding "The Kings Own,' by Captain Marryat, which is the last out. This story was one of the author's earliest,and David Hannay,in his introduc- tion, finds fault with the secondary character of the hero whom he describes as "a mere subordinate personage who drops in and out in a fashion fairly describable as casual." The unhappy ending of the story, Mr. Hanny points out at an exasperating proof of the author's inexperience as this stage of his career. Such a fault, however, seems to find favour with many present-day novelists aspiring at a dramatic finish to their fiction. A lack of interest cannot however be laid to the account of " The King's Own," notwithstanding its discrepancies.— In this month's " Century" magazine two of the most important items which have been running through its pages for some time, conclude. One is W. M. Sloane's "Napoleon." and the other is Mrs. Humphrey Ward's re- markably clever novel " Sir George Tressady." Both have been valuable contributions to the liter- ary matter of the magazine and have interested different sections of readers. Some hitherto un- known particulars of juvenile France are given in an article "About French Children," by Th.Bentzon, which contains peculiar and old-fashioned illustra- tions from the pen of Maurice Boutet de Monvel. " Glave in the Heart of Africa" covers several pages each month,and some of the writers of stories, etc.,in the current issue are: William Dean Howells, Amelia E. Barr, Clinton Scollard, Agnes Blake Poor, and George W. Julian.—" Macmillan's Magazine" contains the usual well-written articles, etc., and " St. Nicholas"puts a captivating budget of reading before its young supporters.</p>
        <p>, . F. WABNE AND CO.—Silas K. Hocking has now earned for himself a high name as a novelist and his books are awaited with interest. One of the latest before the public is entitled "For such is Life," which qnite realizes our expectations. Cor- nishmen are still the author's ideals,but he does not in this instance allow the whole of his novel to be worked out in Cornwall. The scene opens in Australia where Abram Fowey, a much-wronged man, " bides his time." While there he adopts owing to a death-bed promise, the grand- son of his bitterest enemy. Nevertheless Fow6y does his duty by the child and brings him up with his own son. In their childhood however tempta- tion comes to Fowey. Why shouldn't he change the children and substitute his own as the descen- dant of the man who turned him out of house and home? Conscience and revenge have a long struggle and Fowey's ultimate course is in keep- ing with what is expected. One of the lads goes to Cornwall and is adopted as grandson by the man Fowey hates and a fresh "life" is given to the story. Love enters in and the reader makes the acquaintance of two admirablelgirls. Eventu- ally an 'exposure of the supposed fraud is made by a former employe of Fowey's,and everything is about to end as imagined, when a revelation it, made which makes one admire the author for the skilful weaving of his plot. The denouement is quite un- expected and is a distinct improvement to the tale. By other means wrongs are righted and the guilty brought to see the error of their ways.—Mary H. Tennyson makes a bold bid for originality in "Within Her Grasp" The novel, a shilling one, tells of a girl impersonating her dead brother, in order to gain the inheritance of a rich uncle. She goes to this extreme with an idea of the fitness of things as this uncle she believes had usurped her father's patrimony. Though she is physically rather masculine the role taken up by the girl smacks a little of the improbable. As Rex Howard she is compelled to undergo ordeals of London Society which could not be safely borne by a girl. Another fault to be found with the story is the " killing-off " of the genuine Rex Howard He gave promise of being a fine character and would have been a distinct improvement in the story could he have been found a permanent place. With the essence of improbability put on one side, the story might be fairly considered one of interest and worthy of perusal; A CONSTABLE AND CO.—A useful little volume is "The Institutions of Italy," by John P. Coldstream, the author of a kindred work," The Institutions of Austria," which has already been well received. "It is not intended to be an exhaustive treatise on its subject, but is simply an endeavour to meet the felt want of a concise handbook giving such infor- mation on the Institutions of Italy as most travel- lers desire to have in passing through it, or as students wish to possess." So says Mr. Col d- stream , and he has attained his object. With- out doubt the information contained in the 150 pages of the work is valuable any- where and to everybody. A series of similar works dealing with the institutions of other European countries would be equally acceptable. There is a dearth of anything of this kind beyond the well- known and not always appreciated guide-book.— Some exciting storiea of Boer-land are put together under the title of "Tales of South Africa," by H. A. Bryden. An element of mystery pervades many of them, there is romance in all, while the "local , colouring" adds not a little to their general attrac- tiveness. Mr. Bryden can spin a yarn which claims close attention, and he is by no means un- known as a magazine contributor of some value. In the volume there are eleven short stories and each keeps the reader fully interested to the last page. CASSELL AND Co —The publishers of the "Maga- zine of Art" have enhanced the already high value of this periodical by a permanent enlargement of 16 pages. This appreciated venture comes into effect with next month's edition. Already the magazine is at the head of all monthlies for the beauty and ex- cellence of its literary and artistic contents, and it will be doubly a valued production when its size is increased.—The " Quiver " will also be enlarged by a similar number of pages next month. A splendid programme has been arranged for 1807 the follow- ing being some of the tit bits :—" Work in which I am interested" by distinguished workers ; " A charge I have to keep," being the types and moods lof children and how to deal with them; "Sunday in the Palaces" will be described by one who has been privileged to visit most of the Royal Person- ages of Europe; "Pressing Problems of the Mission Field "will be treated by the Rev. H. E. Fox, hon. secretary, Church Missionary. Society, the Rev. Dr. Greenough, of the Baptist Mis- sionary Society; and the Rev. Wardlaw Thomp- son, of the London Missionary Society; "Ancient Prophecies now being Fulfilled" will be dealt with in a series of remarkable papers by the Rev.Dr.Preston,vicar of Runcorn,and other writers ; serial and complete Stories will be contributed by the author of " Pemberton's Piece," Evelyn Everett Green, Alan S. Aubyn, Ethel S. Turner, W. J. Daw- son, S. Baring-Gould, Mrs. Orpen, Albert Hooper, Andrew Home, Agnes Giherne, E. S. Curry, A. E. Wickham, Roma White, Katharine Tynan, &amp;c.—As a guide to the civil law of Great Britain; " Cassell's</p>
        <p>Family Lawyer " should secure a permanent plaee in the homes of the public. The work will comprise about 18 sixpenny parts, the first of which wag issued this month. An introdnction to part 1 the author, ,e A barrister at law " says:—" There are popular books on science,there is a popular boor on medicine, there are popular books of travel and biography. Social questions have been treated popularly, and yet there is no readable popular work on law. My whole object 10 putting the Family Lawyer 'before the public le to explain, in a manner so simple that everyone, elm understand it, the law of England and Scotian as it effects average people in their everyday life. Judging by the initial number we feel confident 10 stating that the work will be an inestimable bocn fie all its subscribers,and the solver of many a problefo„ which would otherwise trouble them. There wio, be dealt with the law of the family man,the law 01 the householder, teela w of the business man, the law of the borrower and lender, the inheritance/ and trusts, and the law of the citizen. GEORGE NEVINES AND Co.—This month's "strand' magazine is a truly excellent number. The article/ it contains are the best we have seen for some months. One of them is Big Game Hunters," 11 Framley Steelcroft, who has interviewed several of the best-known Nimrods and got from them setae interesting accounts of their adventures and riences. An article on " Nose Improvers," by L. O' Lewis,is a revelation as to the desire of many people to alter the design ot nature by mechanical meanfb. Thera is apparently wide scope for nasal maniptr: lation. An illustrated interview with Sir Howarn Grubb, by William Fitzgerald places before the Public a lot of information about the construction of big telescopes. Altogether the "Strand" ha 6 done well this month.—" The Hub" is proving - itself a smart cycling paper.—This week' e Tit bits " has the first instalment of a new story by, Robert Barr, entitled " At War with his Workers; great strike. the story WARD, of a ocir, AND BOWDEN.—In the" Windsor' magazine for October special attention is drawn V) a story by Dr. Conan Doyle, entitled " The Three, Correspondents," which tollows a sketch of tl* author's career., The tale is one of journalistic terprise in the Soudan, and contains some exciting incidents and plenty of the humorous element' There seems to be some deterioration in thejllu trations of one or two items in the "Windsor Those supplied with "The Great Dives Elopement, are by no means up to present day standard. Sexless AND CO.—These publishers have float another of their valuable little pocket guides "le,: everybody." This last edition is " Everybodr Medical Guide," a handbook of reliable medical 10:, formation aisd advice. It deals with every-del ailments and will be found to be of great assistanee in all cases. It is from the pen of a well-knoll West End physician and the publishers commend l' to the public with confidence. The first editioo consists of 50,000 copies.</p>
        <p>11/</p>
      </div>
