      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>BEATd OF M3JOR PICKNIERE.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning last,at 8-30 Major Pickmere,an old and honoured townsman of Warrington, died at his residence, Cedar Lawn, Thelwall, aged 72 years. For a considerable time Mr. Pickmere has been ail- ing, but daring the last six weeks he has been unable to leave the house, and it was believed that his end was near. Though the death was in some degree expected,the news none the less shocked the townspeople, and Manifestations of regret were universal. At once the flags of the Town Hall, Liberal Club, and Conservative Cldb were raised to half-mast, and the shop in Bridge-st. was closed to business. Dr. White attended Major Pickmere in his illness.</p>
        <p>Major Pickmere was the eldest son of Mr. John Richard Pickmere, solicitor, Bank-street, Warring- ton and was born on tile premises now used as an office by Mr. A. Browne, solicitor. Ile ultimately succeeded to the business of his uncle, Mr. Ra'ph Randall Pickmere, wine and spirit merchant, War- rington, which he successfully conducted for a number of years. He was married twice, his first wife being Mary, eldest daughter of the late Mr. William Hunt, surgeon, Stanley-street. The present Mrs. Pickmere was a Miss Brown, of Rainhill, and a sister-in-lawof the late Mr. John Anderton, wine and spirit merchant. The deceased gentleman has been Mayor of Warrington five times: from 1873-4, 1879-80-1-2, and 1887. In 1885 he was made a justice of the peace for the borough, and a magistrate of the West Derby Hundred for the County of Lautaster in March 1801. The Town Council has several times owned him as a member both as councillor and alderman. Since 1880 he has been chairman of the Warrington Savings Bank and was also a member of the managing committee of the Infirmary. Major Pickmere's title came from the Warrington Rifle Volunteers of which he was one of the earliest members, but retired from active connection with the corps several years ago. Major Pickmere became a trustee of the Warring- ton Blue Coat School in 1874, and from that time until quite recently took very great interest in the welfare of the institution. He always attended the annual gatherings for the distribution of prizes, and on many occasions presided:at these annual gatherings. He composed a song specially for Blue Coat boys and gide, and they have often sung it at the distribution of prizes, and it has been very much appreciated. The children have been fre- quently at his house, and he always entertained them with the greatest hospitality. Major Pick- mere was also one of the patrons of the glue Coat Brotherly Society. He frequently presided at the annual dinners and was always ready when the funds required it to give a helping hand in the way of donations. Some oil paintings of his rela- tives appear in the hall of the institution. He was one of the Commissioners of Income, Property, and Land Taxes, at one time a member of the Chamber of Commerce, while his interest in the Infirmary, was unbounded; indeed, it may be said of him that be was never so happy as when he was seeking to improve the moral, social, and edu- cational condition of his follow townsmen. He will be a much-missed man in many directions. His portrait, painted by Mr. Charles during his mayor- alty, will be found amongst other portraits in the Mayor's Parlour at the Town Hall. On Saturday, March 23rd, 1895, Major Pickmere occupied the chair at the annual meeting of the Workingmen's Infirmary Committee. In respond- ing to a vote of thanks he volunteered some inlor- mation about himself which is interesting. Re said his heart was always with the working classes ot the country, as in the first place he was brought up as a• workingman—as an apprentice. That was nearly 50 years ago. One incident he related was new to Warrington people, viz.: that before he was out of his time he was made foreman of all the turners and fitters in his place of employment, because he had had a good education, knew all the drawings, and was considered a tolerably good workman. The first year of his apprenticeship he received ss. a week and in the last year, 9s. a week. ! After being foreman at this place he was made manager of a large works at Glasgow—the Atlas ! Works. He succeeded there and a relative dying in ! 1851, he thought it time to be his own master, and ! he had since remained in Warrington, where he had I been successively in all the offices up to that of Mayor. He considered himself one of the old !citizens of the old town of Warrington. • MAGISTERIAL REFERENCES.</p>
        <p>During-Ch—e-p-ro-g-re; of business at the Police Court news of Major Pickmere's death was received in definite form,and before the magistrates left,the Chatrman,Alderman J. E. Wright, observed that the Bench had incurred a very severe loss through the death of Major Pickmere. He thought every towns- man would admit that a more useful man had never served his town, both as chief magistrate,and in every walk in life. It was the least they could do to express their deepest sympathy with his widow and family. Councillor ROBERTS joined with the Chairman in expressing very great regret at the news, and their deep sympathy with Mrs. Pickmere and family. He endorsed what Alderman Wright had said about the usefulness of Major Pickmere in Warrington. Amongst other things his connection with the Savings Bank had been a matter of the greatest usefulness to the town. In connection with the Infirmary and with every other institution in War- rington which had been calculated to benefit the people generally, Major Pickmere had been more or less associated. During the mayoralty they never had a more genial and courteous man in the position or one who tried more fully to do his duty to the town at large. The speaker regretted personally that he had lost a friend, as well as a man whom they all esteemed so highly. Dr. GontrA.m. said his connection with Major Pickmere had not been on the bench, but as a public official of the borough since 1865. He could support all that had been said by Mr. Wright and Mr. Roberts. Major Pickmere was a man fair to both friend and foe alike. Wherever he went he was fair to all around. He (Dr. Gornall) could mot trust himself to say more. Mr. G. F. F. WHITE, deputy magistrate's clerk, endorsed the remarks of the bench on behalf of his father, Mr. 11. B. White.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>RECENT GALE.</head>
        <p>THE RECENT GALE.</p>
        <p>MORE DESTRUCTION AND LOSS OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>The weather experienced on Saturday afternoon and Sunday morning was of a most extraordinary charaeter, thunderstorms, gales and snowstorms occurring within the 24 hours. At Liverpool a football team was struck by lightening. One of the players was killed and several were injured. Late on Saturday night, during a severe gale a schooner, bound for Runcorn foundered in the Mersey channel, and the crew, it is believed were drowned. Several bodies have been recovered. Heavy falls of snow are reported from various districts. In some parts of the Lake district the snow was deeper than at any time during last winter. On the north Wales coast on Saturday night there was a renewal of the severe weather. A two-rpasted vessel named Jame- son was driven ashore at Old Colwyn and seems likely to become a complete wreck. The captain and a seaman were conveyed in a state of exhaustion to the Marine Hotel. A third man, who bad sustained serious injuries, was removed to the cottage hospital at Landudno,where he died, and the dead body of a fourth man was found wedged in amongst the timber with which the vessel was laden. Another vesßel, the Norwegiaa barque, Allude, from Nova Scotia for Manchester, also timber laden,was wrecked on Sunday morning on Dutchman's Bank,Menai Straits. The crew of eleven men were rescued by the Penmon and Beaumaris lifeboat, commanded by Sir Richard Bulkeley Bart.</p>
      </div>
