      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>WhEKLI REPORT ON THE IIEkLTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINOTON.</p>
        <p>During last week, which was the 40th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington 37 19 Latchford</p>
        <p>Total 4l ' 20</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births was 21. The death rate from all causes was equal to 17 8 per 1,000, against 13 6 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 43.7 the corresponding week of last year. The death' rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 0.8 per :.,000, against 2.5 ,the average rate of the four previous weeks,anc against 20 9 the correspnuding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 5 3 2 7 3 20</p>
        <p>The deaths included--1 from whooping cough, 1 from cancer, 1 from phthisis, 1 from liver disease, 1 from heart disease, '6 from lung disease,2 from train uisease, 2 fro:n stomach disease.</p>
        <p>The folliwing cases of infectious disease have been report,ed during the week :—scarlet fever, 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital on Oct._3rd,79</p>
        <p>METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 523 Fah., and therefore 1 degree lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 67F., was recorded on the 28th inst., and the minimum temperature 42F., on the 29th inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured .67 inch. The general direc- tion was S.W. the reading of tha Anemometer denoting 1390 miles.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, _ Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
