      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>Vitas to tin OZittor,</p>
        <p>FIRE EXTINGUISHING ARRANGEIIENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you oblige me with a few lines in your valu- able paper to draw the attention of the Mayor and Coun- cil to the great want of water for fire extinguishing purposes in Warrington, as shown by the recent fires that have taken place in the borough. The first fire was at Mr. Johns-la's cabinet works in Ship Yard. On that occasion the small fire engine was brought out because there was not sufficient water to supply the large one, although there is in Bridge-street a 12 inch supply pipe from Winwick. The next case was the fire at Jubilee Buildings, which occurred on the 10th May, 1895. I remember the date so well as it was the occasion of my 84th birthday. There were six hydrants on the princi- pal 18 ineh pipe from Wiewick, and it was found to be insufficient, as the brigade had to stop occasionally for want of water. It was on this occasion, although the small engine was at liberty. the Wilderspool brigade was asked to assist and it was thought that but for the latter brigade the fire would have been much more serious. The third case is the fire at Cockhedge in February last, on which occa- sion both engines were employed ,the large one receiving its snpply from the reservoir belonging to the mill. The small one was placed at the entrance gate and was sup- plied from 4 hydrants in Orford-street, which required no yards of hose piping. This engine had to repeatedly, stop through being short of water, showing the great de- ficiency in the water supply in case of fire. The fourth and last fire was at Mr. Daintith's shop in Bridge-street, when the large engine was brought out. The superin- tendent, however, knowing the deficiency in the water supply put nozzles on the delivery pipes which only al- lowed the engine to deliver one half of what it is capable of doing. This was the state of affairs in the past, and it is likely to be much worse in the future as the Cor- poration have undertaken to supply Penketh, Pedgate, and the large institution at Winwick with water which will very seriously reduce the pressure, which is at pre- sent inadequate to the needs of the borough.—Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>W. DIGGLE.</head>
        <p>W. DIGGLE.</p>
        <p>THE ALDERMANIC ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Sir.—lt is not often that I have expressed my opinion on questions which from time to time engage the atten- tion of our Town Council; indeed it is only when mat- ters affecting the morals of the people are concerned that I have ventured to give utterance to my views thereon. Such Et, question arose at the last meeting of the Town Council in connection with the election of an alderman. , It is about the act itself that I wish to write,not the two persons principally concerned in the act. I do not intend to go into past history but simply to confine myself to the deed which was done on Tuesday evening last. It is,l think,admitted by all on both sides of politics that the claims of one of the gentlemen on the ground of length of public service, and useful and efficient work for the public) good, were such as to entitle him to the vacant seat. lam not connected with either political party nor am I a member of either political club ; indeed if certain things which I have heard are true I should consider it a disgrace to be connected with either the one or tho other. So my remarks cannot be:prompted by political partizanship, tout as a burgess of the town and as a Christian citizen I claim the right to express my opinion upon any point which comes under the consideration of the Town Council. On several points kfr. Roberts and I should have to agree to differ, but I have watched for nearly five years his public career and althoueh it has not been free from fault (and which of our lives is?) yet,the whole trend of it has been in the direction of that which is calculated to promote the public good, and in the direction of that which is true and moral and good, and on occasions he has proved himself equal to rising quite above the ()misty region of party politics; and yet when an opportunity arises of bestowing an honour which has been amply earned, a majority of the Town Council stultify themselves by voting contrary to their own previous decisions, and thus by voting perpettating an act which is both despicable and dastardly; or, to put it into plainer language still, mean and cowardly—all honour to the three on their own side who voted against them. This is no mere unimportant question, such as the paving of a street or the widening of a road. It goes deeper. It involves a grave moral question. It is an act in the great fight whiela is now going on between good and evil, truth and falsehood, God and the Devil. The men who act in this way prove themselves to be unfit to have the government of the town in their hands. When questions of public morals arise such men will be found on the side of the Devil and not on God's Nide. Citizens of Warrington! Ratepayers of Warrington I Christians of Warrington I How long are you going to allow our town to be governed by a majority who are thus ready to sacrifice truth and righteousness to the exigencies of party? Is it not high time that the augean stable of our municipal life was swept clean by the purer river of a peaceful and practical Christianity? I. for one, am proud to take my stand by the side of the defeated candidate on this occasion, and I firmly believe that the dastardly act which the majority committed will redound to their own discomfiture. I cannot but think that if they saw how this appears to unbiased minds they would be heartily ashamed of themselves.— I am, sir, faithfully yours, Warrington, October 9th, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EDWARD T. REED.</head>
        <p>EDWARD T. REED.</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED "HOLE AND CORNER" BUSINESS" AT THE TOWN COUNCIL. Sir,—At the meeting of the Town Council, held at Warrington en Tuesday evening last, the Mayor was, to the surprise of everybody, except to the few privileged persons who wnere "In the know" brought forward at the last moment and elected in opposition to Coun- cillor Henry Roberts to the vacant Aldermanship by a majority of two, and the Mayor in his remarks on re- turning thanks with some warmth, stated that the attempt toelect Mr. Roberts had been done in a" hole and corner way," of which he strongly disapproved, and his remarks pointed. as I thought, to the Town Clerk as the culprit. Now, Sir, in justice to the Town Clerk, I feel bound to sav,(because I knew) that he was perfectly innocent of any complicity in the matter, and I cannot refrain from saying how monstrously wrong it is to impute unworthy motives to any one, on mere assiimp- Con, without the clearest proof of his culpability (al- though the Mayor is not to blame for it). To do so, shocks the popular sense of equity and justice. Rather ought we to say with the poet, "Believe not the aaresing tongue. As most weak persons do, Rather believe the statement wrong, That teight, not to be true." Now,sir' whilst I absolve the Town Clerk from any in- tention to do a wrong, I feel free to say, that, in my opinion,it was unfortunate that there was not more time taken to fill up the vacancy—say, at thefollowing meet- ing of the Council, although precisely the same course waslpursued in the recent case of Alderman Chandley.Flis resignation was read over at an ordinary meeting of the Council,held on the 9th Nnvemyer, 1693,and a resolution was there and then passed accepting the resignation and appointing his successor,and yet nobody even suggested that it was done in a" hole and corner way," and I am glad to believe (however I may regret it) that the allega- tion made against the Town Clerk, in this instance, is simply the outcome of prejudice passion er politics, pro- bably all three combined. I may take this opportunity of saying what I was precluded from saying on Tuesday night last at the Council meting, viz., that I knew nothing whatever of any " hole and corner business" in connection with the vacant alder- manship, and I may hark back to the caucus meeting of the Council,to which I, in conjunction with every mem- ber of the Council.wss duly summoned, a custom by the way which has hitherto been found to be very conveni- •ent,and which has helped in no small degree to allay the irritation consequent upon the surrounding incidents of political strife. This meeting was held at the Town Hall in due course, and I attended that meeting and went with a perfectly free and open mind, pledged to vote for no one I was struck on entering the room with the fact that there were so few of my own political colleagues present and I may say that a charming unanimity seem to pervade the minds of nearly all the speakers present in favour of Covncillor Henry Roberts, by reason of his services in connection with the Ship Canal, his general usefulness as a member of the Council, and also by rearm of his being the senior member of the Council, and on my appealing to the Town Clerk for corroboration of that statement, he admitted it was so, without betraying the slightest interest or anxiety in any way. Ravine rezard therefore te my impression that some time ago the Council had come to an understanding that in future all newly created aldermen should be elected to that office according to seniority of public ser- vice, I expressed my intention of entitle: for Connciller Henry Roberts,and in the hneuage of " Othello " I mix' say, " the head and front of my offending, hath this ex- tent, no more!" As a matter of fact, I had not heard of Alderman Holmes's resignation, until I attended the caucus meeting before referred to, when the Town Clerk Placeel in my hands the aforesaid resignation to read to the meeting of which I had the honour to be appointed chairman. The Town Clerk suggested that the appeint- ment might take place on the 6th inst., to which I think none of us sew any serious objection, for'altheugh there wag ne speeific mention on the Council summons (which had been already issued) before Mr. Alderman Holmes'e resienatien reached the meeting, the Town Clerk pointed out to us that the words "and other business" following the specific business to be transacted at the corning meeting of the Council, would be a sufficient intimation to the members of the Council that the vacant seat on the " alderrnanic bench" might, would or could be filled up on that day. I hardly thought so myself. I confess, and 1 was afraid there would he some dissatis- Tantinn exoressed thereat. My impression ie that the Mayor has been grossly misled in reference to the" hole and corner business," the existence of which could never hive had an abiding place in the mind of any fair and seenernue person, unless he were blinded be prejudice or MOOT,. I have always regarded the Mayor, from what I have frequently heard him sax, as one of the Town Clerk's most oast:hug-attic friends and admirers. and I believe him to be so stlll,and if I am not greatly mistaken</p>
        <p>it will so reveal itself, ultimately, when all the froth of this absurd "hole and corner business" is blown away. —I am, sir, your obedient servant, J. HARRISON.</p>
        <p>Latchford, 9th Oct., 1896.</p>
        <p>MR. KENYON AND THE MARRIAGE LAWS. Sir,—Mr. Kenyon very kindly informs your readers in a very short poetical letter that he " cannot at present, or for some time,write any more on this subject." Well, lam sure most people will think that this is a step in the right direction. " A little knowledge is a dangerous thing," and when a man writes to the public Press on a subject he does not understand, he must not complain if he gets a few friendly knocks. Mr. Kenyon says in his letter which appears in your issue of the 3rd inst., "The marriage laws prevent people separating when they no longer regard each other's welfare," So. This is quite enough to prove that Mr. Kenyon does not understand the subject under discussion. For example: The bond of marriage restored by Christ to its original unity and indissolubility, cannot be dissolved by any human power. But, married persons may, however, separate by mutual consent ; and again,if one party ex- poses the other to grave danger of body or of soul, or commits adultery, the innocent partner may obtain a judical separation, or even refuse to cohabit without waiting for the sentence of the judge, provided always that the offence is clearly proved. So that Mr. Kenyon is in error in maintaining that " the marriage laws pre- vent people from separating." Either Mr. Kenyon is a Christian, or he is not. If he is a Christian, he is bound by the Christian law Thus : Matrimony, or the permanent bond between man and wife for the propagation of the human race, was insti- tuted as the union of one man with one womon. (a) God in instituting marriage clearly pronounces its unity. "lit is not good for man to be alone. Let us make him a help like unto himself." (Gen. ii. 18). "God created man to his own image; male and female He created them. And God blessed them, saying : Increase and multiply, and fill the earth." (Gen. i.27,-23). After- wards, it is true, when the life of man had been shortened, God, in order that His chosen people might,be multiplied, permitted simultaneous polygamy. For, although polygamy is less in accordance with the secondary end of marriage, that is, with the mutual help of husband and wife, yet it can- not be said to be contrary to the primary end, that is,the propagation of the human race ; whence it could be per- mitted by God. (b) Chri-t, however, restored marriage to its original unity; for pointing to itsdivine institution, He says: "Have ye not read that He who made man in the beginning made them male and female? And they two shall be made one flesh. Therefore now they are not two, but one flesh." (Matt. xix. 4-8). (o) Christ also restored marriage to its original indissolubility. That marriage was indissoluble in the besinning may be inferred from history, which makes no mention of di- vorce till the introduction of the Mosaic law. But the words of Christ put the matter beyond all doubt." Moses by reason of the hardness of your heart permitted you to put away your wives; but from the beginning it was not so "(Matt. xix., 8.) Christ abolished the right of divorce, and thus restored the indissolubility of the mar- riage bond. This is manifest from the teaching of Christ Himself and of the Apostles, from tradition, as well as from the definitions of the church. (1.) Christ, speaking of the new law, says without restriction : " Every one that putteth away his wife and marrietla another committeth adultery" (Luke xvi., 18). "What, therefore. God bath joined together let no I man put asunder." "Whosoever shall put away his' wife and marry another committeth adultery against her. And if the wife shall put away her husband and be married to another she committeth adultery," (Mark x., 2-12). Christ makes no exception even when the disciples ask for further explanation. Nor does the Apostle make any restriction. "To them that are married, not I, but the Lord cornmandeth, that the wife depart not from her husband. And if she depart, that she remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband. And let not the husband put away his wife " (1, Cor. vii-10-11). The exception which Our Lord makes in the case of adultery (Matt. vi-32) evid- ently does not refer to a severance of the bond of matri- mony, but only to a separation from bed and board ; for in the same place He declares that he who marries a divorced woman—though she may have been divorced fer adultery—commits adultery, which could not be if the bond of marriage were entirely loosed in the case of adultery. In the face of all this, how then can a man calling himself a Christian believe in divorce ? Divorce, in its widest sense, signifies a separation made between man and wife on sufficient grounds and by lawful authority. It may dissolve the marriage bond altogether, so that the man or woman is free to contract a fresh marriage (separatio quoad-vinculum) ; or it may simply relieve one of the parties from the obligation of living with the other (separatio quoad tarum et mensum). Therefore it ,s contrary to true Christian faith to hold with divorce a vinculo.—Yours, Scc.,</p>
        <p>SURSUM CORD A.</p>
        <p>P.S.—The asoertion made by Mr. Kenyon, that the marriage laws "cause drunkenness, sensuality and sel- fishness," is a merely gratuitous statement; in support of which he has not furnished us with even the attempt at proof. The fact is that Christian morality is highest where divorces are fewest in number. Moreover,Chris- tian wives and mothers owe a deep debt of gratitude to the Catholic Church for the honourable position they now hold in society. If they are no longer regarded as the slave, but the equal of their husbands; if they are no longer the toy of his caprice, and liable to be dis- carded at any moment, like the women of Turkey and the Morman wives of Utah; but if they are recognizei as the mistress and queen of their household, they owe their emancipation to the Catholis Church which has always upheld the sacredness of the marriage bond.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>'ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FooTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team seems to have turned the corner leading to success and have now scored their second victory, though only one counts in the League. Two other clubs beside the local fifteen only possess two points in the League table, viz., Morecambe and Salford, but as they had played one match more than Warring- ton the latter has risen to third position from the bottom. This makes things look a bit more presentable, and should victory accrue to the efforts of the homesters to-day they will pass Tyldesley,for thee' bongers " have no fixture.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's match was a friendly with Rochdale St. Clements. Consequently Warrington did not put their best team in the field, the chief absentees being Barber. Burton, and Lawless from the threequarter line. In their place appeared Foster and Radford wings, and Scott centre. Bate was also absent up to the last minute,when he set the spectators' minds at rest by his appearance. ,</p>
        <p>Immediately the game was started, the homesters got a footing in Rochdale territory and Bate initiated some good passing. The first chance which made the score seem possible was when Cross secured the oval, and drawing the Saints close to the touch-line,punted across the field to Radford who had a splendid opening. Un- fortunately, however, he was a little too eager and over ran the ball. By the time he got possession the visiting custodian was in the way and nothing could be done. Not disheartened by this, the homesters played a dash- ing game and kept the Saints on their line. At last a tangible point was obtained, by some neat passing through the hands of Houghton, Bate, and Taylor. Four more p.ints were obtained in this half from a dropped goal by Scott. In the second halt also Warrington had the best of matters and it was only by spurts that thP Saints managed to get on the offensive. There was TO doubt whatever as to the superiority of the homesters, who merited their victory of seven points to nil.</p>
        <p>Speaking of the players individually it is necessary to remark once more what a valuable helper to the hack division," Smack " Bate is. On Saturday he fairly ex celled himself. Time after time he started the passing and on two or three occasions made runs which were useful to his side. On the last occasion he would prob- ably have scored but for slipping on the grass and fall ing. Houghton also played a very steady game anti displayed not a single fault. The two of them completely worsted Elalkyard and Garnett, the Rochdale halves. A fault in the latter seemed to be a tendency to kick in th( centre of the field. Among the threequartere Cross waQ the best, and his quick tackling and useful kicking wen far towards keeping the homesters on the offensive St'ott and Foster both played a very smart game and or Saturday's form would not have disgraced any team it the league. Radford did his best and was useful 0,, several occasions by his speed, but passing and takini the ball are things he has not yet got accustomed to Among the forwards Taylor was again the shining light, not only opening the scoring,but being prominent by sur, tackling, and good work in the pack. Nevins, Swift, Barrett, and Robey, also worked hard. The eight coin bined were much ahead of the visitors' forwards and the gave their half-backs every chance. Heesom was a- certain as ever and his kicking in touch was adrnirabl.</p>
        <p>In the St. Clement's team there are some smart thre-- quarters. All four did justice to themselves when Flalk- yard and Garnett passed, but Costello was the mrws prominent. Several times he made lengthy and brillia, runs and is clearly a promising wing if properly feo Rigg worked hard at full back and performed welt Naturally he had plenty of defensive work cut out fo him.</p>
        <p>For the County fixture with Cheshire at Stockpor, , today, the Lancashire team going without a represent. tive from Warrington. Barber did not play in the tri:- match and the place which he would probably have fil!e has been given to Holmes, of Morecambe. The latter a good man but not-equal to the 'Warrington captah Taylor took part in the trial match, and the " Athlet News " in a criticism on the selection says of him th, he" was worth his place in the forwards before Clem, son (Broughton Rangers)." This is quite true and it hoped that the sturdy Warringtou forward will t accordei a pace in the team which will oppose York shire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ME EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 17, 199 G</head>
        <p>ME EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 17, 199 G</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL POULTRY' FANCIER.</p>
        <p>At the Scotch Fancier's Show held at Alloa on the 10th inst., Mr. A. Widd, of Earlestown, was a successful competitor in the brown legliorn class, gaining first prize for a cock, and second prize for a pullet. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE OF GOOD' TEMPLARS.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the above lodge was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening. Bro. W. Wilson. C.T., presided over a good attendance of members. A report of the No. 1 Convention held at Leigh on Saturday was given by Bro. T. New- man, S.J.T., and adopted. Arrangements were dis- cussed for celebrating the anniversary of the lodge on Saturday next, and a Committee was appointed to complete them The evening's programme was a "Miscellaneous entertainment," when songs, &amp;c.. were given by Bro. Willis, Sisters Hill and Whitty, A short address was given by Bro. James Woods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRITNKENNESS. At the Earlestown police station on. Monday morning before Mr. J. %V. Emmett,Peter McDonnell, ot Grafton-street,Earlestown, was charged with be-</p>
        <p>log drunk in Eariestown on Saturday night and was fined 2s. 6d.. and costs. COMING OF AGE CHLEBRA.TION. On Monday evening the moulders employed at Messrs. T. and T. Vicars' Works, together with a fe vr friends assembled at the Prince of Wales Hotel, Earlestown, on the occasion of Matthew Murphy</p>
        <p>having completed his term of apprenticeship is -a moulder. About 30 sat down to an excellent dinner which was served in good style by Mr. R. Lythgoe, and was thoroughly enjoyed. The chief toast of the evening was" The health of Mr. Murphy "which was enthusiastically honoured. Songs were given by Messrs. Henderson, Heaton, Taylor, Malvern, and Tyson. THE SAD DEATH OF A REFORMATORY BOY-</p>
        <p>AT NEWTON. Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at the Legh Arms Hotel, Newton, on Monday,on the body of William John Bailey, an assistant machineman at Messrs. MeCorquodale and Co.'s Printing Works, who came by his death under circumstances re- ported in last week's "Examiner." There were pre- sent Mr. A. C. McCorquodale and Mr. F. J. NV ood representing the firm, Mr. J. Digweed, superinten- dent of the Liverpool Farm School, ani Mr. R. Tin- ker, Inspector of Factories. Evidence bearing out the facts given in our previous report was sub- mitted, and the jury returned a verdict of 'Acci- dental death:" ST. JAMES' CHURCH. HATDOCS.</p>
        <p>Special services in connection with the harvest thanksgiving were held in the above church on Sunday. The sacred edifice was very prettily deco- rated with a profusion of fruit, lowers, vegetables and corn, several of the lady members of the con- gregation having assisted in the work of decora— tion. The morning preacher was the Rev. .T. M. Courtenay, vicar of Holy Trinity, St. Helens, and the evening, Rev. W. A. Wickham, vicar of St. An- drews, Wigan. In the afternoon there was a scholar's service conducted by the Vicar, Rev. F: Ireland. The collections were in aid ot the schools. WARGRAIrE CHURCH, EABLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connection with the above church, were continued on Sunday. The morning preacher was the Rev. S. Sandy, rector of All Saints, Manchester, and in the evening an address was given by the Rev. Edgar Lambert, senior chaplain of the Mersey Mission to Seamen,in which he gave an outline of the work of the mis- sion. At the morning service the choir sang the anthem ".Man goeth forth to his work and his labour," and in the evening "Praise the Lord 0 Jerusalem." In the afternoon a musical service was given by the chair and organist, when an excellent programme was submitted to a large audience. The items consisted of choruses, solos and organ solos, the soloists being Messrs.. Kay, Nichols, Wright, Misses Corlett, Gorse and Edwards. The collections during the day amounted to £9 168. 3€1., in aid of the church renovation fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FIRE AT W ARGRAVE.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT W ARGRAVE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning a fire was discovered at the dwelling-house 474, Wargrave-road, by the wife of the occupier, William Dennett. About 6.30 a.m. Mrs. Dennett was awakened by a smell of smoke, and getting up found the bedroom was full of smoke. She went downstairs to the kitchen, and found the furniture ablaze. The sofa, table curtains, and a quantity of clothing were on fire. She ran out of the house and called to the next door neigh- bour, a man named Joseph Litherland, who with his son got the fire out in about an hour, by throwing buckets of water on the flames. The fire is sup- posed to have originated through Mr. Dennett mak- ing up a large fire in the kitchen grate before going to work at 5.30 a.m., and a burning piece of coal had fallen out on to the sofa, which was near the fire- place. The damage done to the furniture and cloth- ing amounted to about £5. VOLUNTEER FUNERAL AT EA.RLICSTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Saturdiay the body of Private D. Malvern, of Grafton-street, Earlestown, of the Ist V.B.S.L.R. Newton Company, who died last week of typhoid fever, was accorded a semi-military funeral. The cortege accompanied by 34 files of Volunteers,under the command of Colour-Sergeant Dolan left the house of deceased's father, the coffin being covered with the Union Jack, upon which was deceased's helmet and belt, and had not proceeded far, before it was overtaken by a violent thunderstorm, ant all who accompanied it were drenched with rain. The coffin was borne by the comrades of the deceased and the interment. took place in the burial ground of St. Mary's R.C. Church, the service being con- ducted by the Rev. Father Lennon. In addition to the volunteers, a number of men from Messrs. Borron's glass bottle works were present, including Mr. C. B. F. Borron, J.P. NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The bowling season in conneceion with the above club was brought to a successful termination on Saturday by a handicap,in which all competitors secured a prize. The games were commenced the Saturday previous, but as- there were 65 entries it was impossible to get through them in one after- noon. All the rounds were,. therefore, completed last Sautrday, and after play an adjournment was made to the Pied Bull Hotel,where a knife and fork tea was pattaken of. After the removal of the cloth, Mr. W. Hindley presided, and the meeting devolved into a convivial gathering,where speeches complimentary and eulogistic were made. Songs were given by Messrs. C. Bishop (Liverpool), W. Devereux, J. Liptrott, W. Dudgeon, J. Swann, and F. Brierley. During the evening the prizes were dis- tributed by Messrs. Hindley, G. A. Wood, and W. Longton, the principal winners being H. Hindley, S. Atkins, J. Rim met, G. Wood. D. Rylance, Stur- gess, J Aitken, J. Cameron, T. Mayor, W. Hall, P. Percival, T. Williamson. &amp;c. VIADUCT INSTITUTE BOWLING. CLUE, HAELBSTOWN. The bowling season in connection with the above club was brought to a close on Saturday. Owing to the very inclement state of the weather bowling was out of the question. A large number however assembled at the Viaduct Hotel where they partook of a substantial knife and fork tea provided by Mr. A.,Tunstall. After the meal.the cloth was removed, and Mr. Joseph Emmett, president of the club, was voted to the chair. The usual toasts were given and honoured, that of "Success to the Viaduct In- stitute Bowling Club," being proposed by Mr. S. iVarburton, and responded to by Mr. J. R. Allan (secretars ),who said that the club had brought to a close a fairly successful season„financially and other- wise. They had made a creditable show in the Legh challenge cup competition this season. Last season they occupied a very humiliating position at the bottom, but this year they were a good fourth,being mly one point behind Flaydock and North Ashton and three behind Golborna. He urged the players to-stick together, and success would be their re- ward. Songs were given during the evening by ‘lessrs. J. Gibbins, J. Finch, W. Grimshaw, J. Bar- low, J. R. Allan, G. Butler, W. Williams, W. Tar- mck, J. Gee, W.. Pilling, J. Stacey, G. Appleton, J. Finch, and a recitation by S. Stanley. Mr. Allan proposed the heslth of the prize donors, which was ieartily received. A vote of thanks to the chair- man terminated the proceedings. VIADUCT INSTITUTE, EARLESTOWN. On Monday evening, a concert, free to the mem- lers of the above institute and their wives was Jiven in the large reading room before a crowded audience. The artistes engaged included Miss Fanny De Bouffleurs,(soprano) Miss Minnie Casey (contral- to) Mr. Wm. Sweetman (bass) Mr. Frederick Tuson (tenor) and Mr. Lestie Harris (humour ist) who also iccompanied on the piano. The opening item of a lengthy programme, was the madrigal, "Brightly lawns," in which Miss Bouffieurs, Miss Casey, Mr. 'neon, and Mr. Sweetman took part and rendered o a very finished manner. Miss Minnie Casey fol- )wed with the old ballad "Caller Herrin," which i.e sang very sweetly her efforts being fully appre- iated. She was equally successful in the song "1"lie Gift." Mr. Frederick Tuson who possesses a ery sweet tenor voice, but not powerful, gave a —markanly sweet rendition of the song "Come in- o the garden, Maud," for which he received the •earty plaudits of the audience, his rendering of • The death of Nelson," being also much appreci- . Aed. The duet "The Singing Lesson," a semi- , umourous piece, in which Miss Bzuffteurs and Mr. {arils took part was capitally given and enthusi- -tically applauded. Mr. Sweetman gave a couple f ballads," The Golden Vanity," and " Twanky- illo," his singing being really superb. His song, l'm a roamer," was heartily received, the applause eing fully deserved. Miss Panny Bouffieurs was 'lite at nome in" The Venetian Song" and her ff )rts iu this and "Genevieve" were properly 'elgnised. The trio "The Mariners" by Miss iuffieurs and Messrs. Tuson and Sweetman, was cell given and the duet "The Battle Eve ', Messrs. Tuson and Sweetman was heartily plauded. Mr. Leslie Harris's selections, •-hich were of course encored, included " The Ari-trnas Pantomime,"" The old organ man," and The choral Society."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WARRINGTON BoA.RD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BoA.RD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>k meeting of this Board was held in the Board room at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning. Mr. Davies occupied the chair, and there were also present: Revs. J. H. Riddette, Father Moore, Mrs. Clay, Messrs. G. Pilling, G. Thompson, T. B. Malkin, J. Houghton, W. Houghton, J.P., A. J. Jolley, J. Warburton, T. Halton, J. Billing°. J. Clarke, A. Jolley, J. Dignum and J. C. Sutton (clerk). C. H. Ward (workhouse master), and A. Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools). COUNTY GRANTS TOWARDS UNION TEACHERS' REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>The CLigns stated that by the order of the Local Gov- ernment Surd under .th 6 Act of 1880, the amount due to the Union in substitution of local grants from the county towards remuneration of union teachers was £241 12s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CAtI.S.</head>
        <p>THE CAtI.S.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that all 17:nion calls had now been paid in to the Treasurer. THE NEW REGISTRATION ARRANGEBIENTS AND VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Iloard wrote asking if the vac- cination officers would be affected by the recent altera- tions in the registration arrangements, and whether the Guardians consented to the alterations.—The Clerk stated that he replied that the Guardians did not sanc- tion the proposed alterations in the registration districts in respect of the union of Latchford with Warrington. Tbe Guardians thought Latchford should remain a separate district subject to an extension of the boundary to the Ship Canal. In all other respects they approved of the alterations. He (the Clerk) had conferred with the vaccination officers who consented to alterations in their districts as proposed or as the Board might deter- mine. The matter now rested with the Local Govern- ment Board.</p>
        <p>THE NEW DIETARY TABLES. The Local Government Board wrote approving of the proposed alterations in the dietary tables for officers at the Board's institutions. THE PROPOSED NEW INFIRMARY. The CLERB said the solicitors to the executors of the late Mr. Benjamin Pierpoint nad been to him about com- pleting the purchase of land far the infirmary. He had informed them that the =Min' was standing over until they found what they had to borrow for the building. He had written to Mr. Owen, and had received a reply to the effmet that he was taking up the question, but it was a long one. He had not been able to get at it sooner owing to a pressure of work. It would be Some time before they would be able to obtain the final sanction of the Local Government Board for the purchase of the land. Although he had not done much on paper, the matter had been in his mind a great deal in the settle- ment of different questions. The Local Government Board had written that morning inquiring whether the Guardians were in a position to submit plans.—The Chairman : lam rather surprised the matter has been delayed so long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EXTENSION OF THE BOROUGH</head>
        <p>THE EXTENSION OF THE BOROUGH</p>
        <p>The alum stated that there would be au extension of the Union on November 9th. and it would be necessary to revise the relieving officers' districts. It affected chiefly Mr. Aukland's district. Recently that officer had IGrappenhall, Thel wall and Latchford Without taken out of his district. Latchford Without would now come back; the others would not. But the boundary would extend to the Ship Canal including a part of tippleton. It would be necessary for Mr. Aukland to take up the relieving officer's duties in those districts, and he (Mr. Sutton) would ask the Board to instruct him to make the necessary arrangements.—This was agreed to.</p>
        <p>TEE PROPOSED EMPLOYMENT SCHEME FOR THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>The House Committee recommended that the Clerk ,be instructed to ask some of the forty small unions who had adopted the Brabazoa employment scheme whether 1 its introduction had been beneloial.—Mr. Jolley in rov- ing the adoption of the committee's minutes said that a</p>
        <p>/LA,- " • -</p>
        <p>month ago when the subject was before the Board it was decided to defer it for two months. He had not the slightest wish to reopen that question but those who opposed the adoption of the scheme expressed the wish for further imformation about it. It was contended that the scheme had been successful, but the feeling of the majority of tbe members seemed to be that it was impracticable in similar unions to Warrington. It had been found that it was in use in a number of similar unions, and the committee thought it desirable to get information with respect to the working of it. The in— formation from the Ladies Committee was looked upon with suspicion by some members and therefore it was right that they should seek official information.—The Clerk said it was a matter that really affected the inter-- nal departmental work of the workhouse. The clerks of the various Unions written to were not likely to under-- stand much about it. He suggested that the Board should ask the Master of the House to correspond with the masters of the other house i because they were likely to give a more interesting account of their proce- dure.—Mr. A. Jolley seconded the adoption of the minutes.—Mr. A. J. Jolley said their workhouse master had from the beginning opposed the scheme, and had stated that he did not like it. The Clerk was impartial in the matter, and that was the reason why they asked him to write.—The Clerk said he took it that whether the master or himself wrote they would do it in an offi- cial way.—Mr. Jolley said the committee wished to elicit information. Some seemed to think it was a scheme of faddists. The Unions would perhaps tell thetn whether it had been approved of or tolerated.—Mr. William Houghton did not think there would be much difficulty in the master writing to the Unioas. If the Clerk wrote the clerks would go to the masters, and they would get exactly the same opinien.—The Clerk said he did not think Clerks ef Unions would go to tihat much trouble. They would simply say that so far as they knew the system worked well. (Laughter.)lf it was the desire of the Board that the work should be done through the Clerk he would have pleasure in doing it, but it appeared to him that the Workhouse Masters would better understand the subject than the Clerks.—The commit- tee's recommendation was then passed.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF A TAILOR FOR PADGATE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Snools Committee reported that there were seven applications for the vacancies at Padgate Schools for a tailor and his wife, and after carefully considering them, the committee recommended Mr. and Mrs. Woodhour.e for the position.—The Rev. J. H. Riddette asked how many members were present at the meeting at which it was decided to continue the old arrangement ?—The Clerk read out the names of those present.—The Chair man said the Boaro would remember that they made a recommendation to try a non-resident tailor. The com- mittee went into the matter but they were unanimous in agreeing to continue the old system. He felt quite sure that the matron at the schools could not manage forty or fifty boys without assistance.—The Rev. J. H, Rid- dette wished to know whether a committee could set aside a recommendation of the Board P—The Clerk said the matter was left in the hands of the c mmittee. The B)Ard did not make a definite recommendation.— Mr. Pilling seconded the minutes, and they were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PLEASA.NT SUNDAY AFTERNOON.</head>
        <p>PLEASA.NT SUNDAY AFTERNOON.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Earlestown Mission, the first of a series of " Pleasant Sunday Afternoons" was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Sunday. There was a large attendance, and the proceedings were presided over by Mr. S. Harvey, the speaker for the afternoon being the Rev. H. Wilson,of Ashton. Solos were given by Mr. Spiers, of Haydock, and Miss Spiers gave a violin solo. Mr. S. Dring accompanied. In the evening a special musical service was held, when selections were given by the string band and solos were sung by Mr. Spiers of Haydock. Mrs. George Bunting pre- sided at the piano and Mr. S. Dring at the organ. A short arldress was given by Mr. S. Dring, and col- lections were made on behalf of the Mission fund. A PLATE GLASS INSURANCE SOCIETY FOR EARL/MOWN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a meeting of the trades- men of Earlestown and Newton was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, to consider the advisability of forming a Plate Glass Insurance Society for Earlestown and district. The chair was occupied by Councillor E. T. Evans. Mr.G F.Clarke gave a lucid explanation of the lines on which the society should be conducted, and after some con- sideration it was unanimously decided to form a society under the title of "The Earlestown, Newton and District Mutual Plate Glass Insurance Com- pany." The directors elected were Messrs. T,Moly- neux, G. Owen, W. Attfield, R. B. Toft, and E. T. Evans, The Manchester and District Bank were appointed treasurers, Mr. G. F. Clarke secretary, and. Messrs. J. Evans and S. Harvey auditors. EARLESTOWN WOMEN'S- LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Association was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, the other evening, when there was a fair attendance of at embers this being the first meeting of the session. The Presi- dent, Mrs. Rawlins, opened the meeting with a bright and encouraging address, remarking briefly that they had catise for thankfulness that the Edu- cation Bill had been withdrawn by the Govern- ment, as it would have retarded to a very large extent the progress of education, it being an ex- tremely sectarian measure and aiming at lowering] the standard of Board Schools. She urged the members to endeavour to learn more of the mean- ing of the Bill, as it was likely to be brought again before Parliament next January. She alluded to the resignation of Lord Rosebery stating that he as- signed as his reason for doing so that he found him- self in disagreement with Mr. Gladstone and other leaders of the Liberal party on the Eastern ques- tion.—Miss Marrow read her report of the annual council meetings of the • Women's Liberal Federa- tion held in London in June last, which meetings she and Miss Owen attended as representatives of that association. The report which was lengthy and of a most interesting character was eagerly listened to by all present. It showed the Federa- tion to be increasing largely in membership and influence, there being at tne Council meetings 900 delegates representing 500 affiliated associations, 63 new associations having been formed during this year of Liberal depression. The total number of members of the Federation amounted to 82,000 women, the Earlestown branch contributing 230 of that number. Tea was then handed round, and Mrs. Rawlins announced that the next meeting would be held on November 2nd, when Miss Allatt , would read a paper on "National Schools."</p>
        <p>THE VICAR OF HAYDOCK AND EIS CONGREGATION,</p>
        <p>For some weeks past, as already hinted at in these columns, considerable imitation has been in progress in Haydock amongst the parishioners of St. James' Hay- dock, upon the question ot "es church ritual, since the death of Mr. Artterr Evans, Vicar's warden, a party led by Mr. Robinson, Manager of Messrs. Evans and Co., have been insisting upon a change in the church service, which at the present time approaches the" High," but is similar to what has been in force for many years, whilst the Vicar's party claim that it shall remain un- changed. One or two meetings have been held, and counter petitions have been drawn up and presented, culminating in a meeting being held last Friday, when the Vicar intimated that he would give his decision at a later meeting. On Sunday evening he said a meeting would be held on Thursday night for the ostensible put.- posecof receiving three accounts,`viz.,"Church debt and repair fund, school treat fund, and reel&amp; of rummage sale," adding that he would have something else to say. The meeting was therefore held on Thursday night when there was a large attendance of parishioners. The Vicar (Rev.F.lreland)who was alone on the plattorm said they were met to receive a statement from the Treasurer respecting the amount of the church debt and repair fund, school treat fund, and rummage sale. The Trea- surer was not present. He saw there was a parcel on the table, but there was no treaturer. He asked for the parcel to be sent back to Mr. Edwin Jackson, the trea- surer. It appeared the parcel was addressed to Mr. Walden but that gentleman en!opening it refused to take any responsibility in cennection with it. The Vicar said they were called there to receive a statement from the Treasurer, but he was not there, and it was for the meet- ing to say what was to be done.—Mr. A. Bradshaw said he bac, audited the accounts,and bad found them correct. The Vicar said sueposing the accounts were read, and any questions were asked about any itena who was going to answer them.—Mr. Bradshaw: Most certainly the treasurer, but if any questions are asked, I will do my best to answer them.— Cho. Vicar was satisfied with this and asked Mr. Waldenlto open the parcel, adding that perhaps Mr. A. Bradshaw would say what was what and which was whieh. So far as he was concerned he knew nothing about any of the funds ;he might net have been there.—Mr. Walden asked the Vicar if he would read them, and on the statements being handed up, the Vicar said he was not going to read those lists for any- body, it would take him until to-morrow week.—Mr. A. Bradshaw then read the totals showing the debt on the Church Building Fund to be £346 3s. id. The balance from rnmmage sele was £39 ss. 3d., and from; school treat £2 17s. 4d. The Vicar said they ought to be very thankful to Mr. A. Bradshaw for what he had done that night. He was not treasurer. It was the business of the treasurer te be there...-Mr. A. Bradshaw moved the adoption of the accounts, which Mr. A. Maddison se- conded,and it was unanimously carried.—Mr. Bradshaw asked if any record of the meeting was being taken. He thought some note of the proceedings should be taken,in justice to the treasurer. He believed they had a secre- tary (Mr. Walden) present.—Me. Walden said he was certainly secretaey of the church,but his office had been abrogated for some length of time; he had been entirely ignored.—The Vicar: So have I. He then observed that he had something more to say, and what he should say that night would he final and nothing more. Those men who were at the mewing last Friday night would remember that the first point in connection with the service of the church was the eastward position. He asked was it or was it not stated at the meeting re- ferred to,that all objection to the officiating clergyman adopting the eastward position and the choir turning to the east of the creed,was removed. Was it or was it not a fact P—Mr. Robinson said he did mention that point himself and he believed the meeting assented to it.—The Vicar said another point was the turning to the east of the choir when sineing the Gloria. He had been making some enquiries upon that point from many people, and many churches, and it was not as common a practice as turning to the east in the creed,and so he would give up that point ; So in future they would remain standing as they were when singing the psalms and hymns. With regard to the separation of services on Communion Sun- days and seecial festivals, he said they were going to have service at 10.30, when the order for morning prayer would .be said, without the choir, and people would be out of church before 11 o'clock. At 11 o'clock the bell would be rung as usual for a quarter of an hour,and they would go on with the second service with a choral cele bration of the Holy Communion. Mr. Robinson asked if that wo aid include a sermon with the morning prayer. —The Vicar : No! the sermon would come in the other portion. Whenever there was a late celebration of the Holy Communion, there were generally two clergymen present, and on those occasion' the assistant would act as server. Another point he did not know of until recently when it was let out of the bag quite ilnexpect- edlv, was that before he came there, the offertory bag was always sent round to the choir, and for all these months they had been let off. But in future the collec- tors, and churchwardene and sidesmen would collect from the choir at all services, and so bring their bags right up to him at the sanctuary steps. With regard to the change of vestment,he said there would be no change when the services were separated. These being all the points in dispute, the Vicar declared the meeting closed.</p>
        <p>IRON and BRASS BIIbSIIAA,DS4—GABaaND, Iran- moiager,Warringtaa.— Advt.</p>
        <p>THE FATAL LARKING CASE AT EA RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at the New Market Inn, Earlestown, on Monday morning on the body of George Augustus Linaker, who was killed by being struck with a piece of wood thrown by a lad named Thomas James Lord under circumstances re- ported in last week's "Examiner." Mr. R. Tinker, factory inspector, and Mr. Johnson .manager for Messrs. T. and T. Vicars were present. The boy Lord with his father was also present during the inqpiTy. Mr. N. Sid- deley was foreman of the jury. . . .</p>
        <p>Jain James Linaker said he was a machine man at Messrs. Vicars's works. The deceased was his son, and resided with him at 25 Tamworth-st., Earlestown. He was 16 years of age. He left home about 20 minutes past eight on the Bth inst., in company with witness,but arrived at the works first. Witness worked in the same shop as deceased, and he saw him frequently at work. the last time being a few minutes before the occurrence. At 10.15 he saw him and he was then unconstious. The ambulance was procured and he was taken home. Dr. Valentine was sent for but his son died before he ar- rived. Witness did net see any larking, and did no think it had been going or. for any length of time; if it had been he should have seen it. The lads did lark at times,and he had no,doubt his boy was jest as bad as the others. So far as he knew there was no ill-feeling be- tween the boys. His son never spoke after he was struck.</p>
        <p>James Peat, an apprentice at Messrs. Vicars's works and living at 124, Market-street, Earlestown, said that about 10 minutes past 10 on the Bth inst. he was work- ing in the same room as deceased and Thomas Lord. He saw Lord throw a piece of wood in the direction of deceased, which struck a pair of calipers, he had in his hand,and knocked them out of his hand. Deceased threw it back at Lord, and the latter threw it again,and deceased stooping to avoid it, the missile struck him on the right side of the head. If Linaker had stood still, the wood erkould not have struck him. Lewis Moses lifted him up aud he walked to his lathe, and then fell, and George Lengworth carried him outside. When Lord threw the wood, he said to another lad " mind away Jack." He was certain Lord only threw the wood in play, and was not angry with deceased. They were on very good terms with each other.—By Mr. Tinker: The throwing only lasted fAr about five minutes. They had not been throwing for an hour or so. _</p>
        <p>The Coroner: In view of what the lad Lord has said te her Majesty's Inspector,do you mean to say that this throwing had not been going on for nearly an hour ? Witness : It had not.</p>
        <p>Lewis Birks said he was an iron turner's apprentice, aged 17,and living at 17 Gross-lane,Earlestown. On the date in question he was working in the shop and saw Thomas Lord throw a piece of wood in tbe direction of deceased, knocking a pair of calipers out of his band. Deceased threw it back,and Lord threw it again and hit him upon the right side of his head,knocking him down. Witness assisted him up, and he leaned with his head against his lathe and was afterwards removed out of the shop. Be did not know why the wood was thrown and could not say whether there was any enmity between the lads. He did not see any of the lads throwing pieces of wood about for any considerable time on that parti- cular morning. They had thrown things about on other mornings in play. The lads were on good terms and he knew of nothing to cause enmity between them. rhomas James Lord, 14 years of age,said he had been working at Messrs. Vicars' nearly six months, and had known Linaker about two years. He had always been friendly with him and there hal been no ill-feeling at any time. Peat and another lad named Williams were throwing chalk at each other. Williams went away,and Peat put a piece of chalk on a board and got a piece of wood and threw it,hitting witness oa the fingers. Wit- ness threw the piece of wood at Peat,and it hit Linaker's calipers,knocking them out of his hand. Linaker picked his: calipers and the piece of wood up, and threw the latter at witness and hit him in the stomach. Witness threw it back at Linaker and it struck him on the head.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said after this evidence it would be as well to have Peat in again. When he entered the room the Coroner asked him if it was correct that he had thrown a piece of chalk. Peat said he had not thrown.He was closely questioned by the Coroner bet still adhered to his statement that he did not throw, and did not see any chalk. Lord repeated his account of the affair but Peat still denied the correctness of the state went, and the Coroner said they had it between them. In answer to Mr. Tucker, Lord said he and another lad named Williams had been throwing before that, about nine o'clock.</p>
        <p>_ Dr. Valentinesaid that about 10.30 on ThnrsflaT morn- ing he was sent for tl attend the deceased at M Tam- worth-street. when he arrived the boy was dead. On examination of the head, he founa a small swelling over the right temple, on the top of this swelling was a scratch Of an angular form. Tne opinion he expressed at the time was that deceased was suffering from dere- bral hemorrhage. On Friday he made a post nscrtens ex- amination of the body and on removing the skull cap he found a large clot of blood pressing on the brain, which had also caused laclration. The clot was found directly opposite the wound on the scalp. His opiuion was the cause of death was due to the clot of blood pressing on the brain, and causing the laceration. The clot was due to bleeding from the middle meniginal artery. It would not require a considerable degree of force to inflict the wound. &amp;boy would not have to thrown with any great strength to cause the wound. There was no fracture of the skull,but simply the lacera- tion of the artery, which was opposite the external wound. The Coroner briefly summed up, pointing out that thel lads had been larking as lads would. do, and the only question for the jury was whether Lord had any crimi- nal intent' at the time he threw the wood, if they thcught so, then the case would be one of manslaughter, but if they did not think so they would have to give a verdict at death from misadventure. After a brief consultation the jury gave a unanimous verdict of "Death by misadventure." The Coroner, conveying the verdict to the lad Lord,said it meant that the jury did not think he had any criminal intent in the matter. He thoroughly agreed with the jury, and if it had been his own boy he did not see how he could have blamed Lord, as he felt certain he did not intend to do any harm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>GIFTS.</head>
        <p>GIFTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WRIGHT reported gifts of fruit and flowers from the Rev. B. B. Prescott, of Latchford; Rev. W. H. Drummond,Cairo-street, Warrington; ladies from Bold- street chapel ; Miss Green,of the Railway Mission; aad books sad periodicals from Miss White, Legh-et.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>DEPUTATION TO LEYFIELD SCIWOLS, WEST DERBY.</head>
        <p>THE DEPUTATION TO LEYFIELD SCIWOLS, WEST DERBY.</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING said he attended as one of a deputation to Leyfield Schools, West Derby. They were well re- ceived and it was an interesting out. He did not know whether they had learned much. It was very interest- ing to see the different arrangements. and after seeing the lavatory arrangements they might be able to im- prove their awn. (Hear,hear.) The schools were very clean and well managed.—The Rev. J. H. Riddette : Could you support the application from the Catholic authorittes P—Mr. William Houghton said as one of the deputation,if they were short of room at Padgate schools he could strongly support the transfer of their Catholic ehildren to Leyfield schools. They were a model of good arrangement. It was a surprise visit to the schoolseand if they had known beforehand the officials could net have had the schools in a better condition.—Mrs. Clay said she quite endorsed what had been said with refer- ence to the schools. It was a treat t) visit them, and the method was everything that could be desired.— The Rev. J. H. Riddette said he was gratified with the report. He should feel it his duty to suppsrt the trans- fer of the Catholic children irrespective of the number in Padgate Schools.—The Rev. Father Moore said the ex- pense of the children if sent to the Leyfield Schools would not be as much. The cost at present at Padgate was 54. 63. per head, and he was in a position to say that Dr. Whiteside would take the Catholic children for ss. each.—The Chairman expressed the opinion that a difficulty would be experienced if they sent the best children away and left the worst behind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MEMBER FOR LITTLE SANKEY.</head>
        <p>THE MEMBER FOR LITTLE SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. TIInMPSON said as that was probably the last occasion be would appear on that Board he would like to thank the constituency that gave him a seat on that I3oard, and in the second place he would like to thank the officials of the Board. especially Mr. Sutton, the clerk, for the kind way he had given him any informa- tion. He also begged to thank every gentleman present and absent belonging to that Board for the courtesy they had extended to him, though he was a comparative stranger in Warrington, He appreciated that courtesy very much and whether he appeared in that Board room again or not, he hoped the friendship that had grown between them would extend as long as he was in War- rington. (ilear, hear.)—The Chairman said if the Board lost the services of Mr. Thompson they would lose one of the best members of the Board. He should be sorry if that occurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>BEITIMATHII.</head>
        <p>THE BEITIMATHII.</p>
        <p>The CLERK presented his statement upon the estim- ates for the half-year ending Lady Day, 1897,which was taken as read. It stated that the outlay in the half-year ending at Lady Dly next is calculated as follows :—for in-maintenance, £2,100 ; out relief, £2,200;: lunatics, £1,650 ; salaries and superannuations, £1,530 • rations, £220 ; non-resident poor, £l2 ; extra medical fees, £2O; vaccination. £l5O • registration, £150; Assessment Co- mmittee, £125 - legal expenses, £2O ; miscellaneous ex- penditure £1,500; total, £9,677. Calculation of Com- mon Fund credits at £2,620. leaves a total estimate for Common Fund purposes of £7,057. For the to past half-years the common charges were estimated at sd. and 41. in the 2. Five pence in the 2: on the present rateable value of 2355.431' tin succession to £352,246 and £348,788) will produce £7,403,and that rate is fixed. The balances enable,notwithstanding increase of County Rates a moderate reduction even from the lower of the two preceding calls, except as to two townships in each of which a slight increase results. LI November part of a lost rateable value will be regaineGi by Union exten- sion. This new increment cannot be utilized in the present estimates; financial adjustments being first necessary. The burdens of the half-year will, however, to some extent be eased by the gain. The Union Loan Debt has been reduced to £8,848 10e., being less than 6d. in the 2 on the rateable value. Adverting to Statutory enactments, the provisions of "The Poor Law Officers'Superannuation Act, 1895" are operative from September 30th. The•distretionary power possessed by Guardians to award superannuation grants to Poor Law Officers whose age and service come within the prescribed Statutory conditions is derived from seven statutes. The power is not taken away by the new law. In the very long period in which the Power has existed no application by amentieed officer has been refused, yet in two cases, only until recent dates, had grants peen awarded and both had short duration. By the present Act a fund is to be formed out of the salaries of officers and servants. The rate of their contributions will be : by officers with less than 5 years' service, aper cent. ; by officers with more than 5 and less than 15 years' service, 21. per cent. ;: by officers with more than 15,years' service, 3, per cent. The allowances will be as follows :—The minimum of service is to be ten years. An allewance of ten.sixtieths of the average amount of wages and emoluments during the previous 5 years,one- sixtieth to be added for each additional year's service until the maximum of 4a sixtieths is reached. The age limit of the Act is 664. and the aggregate amount of ser- vice required to get a full pension is 40 years. If any of the present offilers prefer to be under the old law they may continue so by giving notice thereof within three months of September 30th. All officers appointed here- after will come under the new Act. The principles ot the Agricultural Rates Act, 1896, are simple, but the subsid- ary provisions of the Act and of the regulations made under it by the Local Government Board create machin- ery of a complicated character. The Assessment Corn- mittee,with the official help afforded to them,have com- plied with the provisions and regulations so far as is to to this date required of them. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Buckingham presided on Tuesday at the annual meeting of the Manchester Ladies' Branch of the Missions to Seamen. The work of the Society was cordially commended to the generosity of the public. Canon Scott, in whose oarish the Salford docks are situ- ated, stated that the condition of the people living near the docks was " steadily changing for the worse." The news from Rhodesia relates chiefly to the killing of small parties of natives who venture to resist the British forces. The " rebels" are generally driven off, " leaving a few killed "here and there. The heroic "patrols" who encounter them appear to pass through the conflict almost unscathed. The Lab)ur Department of the Board of Trade reports that there was a slight rise last month in the percentage of unemployed as compared with the state of things in August. The number of fresh disputes during the month was 51, eight being in the textile trades. About 28,000 persons had their wages advanced, and 108.000 had them decreased. It was estimated, however, that the effect was an average advance in the wages of the total numb.r affected.</p>
