        <p>WARRINGIQN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday. Mr. W. Houghton. JP., presided, and there were also present—Messrs. J. Johnson, J. Warburton, G Thompson, J. Dignum, • Halton, J. W. Knowles, J. Houghton, S. Sutton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), Dr. Stephton (medical officer),and Mr. F. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>BALANCE IN HAND,</head>
        <p>BALANCE IN HAND,</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the balance in favour of the Board was £376 4. 4d. and on the Burtonwood Sewage loan account. £1,195.</p>
        <p>PENKETII AND GREAT SANKEY WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk presented the draft of agreement be- tween the Council and the Warrington Corporation for the supply of the townships of Penketh and Great SankeY with water.—The agreement was as follows ; that the Corporation shall supply and construct the necessary mains and pipes as shown on the plan attached for supplying Great Sankey and Penketh with water; that the Corporation shail also fix hydrants for extinguishing fires ;that the Rural District Council shall pay to the Corporation such a sum as when added to the rates and charges paid by persons supplied with water within the township (not including any charges paid by any person now supplied with water by the Corporation in the township of Great Sankey) shall be equal to a sum cal- culated at 5: per cant. upon the actual cost of providing and laying the mains and pipes, when and so soon as the amount received by the Corporation for the supply of water to the said township (not including any ctiargeo paid by any person now supplied with water by the Cor- poration in the township of Great Sankey),shall be equal to or in excess of the sum or rate calculated at 8 per per cent. upon•the cost of all payments by the Rural Dis- trict Council shall cease, and in any case no payment shall be made by the Rural District Council under thiS I agreement after the first day of January, 1891.—The Clerk said that until he saw the agreement, he was not aware that Great Sankey was interested. If-, however, appeared that the Corporation must be supplying people in that township: So far as he read the agreement,they would only hold the Council responsible for Penketh.-- Mr. Dignum : Perhaps it refers to the Lead Works and Houghton's Works.—Mr. Owen : Efoughton's are only being supplied for drinking purposes.—Mr. Thompson: We pay £lOO for the water, and Houghton's pay about £2O per annum.—Mr. Knowles thought that the CEA' poration ought to make a deduction of something for what they were receiving in Great Sankey. in the total cost of the work. If they were charging the whole cost of the work from Sankey Bridges on the district, theit should allow* for the benefit they received from supply- ing the works in Great Sankev.—lt was eventually de- cided to refer the matvr to the Surveyor, the Clerk and the Town Clerk, to revise the agreement. Mr. Owen pointed out that Great Sankey was not committed to anything.</p>
        <p>A COMPLAINT FROM PIiNEETE. A letter was read from Mr. J. J. Owen, Brookside Farm, Penketh, complaining that when the main sewer cut through his field the field drains were not re•cott• nected.—Mr. W. Owen and Inspector Kerfoot said that the facts were in accordance with the letter, and per mission was given to Mr. Kerfoot to see Mr. Owen tO try to wit the mattor right. MEDICAL_ OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Sephton reported that during the month of Aug. there had be..n 19' births and six deaths. The death rate was equal to 63 per 1,000, against 11.2 the average ef the last three months, and 11.2 the corresponding month of last year. The death rate from zymotic diseases wee nil,against the average of the last three months, and nil the corresponding month last year. There were 13 deaths of the zymotic class. The cases of infections dir ease reperted were—scarlet fever two cases, typhoid fever one case, and erysipelas one case. For September there had been 22- births and e;g.ht deaths. The death rate was equal to g. 4 per /,000.against 126 the average of the last three months and 11.2 the corresponding period last year. The death rate from zymotic was equal to 144: per 1,000, against two the average ei• the last three months, and 21 the corresponding month of last year. The deaths from zymotic diseases were erysipelas one case,and whooping cough one case. The, cases of infectious diseases were—scarlet fever six, atio typhoid three. He also reported a great jar provement in the knackers yard, Longford! The Chairman : The death rate is very low, almost the lowest I remember, I see it is while the doctor has bee° on his holidays (laughter). We had better give 011 some more (renewed laughter).—Dr. Sepliton I sbeo be very glad to have some more.—The reports were adopted.</p>
        <p>SURVEYOR'S REPORT. The Surveyor's report, which contained nothing of public importance was passed unanimously.</p>
        <p>MR TITOBVPSON'S FAREWELL TO TELE COUNCIL. Mr. Thompson said that as that was his last apPe6r. ance as a member of that Board, owing to Little Saul being incorporated in the borough he wished to than,. the memloers and officials of the Council for their 'dfl.?. ness to him during the time he had been with them. Jai' had made many friendships which he hoped still to rn: tain.—The Chairman said that they all regretted that Mr. Thompson had to recire from the Board, as he had always shown great ability in his dealings with itS busi. ness.—The Clerk said that on behalf of the officials be would also like to thank Mr. Thompson for the unif°,72, kindness which he had shown towards them during membership on the Board, Coming as a comPlew, stranger to them he had shown himself a very usefo member, and they all regretted that he could not rem, with therm</p>
